var e=[{topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Medicinal chemistry asks a simple question: why does this molecule work and that one does not? The answer usually sits in two places, the physicochemical properties of the molecule (how greasy it is, how ionised it is, how big its groups are) and its structure-activity relationship, or SAR, which is the map of which structural pieces matter.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The words you must know`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Pharmacophore: the minimum three-dimensional arrangement of atoms or groups needed for activity. Remove one and activity is lost.`,`Auxophore (or auxophoric group): a group that is not essential but modifies potency, duration or absorption.`,`SAR: how activity changes when structure is changed one step at a time.`,`QSAR: quantitative SAR, where activity is fitted to numbers rather than described in words.`,`Bioisosteres: groups with similar size, shape or electron distribution that can replace one another with retention of biological activity.`,`Lead compound: the starting molecule with useful but imperfect activity that is then optimised.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Lipophilicity: the partition coefficient`},{kind:`para`,text:`A drug must dissolve in aqueous body fluids and then cross fatty membranes. Lipophilicity is measured by shaking the drug between n-octanol and water and comparing the concentrations. Octanol is used because its hydroxyl group and long chain mimic a membrane phospholipid.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`P = C(octanol) / C(water)   and   log P = log10 P`,caption:`P is the partition coefficient of the unionised drug. C(octanol) is the concentration in the octanol phase, C(water) the concentration in the aqueous phase. Log P above 0 means the drug prefers lipid; below 0 means it prefers water.`},{kind:`para`,text:`When ionisation is allowed for at a stated pH the term used is log D, the distribution coefficient. For a neutral drug log D equals log P at every pH.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ionisation and pKa`},{kind:`para`,text:`Only the unionised form crosses membranes easily by passive diffusion, but the ionised form is usually the one that binds the receptor and it is the form that gives water solubility. Most drugs are weak acids or weak bases, so the fraction ionised depends on pH.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Weak acid: pH = pKa + log ([ionised] / [unionised])`,caption:`pKa is the negative log of the acid dissociation constant. For a weak base the log term inverts to log ([unionised] / [ionised]). At pH = pKa the drug is exactly 50 percent ionised.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Like dissolves like, like stays like`,text:`Acidic drugs stay unionised in acid (stomach, so aspirin is absorbed from the stomach). Basic drugs stay unionised in base (intestine). Ionisation traps the drug in that compartment.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The Ferguson principle`},{kind:`para`,text:`Ferguson noticed that for general anaesthetics and other non-specific depressants, what matters is not the absolute concentration but the thermodynamic activity, that is, how close the drug is to its own saturation solubility. Drugs acting by a physical, non-specific mechanism need a high thermodynamic activity; drugs acting on a specific receptor work far below saturation.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two facts that repeat`,text:`Optimum log P for oral absorption and for many general purposes is around 2; for entry into the central nervous system the commonly quoted optimum is also about 2 (roughly 1.5 to 2.7). A log P far above 5 usually means poor aqueous solubility and heavy plasma protein binding.`}]},{topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`QSAR replaces adjectives with numbers. Each substituent is given a value for how greasy it is (pi), how it pushes or pulls electrons (sigma), and how bulky it is (Es or MR). Activity is then fitted to those numbers by regression so that the next analogue can be predicted rather than guessed.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The four classical QSAR parameters`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`Property measured`,`Reference / origin`,`Sign convention`],rows:[[`pi`,`Lipophilicity of a substituent`,`Hansch, from log P values`,`Positive = more lipophilic than H`],[`sigma`,`Electronic effect`,`Hammett, benzoic acid ionisation`,`Positive = electron withdrawing`],[`Es`,`Steric bulk`,`Taft, ester hydrolysis rates`,`More negative = bulkier`],[`MR`,`Molar refractivity (bulk + polarisability)`,`Lorentz-Lorenz equation`,`Larger = bigger, more polarisable`]],caption:`Es for hydrogen is defined as zero, so every larger group has a negative Es.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`pi(X) = log P(R-X) - log P(R-H)`,caption:`pi is the lipophilicity contribution of substituent X, obtained by subtracting the log P of the parent unsubstituted compound from that of the substituted one.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (Kx / Kh) = sigma . rho`,caption:`Hammett equation. Kx is the equilibrium (or rate) constant for the substituted compound, Kh for the unsubstituted, sigma is the substituent electronic constant and rho is the reaction constant, which is 1 for benzoic acid ionisation in water at 25 degrees Celsius.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hansch analysis (linear free energy, extrathermodynamic)`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (1/C) = -k1 (log P)^2 + k2 (log P) + k3 sigma + k4 Es + k5`,caption:`C is the molar concentration producing a fixed biological response, so log (1/C) is potency. k1 to k5 are regression coefficients. The negative squared term makes the plot a parabola with an optimum log P (log P0).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Hansch is a continuous model: it needs a series of analogues that vary in the same parameters and it gives an equation you can extrapolate from.`,`The parabolic form is the classic random-walk model; Kubinyi later proposed the bilinear model, which fits a sharper break at the optimum.`,`Statistical quality is judged by n (number of compounds), r or r squared (correlation), s (standard error) and F (Fisher ratio). A useful rule is at least 5 compounds per parameter used.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Free-Wilson analysis (de novo, additivity)`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (1/C) = sum of (ai . xi) + mu`,caption:`ai is the activity contribution of the substituent at position i, xi is 1 if that substituent is present and 0 if it is absent, and mu is the activity of the unsubstituted parent. No physicochemical constants are needed.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Hansch`,`Free-Wilson`],rows:[[`Descriptors used`,`Measured constants (pi, sigma, Es)`,`Indicator variables 0 or 1`],[`Model type`,`Extrathermodynamic, continuous`,`Additive, de novo`],[`Prediction outside the set`,`Possible`,`Not possible`],[`Needs constants tabulated?`,`Yes`,`No`],[`Handles unusual substituents`,`Poorly`,`Well`]],caption:`The mixed approach that uses both is called the Hansch-Fujita-Ban or mixed approach.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Choosing which analogue to make next`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Craig plot: a scatter plot of sigma on one axis against pi on the other. Substituents fall into four quadrants (+pi +sigma, +pi -sigma, -pi +sigma, -pi -sigma). Pick one from each quadrant so that pi and sigma are varied independently and are not collinear.`,`Topliss decision tree: a non-mathematical, non-computer scheme. Synthesise the 4-chloro analogue of a lead aromatic ring first, then move up, down or sideways in the tree depending on whether the new compound is more potent, equipotent or less potent.`,`Topliss also published an aliphatic branch for side-chain modification, starting from the isopropyl analogue.`,`Cluster analysis and the Craig plot both exist to avoid collinearity, which makes a regression equation statistically meaningless.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Craig plot versus Topliss tree`,text:`The Craig plot is a graphical selection tool used before synthesis to spread substituents across parameter space. The Topliss tree is an operational scheme applied after each analogue is tested, one compound at a time. Examiners swap the two.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Lipinski rule of five`,text:`Poor oral absorption is likely if two or more of these are violated: molecular weight above 500, log P above 5, more than 5 hydrogen bond donors, more than 10 hydrogen bond acceptors. Veber added rotatable bonds not more than 10 and polar surface area not more than 140 square angstroms.`}]},{topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the advanced level the examiners test the exceptions: which bioisostere replacement is classical and which is not, why the optimum log P differs for different sites of action, and what the Hammett and Taft constants actually derive from.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Bioisosterism in detail`},{kind:`para`,text:`Langmuir defined isosteres as atoms or groups with the same number of atoms and the same number and arrangement of valence electrons. Grimm hydride displacement law generates them: adding a hydrogen atom plus an electron to an atom gives a pseudoatom that behaves like the element to its right. Erlenmeyer widened the definition to the number of peripheral electrons. Friedman coined bioisostere for isosteres that also share biological activity.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Examples of interchange`,`Illustration`],rows:[[`Monovalent classical`,`H / F, OH / NH2, Cl / SH, Br / iPr`,`5-fluorouracil from uracil`],[`Divalent classical`,`O / S / NH / CH2`,`Thiobarbiturates from barbiturates`],[`Trivalent classical`,`N / CH, P / As`,`Pyridine from benzene`],[`Tetravalent classical`,`C / Si / N+ / P+`,`Quaternary ammonium mimics`],[`Ring equivalents`,`Benzene / thiophene / pyridine`,`Sufentanil thiophene ring`],[`Non-classical`,`COOH / tetrazole / sulfonamide`,`Losartan tetrazole for carboxyl`]],caption:`Non-classical bioisosteres do not obey the electron-count rules but reproduce the key steric and electronic features, for example an ester replaced by a 1,2,4-oxadiazole.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Optimum lipophilicity varies with the barrier`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Target site`,`Approximate optimum log P`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Oral absorption, general`,`about 2`,`Balance of solubility and permeation`],[`Central nervous system`,`about 2 (1.5 to 2.7)`,`Blood-brain barrier is lipid-rich`],[`Buccal absorption`,`about 4 to 5`,`No unstirred aqueous barrier of note`],[`Non-specific cytotoxicity`,`increases with log P`,`Membrane disruption, no optimum`]]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`log (1/C) = -0.24 (log P)^2 + 1.0 (log P) + 2.0 (illustrative form)`,caption:`Differentiating a parabolic Hansch equation and setting the derivative to zero gives log P0 = k2 / (2 k1), the lipophilicity that maximises potency. This is the standard way an optimum log P is calculated in an exam.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Ferguson principle quantified`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Thermodynamic activity = Ct / Cs  (or pt / ps for a vapour)`,caption:`Ct is the molar concentration producing the effect, Cs is the saturation solubility of the same compound; pt is the partial pressure required and ps is the saturated vapour pressure.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Thermodynamic activity between about 0.01 and 1 at the effective dose indicates a physical, structurally non-specific mechanism (general anaesthetics, many preservatives).`,`Thermodynamic activity below about 0.001 indicates a structurally specific, receptor-mediated action.`,`The principle explains why chemically unrelated gases such as nitrous oxide, chloroform, cyclopropane and xenon all anaesthetise at the same thermodynamic activity, and it underlies the Meyer-Overton correlation of anaesthetic potency with olive oil solubility.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Hammett constants are not for ortho substituents`,text:`Sigma values are tabulated only for meta and para positions because an ortho substituent adds a steric contribution to the electronic one. Taft separated the two by studying ester hydrolysis under acidic conditions (steric only, giving Es) and basic conditions (steric plus electronic, giving sigma-star).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers worth memorising`,text:`sigma(para-NO2) is +0.78, sigma(para-OCH3) is -0.27, sigma(H) is 0. pi(Cl) is +0.71, pi(CH3) is +0.56, pi(OH) is -0.67, pi(H) is 0. Es(H) is 0 and Es(t-butyl) is -1.54.`}]},{topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A prodrug is a molecule that is inactive, or much less active, as given, and that is converted in the body by an enzyme or by chemistry into the active drug. Albert coined the term in 1958. The point is to fix a problem that the active drug alone cannot solve: bad taste, poor absorption, pain on injection, no water solubility, or reaching the wrong tissue.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two structural families`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carrier-linked prodrug: the active drug is joined to a temporary carrier, usually by an ester or amide bond, and the carrier is cleaved off. Example: enalapril, the ethyl ester of enalaprilat.`,`Bioprecursor prodrug: there is no carrier at all. The molecule itself is metabolically transformed, usually by oxidation or reduction, into the active species. Example: levodopa decarboxylated to dopamine.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why a prodrug is made`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Problem solved`,`Prodrug`,`Active drug released`],rows:[[`Bitter taste`,`Chloramphenicol palmitate`,`Chloramphenicol`],[`Need for an IV solution`,`Chloramphenicol succinate`,`Chloramphenicol`],[`Poor oral absorption`,`Bacampicillin, pivampicillin`,`Ampicillin`],[`Poor oral absorption`,`Enalapril`,`Enalaprilat`],[`Cannot cross blood-brain barrier`,`Levodopa`,`Dopamine`],[`Poor corneal penetration`,`Dipivefrine`,`Adrenaline`],[`Needs to act only in the colon`,`Sulfasalazine`,`5-aminosalicylic acid`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Note that chloramphenicol appears twice with opposite aims. The palmitate ester is a greasy, tasteless, water-insoluble suspension for children, hydrolysed by pancreatic lipase in the gut. The sodium succinate hemiester is water soluble and is used for injection, hydrolysed by plasma and tissue esterases.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Palmitate for the palate, succinate for the syringe`,text:`Chloramphenicol palmitate masks taste for oral suspension; chloramphenicol sodium succinate gives solubility for intravenous use.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Soft drugs and hard drugs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Soft drug: active as given, then designed to be broken down predictably to an inactive, non-toxic metabolite in one predictable step. Examples are esmolol, remifentanil, articaine and loteprednol etabonate. A soft drug is the opposite of a prodrug.`,`Hard drug: designed so that it resists metabolism altogether and is excreted unchanged, avoiding reactive metabolites and drug interactions.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single most asked pair`,text:`Prodrug is inactive and becomes active. Soft drug is active and becomes inactive. Do not confuse either with an antedrug, which is a soft drug intended to act locally and be inactivated as soon as it enters the systemic circulation.`}]},{topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable framework is the Wu classification, which sorts prodrugs by where they are converted, and the design vocabulary of rational drug design: lead identification, lead optimisation and the specific molecular tactics used to modify a lead.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Wu classification by site of conversion`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Site of bioactivation`,`Subtype`,`Example`],rows:[[`Type IA`,`Intracellular, at the target cell`,`Therapeutic target cells`,`5-fluorouracil, cyclophosphamide, zidovudine`],[`Type IB`,`Intracellular, metabolic tissue`,`Liver, gut wall, lung`,`Levodopa, carbamazepine, molsidomine`],[`Type IIA`,`Extracellular, GI fluids`,`Gut lumen or microflora`,`Sulfasalazine, loperamide oxide`],[`Type IIB`,`Extracellular, systemic fluids`,`Plasma, blood, other fluids`,`Fosphenytoin, chloramphenicol succinate`],[`Type IIC`,`Extracellular, near the target`,`Therapeutic target tissue`,`ADEPT and antibody-directed systems`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Named prodrugs you must be able to place`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Prodrug`,`Chemistry of the link`,`Activating step`,`Active form`],rows:[[`Enalapril`,`Ethyl ester`,`Hepatic esterase`,`Enalaprilat`],[`Bacampicillin`,`Ethoxycarbonyloxyethyl ester`,`Gut wall esterase`,`Ampicillin`],[`Dipivefrine`,`Dipivalyl (bis-pivalate) ester`,`Corneal esterase`,`Adrenaline`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`Azo bond`,`Colonic bacterial azoreductase`,`5-ASA + sulfapyridine`],[`Levodopa`,`No carrier (bioprecursor)`,`Aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase`,`Dopamine`],[`Chloramphenicol palmitate`,`Fatty acid ester`,`Pancreatic lipase`,`Chloramphenicol`],[`Sulindac`,`Sulfoxide (bioprecursor)`,`Reduction`,`Sulfide metabolite`],[`Cyclophosphamide`,`No carrier (bioprecursor)`,`CYP2B6 hydroxylation`,`Phosphoramide mustard`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Levodopa is the model answer`,text:`Dopamine is too polar to cross the blood-brain barrier. Levodopa is an amino acid, so it is carried across by the large neutral amino acid transporter LAT1 and then decarboxylated in the brain. Carbidopa and benserazide are peripheral decarboxylase inhibitors that do not cross the barrier, so more levodopa survives to reach the brain.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mutual prodrugs (codrugs)`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Two pharmacologically active molecules joined together, each acting as the carrier of the other.`,`Sulfasalazine: 5-aminosalicylic acid (anti-inflammatory in the colon) joined by an azo bond to sulfapyridine (the carrier, also antibacterial).`,`Sultamicillin: ampicillin joined by a methylene dioxide link to sulbactam, a beta-lactamase inhibitor.`,`Benorylate: aspirin esterified with paracetamol.`,`Estramustine: oestradiol joined by a carbamate to normustine, targeting the mustard to prostate tissue.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Rational drug design vocabulary`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Lead identification: find a starting molecule from natural products, screening, an existing drug side effect, or the endogenous ligand.`,`Lead optimisation: change the lead systematically to raise potency, selectivity and drug-likeness.`,`Homologation: lengthen an alkyl chain one CH2 at a time and look for the potency maximum.`,`Chain branching and ring-chain transformation: change flexibility and shape.`,`Isosteric and bioisosteric replacement: swap a group for one of similar size or electronics.`,`Simplification (dissection) of a complex natural lead, or conversely rigidification to lock the active conformation.`,`Prodrug formation: the last resort, applied once the pharmacodynamics are right but the pharmacokinetics are not.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Not every ester is a prodrug`,text:`Aspirin is an ester but it is not a prodrug of salicylic acid, because aspirin itself acetylates cyclo-oxygenase, which salicylic acid cannot do. A prodrug must be essentially inactive in the form administered.`}]},{topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions test the cleverness of the linker chemistry, the reasons prodrugs fail, and the boundary cases between prodrug, soft drug and active metabolite.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Linker chemistry and the cascade principle`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A drug with only a hindered hydroxyl or a carboxyl cannot always be esterified usefully, so a double ester or cascade latentiation is used: the outer ester is cleaved by esterase and the intermediate then collapses spontaneously.`,`Bacampicillin, pivampicillin and talampicillin are double esters of ampicillin; hydrolysis releases an unstable hydroxymethyl ester that decomposes to ampicillin plus formaldehyde. Pivalate esters release pivalic acid, which depletes carnitine on prolonged use.`,`Fosphenytoin is the phosphate ester of the hydroxymethyl derivative of phenytoin: water soluble, painless intramuscularly, and hydrolysed by alkaline phosphatase to phenytoin plus formaldehyde.`,`Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate and tenofovir alafenamide are two different prodrugs of the same phosphonate; alafenamide is a phosphoramidate (ProTide) that is stable in plasma and cleaved inside lymphocytes, allowing a much smaller dose.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Site-selective and targeted prodrugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Strategy`,`Trigger exploited`,`Example`],rows:[[`Colon targeting`,`Bacterial azoreductase`,`Sulfasalazine, olsalazine, balsalazide`],[`Kidney targeting`,`Renal gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase`,`Gamma-glutamyl-L-dopa`],[`Tumour targeting`,`Tumour-associated enzyme or antibody-enzyme`,`ADEPT, capecitabine`],[`Brain targeting`,`Dihydropyridine redox carrier`,`Chemical delivery systems of Bodor`],[`Eye targeting`,`Corneal esterase, higher lipophilicity`,`Dipivefrine, latanoprost`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Capecitabine is a three-step cascade: carboxylesterase in the liver, then cytidine deaminase, then thymidine phosphorylase, which is enriched in tumour tissue, finally liberating 5-fluorouracil where it is wanted.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Soft drug design in detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Inactive metabolite approach: start from a known inactive metabolite and build activity back in with a group that is cleaved to give that same metabolite. Loteprednol etabonate is designed from the inactive 17-carboxylic acid metabolite of prednisolone.`,`Soft analogue approach: insert a metabolically labile ester into an existing drug. Esmolol is a methyl ester analogue of metoprolol, hydrolysed by red cell esterases, giving a half-life of about 9 minutes.`,`Remifentanil carries a methyl propanoate ester on the piperidine nitrogen and is hydrolysed by non-specific blood and tissue esterases, so its context-sensitive half-time does not increase with infusion duration.`,`Articaine is unique among amide local anaesthetics because it carries an extra methyl ester on its thiophene ring and is inactivated in plasma.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Prodrug versus active metabolite`,text:`Codeine is often called a prodrug of morphine because CYP2D6 O-demethylates it, but codeine also has weak intrinsic activity. Terfenadine is a true prodrug of fexofenadine and was withdrawn precisely because the parent, when CYP3A4 was inhibited, blocked hERG channels; fexofenadine does not. This is the classic example of the prodrug being the toxic species.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why prodrugs fail`,text:`Incomplete or variable conversion, toxic promoiety (pivalic acid, formaldehyde), first-pass loss of the released drug, species differences in the activating enzyme, and genetic polymorphism in the activating enzyme (clopidogrel needs CYP2C19; poor metabolisers get little effect).`}]},{topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The body treats a drug as a foreign chemical and tries to make it water soluble so the kidney can excrete it. That job is done in two stages, called Phase I and Phase II, mostly in the liver, mostly in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum (recovered in the laboratory as the microsomal fraction).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two phases`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Phase I`,`Phase II`],rows:[[`Nature of reaction`,`Oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis`,`Conjugation`],[`What it does`,`Adds or exposes a functional group`,`Attaches a large polar molecule`],[`Also called`,`Functionalisation`,`Synthetic or conjugative`],[`Main enzyme system`,`Cytochrome P450`,`Transferases (UGT, SULT, GST, NAT)`],[`Product`,`Often still active, sometimes toxic`,`Almost always inactive and polar`],[`Energy needed`,`NADPH and oxygen`,`A high-energy cofactor`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Phase I reactions`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oxidation is by far the commonest: aromatic hydroxylation, aliphatic hydroxylation, N-dealkylation, O-dealkylation, S-dealkylation, N-oxidation, S-oxidation, deamination, desulfuration.`,`Reduction: azo reduction (prontosil to sulfanilamide), nitro reduction (chloramphenicol, nitrazepam), carbonyl reduction, often helped by gut bacteria.`,`Hydrolysis: esters and amides split by esterases and amidases. Esters hydrolyse faster than amides, which is why procaine lasts minutes and lignocaine lasts hours.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Phase II conjugations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Conjugation`,`Cofactor (donor)`,`Enzyme`,`Typical substrate`],rows:[[`Glucuronidation`,`UDP-glucuronic acid`,`UGT`,`Paracetamol, morphine, bilirubin`],[`Sulfation`,`PAPS`,`Sulfotransferase`,`Paracetamol, steroids`],[`Glutathione`,`Glutathione`,`GST`,`NAPQI, epoxides`],[`Acetylation`,`Acetyl coenzyme A`,`NAT1 and NAT2`,`Isoniazid, sulfonamides`],[`Methylation`,`S-adenosyl methionine`,`COMT, TPMT, NMT`,`Adrenaline, 6-mercaptopurine`],[`Amino acid (glycine)`,`Coenzyme A then glycine`,`Acyl-CoA glycine transferase`,`Benzoic acid to hippuric acid`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single commonest conjugation`,text:`Glucuronidation is the most common Phase II pathway; the cofactor is uridine diphosphate glucuronic acid and the enzyme is a microsomal UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. It is the only Phase II route that is microsomal; the rest are cytosolic (except some methylation and amino acid steps in mitochondria).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Not every conjugate is more soluble`,text:`Acetylation and methylation usually make the metabolite less water soluble than the parent. Acetylated sulfonamides are the reason older sulfonamides caused crystalluria.`}]},{topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The cytochrome P450 superfamily is a group of haem-thiolate mono-oxygenases named for the 450 nanometre absorbance of their reduced carbon monoxide complex. Six isoforms carry almost all drug oxidation, and the substrate, inhibitor and inducer lists for them are examined every single year.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`RH + O2 + NADPH + H+  ->  ROH + H2O + NADP+`,caption:`The general mono-oxygenase stoichiometry. RH is the drug, ROH the hydroxylated metabolite. One atom of molecular oxygen enters the product and the other becomes water, which is why P450s are called mixed-function oxidases.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`CYP isoforms: substrates, inhibitors, inducers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Isoform`,`Key substrates`,`Key inhibitors`,`Key inducers`],rows:[[`CYP3A4`,`Nifedipine, midazolam, ciclosporin, atorvastatin, erythromycin`,`Ketoconazole, ritonavir, clarithromycin, grapefruit juice`,`Rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, St John wort`],[`CYP2D6`,`Codeine, metoprolol, dextromethorphan, TCAs, haloperidol`,`Quinidine, fluoxetine, paroxetine, bupropion`,`Not appreciably inducible`],[`CYP2C9`,`S-warfarin, phenytoin, tolbutamide, losartan, NSAIDs`,`Fluconazole, amiodarone, metronidazole`,`Rifampicin`],[`CYP2C19`,`Omeprazole, clopidogrel, diazepam, phenytoin`,`Omeprazole, fluvoxamine, ticlopidine`,`Rifampicin`],[`CYP1A2`,`Theophylline, caffeine, clozapine, paracetamol`,`Ciprofloxacin, fluvoxamine, cimetidine`,`Tobacco smoke, charcoal-grilled meat, omeprazole`],[`CYP2E1`,`Ethanol, paracetamol, halothane, isoniazid`,`Disulfiram`,`Chronic ethanol, isoniazid`]],caption:`CYP3A4 handles roughly half of all marketed drugs; CYP2D6 is the most clinically important polymorphic isoform.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Inducers: CRAP GPS`,text:`Carbamazepine, Rifampicin, Alcohol (chronic), Phenytoin, Griseofulvin, Phenobarbitone, Sulfonylureas and Smoking. Inhibitors: the azoles, the macrolides except azithromycin, the HIV protease inhibitors, cimetidine, ciprofloxacin, valproate and grapefruit juice.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Genetic polymorphism in metabolism`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Enzyme`,`Phenotype`,`Drug affected`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`CYP2D6`,`Poor / ultrarapid metaboliser`,`Codeine`,`No analgesia / morphine toxicity`],[`CYP2C19`,`Poor metaboliser`,`Clopidogrel`,`Failure to activate, stent thrombosis`],[`NAT2`,`Slow acetylator`,`Isoniazid, hydralazine, procainamide`,`Neuropathy, drug-induced lupus`],[`TPMT`,`Low activity`,`6-mercaptopurine, azathioprine`,`Severe myelosuppression`],[`Plasma cholinesterase`,`Atypical variant`,`Suxamethonium`,`Prolonged apnoea`],[`G6PD`,`Deficiency`,`Primaquine, dapsone, nitrofurantoin`,`Haemolysis`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Paracetamol: the worked example`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`At normal dose about 90 percent is conjugated: glucuronide (major in adults) and sulfate (relatively more important in children).`,`About 5 to 10 percent is oxidised by CYP2E1 (and CYP3A4, CYP1A2) to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine, NAPQI, a reactive electrophile.`,`NAPQI is normally mopped up by glutathione and excreted as mercapturic acid.`,`In overdose the conjugation routes saturate, glutathione falls below about 30 percent of normal, and NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins causing centrilobular necrosis.`,`N-acetylcysteine is the antidote because it replenishes glutathione; it is most effective within 8 to 10 hours.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Benzodiazepines that skip Phase I`,text:`Oxazepam, temazepam and lorazepam already carry a 3-hydroxy group, so they go straight to glucuronidation. They are the safe choice in liver disease and in the elderly. Remember them as LOT.`}]},{topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced metabolism questions turn on the catalytic cycle, on which reactions are not microsomal, on unusual bioactivation, and on the arithmetic of first-pass extraction.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The P450 catalytic cycle`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Substrate binds to the ferric (Fe3+) enzyme, displacing water and shifting the spin state.`,`One electron from NADPH via NADPH-cytochrome P450 reductase reduces iron to ferrous (Fe2+).`,`Molecular oxygen binds to give an oxy-ferrous complex.`,`A second electron (from the reductase or from cytochrome b5) and two protons give the iron-oxo species, Compound I, releasing water.`,`Compound I abstracts a hydrogen from the substrate and rebounds the hydroxyl, giving the product; the ferric enzyme is regenerated.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The reductase is a flavoprotein carrying both FAD and FMN. Carbon monoxide binds the ferrous form and gives the characteristic Soret band at 450 nanometres, which is how the family was named.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Non-microsomal and unusual routes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Enzyme`,`Location`,`Reaction`,`Drug example`],rows:[[`Monoamine oxidase`,`Mitochondrial outer membrane`,`Oxidative deamination`,`Adrenaline, tyramine`],[`Alcohol dehydrogenase`,`Cytosol`,`Alcohol to aldehyde`,`Ethanol, methanol`],[`Aldehyde oxidase`,`Cytosol`,`Oxidation of N-heterocycles`,`Zaleplon, methotrexate`],[`Xanthine oxidase`,`Cytosol`,`Purine oxidation`,`6-mercaptopurine, azathioprine`],[`Plasma cholinesterase`,`Plasma`,`Ester hydrolysis`,`Suxamethonium, procaine`],[`Flavin mono-oxygenase`,`Microsomal`,`N- and S-oxidation`,`Nicotine, cimetidine`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ethanol kinetics`,text:`Alcohol dehydrogenase saturates at ordinary drinking concentrations, so ethanol follows zero-order (Michaelis-Menten saturation) kinetics at about 10 millilitres per hour. Phenytoin, aspirin at high dose and theophylline show the same capacity-limited behaviour.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Toxic bioactivation to know by name`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Paracetamol to NAPQI by CYP2E1 (hepatic necrosis).`,`Isoniazid to acetylhydrazine and hydrazine (hepatitis, worse in fast acetylators for the acetylhydrazine route).`,`Halothane oxidised by CYP2E1 to trifluoroacetyl chloride, which haptenises proteins and causes immune hepatitis.`,`Methanol to formaldehyde then formic acid by alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenase (optic nerve damage); treated with fomepizole or ethanol.`,`Cyclophosphamide to acrolein (haemorrhagic cystitis), prevented by mesna, which supplies a thiol.`,`Benzo(a)pyrene to the diol epoxide by CYP1A1 and epoxide hydrolase (carcinogenesis).`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`F(oral) = f(absorbed) x (1 - E(gut)) x (1 - E(hepatic))    with  E(hepatic) = CL(int) / (Q + CL(int))`,caption:`F is oral bioavailability, E the extraction ratio, Q hepatic blood flow (about 1.5 litres per minute), CL(int) the intrinsic clearance. High extraction drugs such as propranolol, morphine, lignocaine, glyceryl trinitrate and verapamil are flow-limited; low extraction drugs such as warfarin and phenytoin are capacity-limited and are the ones sensitive to enzyme induction.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Enterohepatic recycling`,text:`Glucuronides above about 300 to 500 daltons are excreted in bile, hydrolysed by bacterial beta-glucuronidase in the gut, and the freed drug is reabsorbed. This prolongs the half-life of oral contraceptives, morphine, rifampicin and phenolphthalein, and explains why broad-spectrum antibiotics can reduce contraceptive efficacy.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Central nervous system drugs are grouped in medicinal chemistry by their ring skeleton, because the ring decides the class. Learn the parent nucleus first, then the substituent rules hang off it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The parent nuclei`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Parent nucleus`,`Prototype drug`,`Main use`],rows:[[`Barbiturates`,`Barbituric acid (malonylurea)`,`Phenobarbitone`,`Sedative, anticonvulsant`],[`Benzodiazepines`,`5-aryl-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one`,`Diazepam`,`Anxiolytic, hypnotic`],[`Hydantoins`,`Imidazolidine-2,4-dione`,`Phenytoin`,`Tonic-clonic seizures`],[`Succinimides`,`Pyrrolidine-2,5-dione`,`Ethosuximide`,`Absence seizures`],[`Phenothiazines`,`Tricyclic S and N ring`,`Chlorpromazine`,`Antipsychotic`],[`Tricyclic antidepressants`,`Dibenzazepine or dibenzocycloheptadiene`,`Imipramine, amitriptyline`,`Depression`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Barbiturates: the first rules`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Barbituric acid itself has no depressant activity. Both hydrogens at position 5 must be replaced by carbon groups.`,`The drug is acidic because of the imide hydrogen at N1 or N3, which is why sodium salts are made for injection.`,`The total number of carbon atoms in the two C5 substituents should be about 4 to 8; too few gives no activity and more than about 10 gives convulsions rather than sedation.`,`Replacing the C2 oxygen with sulfur gives a thiobarbiturate (thiopental), which is far more lipid soluble and therefore ultra short acting.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Benzodiazepines: the first rules`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`An electron-withdrawing group at position 7 (chlorine in diazepam, nitro in nitrazepam and clonazepam) is essential.`,`The 5-phenyl ring should be unsubstituted or carry a small electron-withdrawing group at the 2 prime position (fluorine in flurazepam and midazolam).`,`They act at the GABA-A receptor and increase the frequency of chloride channel opening; barbiturates increase the duration of opening.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Frequency versus duration`,text:`Benzodiazepines increase Frequency (both start with the same idea of many short events). Barbiturates increase Duration and at high dose open the channel directly, which is why they have no ceiling and are far more lethal in overdose.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Phenytoin is 5,5-diphenylhydantoin`,text:`Aryl groups at position 5 give anticonvulsant activity without hypnotic activity. Compare with barbiturates, where the same aryl group at C5 (phenobarbitone) gives both.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This level is the substituent-by-substituent SAR that GPAT actually asks: which change lengthens duration, which change shifts a phenothiazine from antihistaminic to antipsychotic, and which nitrogen substitution turns an antidepressant from a serotonin agent into a noradrenaline agent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Barbiturate SAR`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Structural change`,`Effect`,`Example`],rows:[[`Branched or unsaturated C5 chain`,`More potent, shorter duration`,`Secobarbital, amobarbital`],[`Total C5 carbons above 10`,`Convulsant, not sedative`,`-`],[`Aryl at C5`,`Anticonvulsant selectivity`,`Phenobarbitone`],[`C2 oxygen replaced by sulfur`,`Ultra short acting`,`Thiopental`],[`N1 methylation`,`Shorter duration, faster onset`,`Methohexital`],[`Both nitrogens methylated`,`Convulsant activity`,`-`],[`Alicyclic ring at C5`,`Retains hypnotic activity`,`Cyclobarbital`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Duration of action is decided almost entirely by lipophilicity and redistribution, not by elimination. Thiopental wakes the patient in minutes because it redistributes from brain to muscle and fat, while its elimination half-life is many hours.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Benzodiazepine SAR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Position 7: electron-withdrawing group essential. Potency order roughly NO2 greater than Br greater than CF3 greater than Cl greater than H. Electron-donating groups abolish activity.`,`Positions 6, 8 and 9: any substituent decreases activity.`,`Position 5: an aryl ring is needed; 2 prime substitution with F or Cl increases activity, but 4 prime substitution decreases it.`,`Position 3: a hydroxyl (oxazepam, lorazepam, temazepam) allows direct glucuronidation, so no active metabolite is formed.`,`Position 1 and 2: a fused triazolo or imidazo ring markedly raises potency and shortens half-life (alprazolam, triazolam, midazolam).`,`The 4,5 double bond of midazolam gives a basic nitrogen, so the drug is water soluble in acid ampoule solution and closes to the lipophilic form at physiological pH.`,`The 1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one carbonyl and the 4,5 imine are both part of the pharmacophore.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Phenothiazine SAR`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Three carbon atoms must separate the ring nitrogen N10 from the basic side chain nitrogen for antipsychotic activity. Two carbons give antihistaminic and antiemetic activity instead, which is why promethazine is an antihistamine and not a neuroleptic.`,`An electron-withdrawing substituent at position 2 raises potency: CF3 (fluphenazine, trifluoperazine) greater than Cl (chlorpromazine) greater than H (promazine).`,`The side chain amine type sets the profile: piperazine gives the highest potency and most extrapyramidal effects, aliphatic dimethylamino gives sedation and hypotension, piperidine (thioridazine) gives the most anticholinergic effect and the least extrapyramidal effect.`,`Branching of the propyl chain reduces activity, so the chain must be unbranched.`,`Replacing the sulfur bridge by an ethylene bridge gives the thioxanthenes (flupenthixol, thiothixene) and by a nitrogen gives dibenzazepines.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two carbons or three`,text:`Promethazine and chlorpromazine differ mainly in that promethazine has a branched two-carbon chain. That single difference converts an antipsychotic into an antihistamine. This comparison appears repeatedly.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tricyclic antidepressant SAR`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Tricyclic system`,`Amine`,`Selectivity`],rows:[[`Imipramine`,`Dibenzazepine (iminodibenzyl)`,`Tertiary`,`Serotonin and noradrenaline`],[`Desipramine`,`Dibenzazepine`,`Secondary`,`Noradrenaline`],[`Amitriptyline`,`Dibenzocycloheptadiene`,`Tertiary`,`Serotonin and noradrenaline`],[`Nortriptyline`,`Dibenzocycloheptadiene`,`Secondary`,`Noradrenaline`],[`Doxepin`,`Dibenzoxepine`,`Tertiary`,`Serotonin, strongly antihistaminic`],[`Clomipramine`,`Chlorinated dibenzazepine`,`Tertiary`,`Most serotonin-selective TCA`]],caption:`N-demethylation of the tertiary amine in the body gives the corresponding secondary amine, which is itself a marketed drug.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Ring geometry decides the pharmacology`,text:`A seven-membered central ring makes the tricyclic system non-planar and angular, giving antidepressant activity. A six-membered central ring, as in phenothiazines, holds the molecule nearly planar and gives neuroleptic activity.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is chemistry that explains clinical behaviour: tautomerism and acidity, ring-opening, active metabolites, and the newer scaffolds.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Acidity and tautomerism of barbiturates`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Barbituric acid has pKa about 4.0 because of the acidic C5 methylene flanked by two carbonyls; 5,5-disubstituted barbiturates lose that hydrogen, so their pKa rises to about 7.4 to 8.0 and the acidity now comes from the imide NH.`,`Thiobarbiturates are more acidic (pKa about 7.4 for thiopental) and much more lipophilic than the corresponding oxybarbiturates.`,`Sodium salts are strongly alkaline in solution (thiopental solution pH about 10 to 11) and are incompatible with acidic drugs such as suxamethonium; they also hydrolyse on standing, so they are reconstituted before use.`,`Alkaline urine increases the ionised fraction of phenobarbitone and speeds its excretion, which is the basis of urinary alkalinisation in overdose.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Benzodiazepine chemistry that matters clinically`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chlordiazepoxide is a 4-oxide with a methylamino group at position 2 and no 2-carbonyl; it is the odd one out structurally.`,`Clorazepate is a prodrug: it decarboxylates in gastric acid to give nordiazepam (desmethyldiazepam).`,`Nordiazepam is the long-lived common metabolite of diazepam, clorazepate, prazepam and chlordiazepoxide, and its half-life of 40 to 100 hours explains accumulation in the elderly.`,`Flumazenil is an imidazobenzodiazepine that lacks the 5-aryl ring; it is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site.`,`Zolpidem (imidazopyridine), zaleplon (pyrazolopyrimidine) and zopiclone (cyclopyrrolone) are chemically unrelated to benzodiazepines but bind the same site with selectivity for the alpha-1 subunit.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiepileptic structure and spectrum`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Chemical class`,`Distinguishing feature`],rows:[[`Phenytoin`,`Hydantoin`,`Zero-order kinetics, gum hyperplasia`],[`Trimethadione`,`Oxazolidinedione`,`Historic absence-seizure drug`],[`Ethosuximide`,`Succinimide`,`T-type calcium channel, absence seizures`],[`Carbamazepine`,`Iminostilbene (dibenzazepine carboxamide)`,`10,11-epoxide active metabolite, autoinduction`],[`Valproate`,`Branched carboxylic acid`,`Broad spectrum, inhibits many enzymes`],[`Lamotrigine`,`Phenyltriazine`,`Folate antagonist origin, rash`],[`Vigabatrin`,`GABA analogue`,`Irreversible GABA transaminase inhibitor`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Carbamazepine looks like a tricyclic antidepressant`,text:`Carbamazepine shares the iminostilbene tricycle with imipramine but carries a carbamoyl group instead of a basic side chain, so it is an antiepileptic and mood stabiliser, not an antidepressant. Oxcarbazepine is its 10-keto analogue and avoids epoxide formation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Atypical antipsychotic scaffolds`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Clozapine and olanzapine are dibenzodiazepine and thienobenzodiazepine tricycles with a piperazine attached at the central ring, giving high 5-HT2A to D2 ratio.`,`Risperidone is a benzisoxazole and its 9-hydroxy metabolite is marketed as paliperidone.`,`Aripiprazole is a quinolinone and is a D2 partial agonist rather than an antagonist.`,`Haloperidol is a butyrophenone: the 4-fluorobutyrophenone chain plus a 4-aryl piperidine bearing a 4-hydroxyl group.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Fluoxetine versus the TCAs`,text:`Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors dropped the tricycle entirely. Fluoxetine is an aryloxypropylamine with a para-trifluoromethylphenoxy group; the trifluoromethyl group and the S enantiomer confer serotonin selectivity, and the absence of the tricycle removes the muscarinic, alpha-1 and histamine H1 blockade responsible for TCA toxicity.`}]},{topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Autonomic drugs are built around two natural transmitters, acetylcholine and noradrenaline. Almost every synthetic agent in this class is a deliberate modification of one of those two molecules, so learn the parent first.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The catecholamine skeleton`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Catechol means a benzene ring bearing hydroxyl groups at positions 3 and 4.`,`A two-carbon side chain connects the ring to a basic nitrogen; a hydroxyl on the beta carbon (the carbon next to the ring) is present in adrenaline, noradrenaline and isoprenaline.`,`Dopamine has the catechol ring and the side chain but no beta hydroxyl.`,`Catecholamines are rapidly destroyed by catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT), which methylates a ring hydroxyl, and by monoamine oxidase (MAO), which deaminates the side chain. That is why they are not orally active.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Nitrogen substituent`,`Main receptor action`],rows:[[`Noradrenaline`,`H`,`Alpha 1, alpha 2, beta 1`],[`Adrenaline`,`Methyl`,`Alpha and beta, all`],[`Isoprenaline`,`Isopropyl`,`Beta 1 and beta 2`],[`Salbutamol`,`tert-Butyl`,`Beta 2 selective`],[`Terbutaline`,`tert-Butyl`,`Beta 2 selective`]],caption:`The bigger the group on nitrogen, the more the drug moves from alpha to beta and then to beta 2.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The acetylcholine skeleton`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Acetylcholine is an ester of choline; it has a quaternary nitrogen, an ester group and a two-carbon bridge between them, the so-called five-atom rule.`,`Muscarinic agonists keep the quaternary nitrogen: carbachol has a carbamate (resistant to cholinesterase), bethanechol adds a beta-methyl for muscarinic selectivity, methacholine has the beta-methyl only.`,`Antimuscarinics are esters of an amino alcohol with a bulky aromatic acid: atropine is tropine plus tropic acid.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Bigger N, more beta`,text:`H gives alpha activity (noradrenaline), methyl gives both (adrenaline), isopropyl gives beta (isoprenaline), tert-butyl gives beta 2 (salbutamol, terbutaline).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Atropine is racemic, hyoscyamine is not`,text:`The naturally occurring alkaloid is (S)-hyoscyamine, which racemises during extraction to give atropine. Almost all the antimuscarinic activity resides in the S isomer, so hyoscyamine is about twice as potent as the racemate.`}]},{topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable SAR here is three-fold: what each part of the catecholamine does, how the beta blocker aryloxypropanolamine was invented, and what the atropine molecule needs in order to block muscarinic receptors.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Catecholamine SAR, part by part`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Structural feature`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`3,4-dihydroxy (catechol)`,`Maximum direct agonist potency, but COMT substrate`],[`3,5-dihydroxy (resorcinol)`,`Resists COMT, beta 2 selective (terbutaline)`],[`3-hydroxymethyl-4-hydroxy (saligenin)`,`Resists COMT, beta 2 selective (salbutamol)`],[`Remove one ring OH`,`Reduced direct potency, more indirect action`],[`Remove both ring OH`,`Mainly indirect, orally active, CNS entry (amphetamine)`],[`Beta hydroxyl, R configuration`,`Essential for full direct activity`],[`Alpha methyl on side chain`,`Blocks MAO, oral activity, longer action (ephedrine)`],[`N substituent larger than isopropyl`,`Beta 2 selectivity`],[`N substituent very large and lipophilic`,`Very long acting beta 2 (salmeterol, formoterol)`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Easson-Stedman hypothesis`},{kind:`para`,text:`Easson and Stedman explained the stereoselectivity of adrenaline with a three-point attachment model. The receptor recognises three features: the catechol hydroxyls, the beta hydroxyl and the protonated amine. Only the R(-) enantiomer can present all three at once.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`(R)-(-)-adrenaline is the active isomer and is roughly 10 to 50 times more potent than the (S)-(+)-isomer.`,`The (S)-(+)-isomer has about the same potency as the des-hydroxy analogue, that is, the compound in which the beta hydroxyl has been removed altogether.`,`That equality is the proof: if the S isomer merely presented the beta hydroxyl in the wrong direction, it would behave exactly like a molecule that has no beta hydroxyl at all, which is what is observed.`,`Hence a minimum of three points of interaction is required, and this is the standard general model for stereoselective drug action.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Say the S isomer equals the deoxy compound`,text:`Any question on Easson-Stedman is answered by the statement that the less active enantiomer is equipotent with the analogue lacking the chiral hydroxyl, demonstrating three-point attachment.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Beta blocker SAR`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Ar-O-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-NH-R    (aryloxypropanolamine)`,caption:`Ar is an aryl or heteroaryl ring, R is isopropyl or tert-butyl. Inserting the OCH2 group into the older arylethanolamine (like dichloroisoprenaline) raised potency roughly a hundredfold and gave the modern class.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A bulky secondary amine substituent (isopropyl or tert-butyl) is required; smaller groups give agonists rather than antagonists.`,`The S configuration at the carbinol carbon carries essentially all beta-blocking activity, for example S(-)-propranolol; the R isomer of propranolol retains some sodium-channel membrane-stabilising effect.`,`A para substituent on the aryl ring gives beta 1 selectivity: metoprolol (methoxyethyl), atenolol (carbamoylmethyl), betaxolol, bisoprolol.`,`A fused bicyclic aryl ring, such as the naphthalene of propranolol, gives non-selective blockade and high lipophilicity, hence CNS side effects.`,`Partial agonists (intrinsic sympathomimetic activity) include pindolol and acebutolol; alpha plus beta blockade is seen with labetalol and carvedilol.`,`Esmolol carries a methyl ester on the para chain and is hydrolysed by red cell esterases, giving an ultra short half-life of about 9 minutes.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antimuscarinic SAR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The classical requirement is an ester of a bulky acid with an amino alcohol: at least one aromatic or bulky cyclic ring on the acyl part, a hydroxyl or similar hydrogen-bond donor on the alpha carbon of the acid, and a cationic nitrogen about 5 to 7 angstroms from the ester.`,`Removing the hydroxymethyl group of tropic acid greatly reduces potency, showing that the hydrogen bond matters.`,`Quaternisation (ipratropium, tiotropium, methylatropine, propantheline, glycopyrrolate) prevents entry into the brain and increases ganglion blockade at high dose.`,`Hyoscine (scopolamine) differs from atropine by a 6,7-epoxide bridge; it is more lipophilic, enters the brain and is sedative and antiemetic, whereas atropine is stimulant at high dose.`,`Pirenzepine is a selective M1 blocker built on a tricyclic pyridobenzodiazepine; tiotropium is kinetically M3-selective because it dissociates very slowly from M3.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Selectivity is not the same as potency`,text:`Atenolol is less lipophilic and less potent per milligram than propranolol but is beta 1 selective. Selectivity comes from the para substituent, not from lipophilicity, and it is always relative and lost at high dose.`}]},{topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced autonomic chemistry rewards precision about stereochemistry, about the enzymes that shape duration, and about the chemistry of cholinesterase reactivation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stereochemistry summary`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Active configuration`,`Potency ratio, approximate`],rows:[[`Adrenaline`,`R(-)`,`10 to 50 to 1`],[`Noradrenaline`,`R(-)`,`About 10 to 1`],[`Propranolol`,`S(-)`,`About 100 to 1`],[`Salbutamol`,`R(-) (levalbuterol)`,`About 100 to 1`],[`Hyoscyamine`,`S(-)`,`About 100 to 1 over R`],[`Ephedrine`,`(1R,2S)-(-)-ephedrine`,`Diastereomer pseudoephedrine is weaker`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Sign of rotation is not configuration`,text:`Both adrenaline and propranolol are laevorotatory in their active form, but adrenaline is R and propranolol is S. The change of descriptor is caused by the ether oxygen changing the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priorities, not by any change in the spatial arrangement of the pharmacophore.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Metabolism that sets duration`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`COMT methylates the meta hydroxyl using S-adenosyl methionine, giving metanephrine and normetanephrine; MAO deaminates to the aldehyde and then to vanillylmandelic acid, the urinary marker of phaeochromocytoma.`,`Resorcinol and saligenin rings escape COMT entirely; an alpha methyl group escapes MAO. Salmeterol escapes both and additionally binds an exosite on the beta 2 receptor with its long lipophilic tail, giving 12 hours of action.`,`Isoprenaline is not a MAO substrate because of the isopropyl group but is a good COMT substrate, so it is inactive orally.`,`Dopamine is given as an infusion only, and dobutamine is a racemate in which the (-)-isomer is an alpha 1 agonist and the (+)-isomer an alpha 1 antagonist, the net effect being mainly beta 1.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cholinesterase inhibitors and reactivation`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Chemistry`,`Bond to the enzyme`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Edrophonium`,`Quaternary alcohol`,`Ionic and hydrogen bonding only`,`Minutes`],[`Neostigmine`,`Quaternary carbamate`,`Carbamoylated serine`,`30 to 60 minutes`],[`Physostigmine`,`Tertiary carbamate`,`Carbamoylated serine`,`Similar, enters CNS`],[`Organophosphates`,`Phosphate or phosphonate ester`,`Phosphorylated serine`,`Effectively irreversible`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Pralidoxime is a quaternary pyridinium aldoxime. Its oxime anion attacks the phosphorus of the phosphorylated enzyme and pulls the phosphoryl group off, regenerating free acetylcholinesterase. It must be given before ageing, the loss of one alkoxy group from the phosphorylated enzyme, which makes the bond permanent. Because pralidoxime is quaternary it does not enter the brain; obidoxime and the ageing time of the particular organophosphate decide practical usefulness.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Neuromuscular blocker chemistry`,text:`Suxamethonium is two acetylcholine molecules joined tail to tail and is hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase. Non-depolarising blockers are bulky bis-quaternary compounds: the aminosteroids (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium) and the benzylisoquinolines (tubocurarine, atracurium, mivacurium). Atracurium is unique in undergoing spontaneous Hofmann elimination at body pH and temperature, giving laudanosine.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Cardiovascular medicinal chemistry is organised by target: the renin-angiotensin system, calcium channels, the sodium pump, cardiac ion channels and the nitric oxide pathway. Each has a small number of scaffolds that must be recognised on sight.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main scaffolds`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Recognisable feature`,`Prototype`,`Suffix`],rows:[[`ACE inhibitors`,`Zinc-binding group plus proline-like end`,`Captopril`,`-pril`],[`Angiotensin receptor blockers`,`Biphenyl tetrazole`,`Losartan`,`-sartan`],[`Dihydropyridines`,`1,4-dihydropyridine with C4 aryl`,`Nifedipine`,`-dipine`],[`Cardiac glycosides`,`Steroid plus lactone plus sugars`,`Digoxin`,`-`],[`Statins`,`Dihydroxyheptanoic acid or lactone`,`Atorvastatin`,`-statin`],[`Organic nitrates`,`O-NO2 ester groups`,`Glyceryl trinitrate`,`-`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`ACE inhibitors in one paragraph`},{kind:`para`,text:`Angiotensin converting enzyme is a zinc metallopeptidase. Every ACE inhibitor carries a group that grips the zinc ion. Captopril uses a sulfhydryl group, most of the rest use a carboxylate, and fosinopril uses a phosphinate. Because the free diacids are poorly absorbed, nearly all are given as ethyl esters that the liver hydrolyses.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The two that are not prodrugs`,text:`Captopril and lisinopril are active as given. Every other -pril in common use, including enalapril, ramipril, quinapril, perindopril, trandolapril and fosinopril, is an ester prodrug of the corresponding -prilat.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Calcium channel blockers in one paragraph`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dihydropyridines (nifedipine, amlodipine, felodipine) act mainly on vascular smooth muscle and cause reflex tachycardia.`,`Verapamil is a phenylalkylamine and acts mainly on the heart, slowing rate and conduction.`,`Diltiazem is a benzothiazepine and sits in between.`,`All three bind the L-type calcium channel alpha-1 subunit but at three different sites.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Vessels or ventricle`,text:`Dihydropyridines go to the Vessels; Verapamil goes to the Ventricle (and the AV node); Diltiazem does a bit of both.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At this level you must be able to state the pharmacophore of each cardiovascular class and predict what happens when a given group is changed, plus place every antiarrhythmic in the Vaughan Williams scheme.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`ACE inhibitor SAR`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`A zinc-binding group: thiol (captopril, potency high but causes rash, taste loss and proteinuria), carboxylate (enalaprilat and most others), or phosphinate (fosinopril, which is the only one with significant biliary excretion).`,`A terminal carboxylate that mimics the C-terminal carboxyl of the substrate and binds a cationic site on the enzyme.`,`A hydrophobic ring, usually proline or a bicyclic proline surrogate, occupying the S2 prime pocket. Ramipril has a bicyclic, quinapril a tetrahydroisoquinoline, perindopril an octahydroindole.`,`A methyl group corresponding to the alanine side chain of the natural substrate, improving fit at S1 prime.`,`The stereochemistry must be S at each centre; the all-S diastereomer is the active one.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`ACE also degrades bradykinin, so inhibition raises bradykinin and substance P, producing the dry cough and, rarely, angioedema. Sartans, which block the AT1 receptor instead, do not raise bradykinin and therefore do not cause cough.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Dihydropyridine SAR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The 1,4-dihydropyridine ring must remain reduced; oxidation to the pyridine (which happens on exposure to light, hence the amber packaging of nifedipine) abolishes activity.`,`The C4 aryl ring must be ortho or meta substituted; a para substituent or an unsubstituted phenyl lowers activity sharply.`,`The C4 aryl ring sits nearly perpendicular to the dihydropyridine ring in the bound conformation, giving the molecule its characteristic flat-plus-vertical shape.`,`Esters at C3 and C5 are needed; making them different creates a chiral centre at C4, as in nitrendipine and amlodipine, and the enantiomers differ in potency.`,`Methyl groups at C2 and C6 are optimal.`,`Amlodipine carries a basic 2-aminoethoxymethyl side chain at C2, so it is ionised at physiological pH, binds membranes slowly and has a half-life of 35 to 50 hours.`,`Nimodipine is made more lipophilic so that it penetrates the brain and is used in subarachnoid haemorrhage.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cardiac glycoside SAR`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Structural feature`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Steroid ring fusion`,`A/B cis, B/C trans, C/D cis`],[`C14 hydroxyl`,`Beta orientation, essential`],[`C3 hydroxyl`,`Beta orientation, site of sugar attachment`],[`C17 substituent`,`Beta-oriented unsaturated lactone`],[`Lactone size`,`Five-membered butenolide = cardenolide (digoxin); six-membered = bufadienolide (squill)`],[`Saturating the lactone`,`Activity falls sharply`],[`Sugars at C3`,`Not needed for activity; they modify potency, onset and duration`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Digoxin differs from digitoxin only by an additional 12-beta hydroxyl. That single hydroxyl makes digoxin more polar, so digoxin is excreted largely unchanged by the kidney with a half-life of about 36 to 40 hours, while digitoxin is metabolised in the liver with a half-life of 5 to 7 days.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vaughan Williams classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Mechanism`,`Drugs`,`Effect on action potential`],rows:[[`IA`,`Moderate Na block, K block`,`Quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide`,`Prolongs duration and QT`],[`IB`,`Weak Na block, fast off-rate`,`Lignocaine, mexiletine, phenytoin`,`Shortens duration`],[`IC`,`Strong Na block, slow off-rate`,`Flecainide, propafenone`,`Little change in duration, widens QRS`],[`II`,`Beta adrenergic blockade`,`Propranolol, metoprolol, esmolol`,`Slows SA and AV nodes`],[`III`,`Potassium channel blockade`,`Amiodarone, sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide`,`Marked prolongation of duration`],[`IV`,`L-type calcium blockade`,`Verapamil, diltiazem`,`Slows AV node`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Amiodarone is a chemical oddity`,text:`It is a di-iodinated benzofuran with a diethylaminoethoxy side chain. Its iodine content explains the thyroid dysfunction, its extreme lipophilicity explains a volume of distribution of about 60 to 70 litres per kilogram and a half-life of 40 to 60 days, and its class III label hides the fact that it also has class I, II and IV actions.`}]},{topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced cardiovascular questions turn on stereochemistry, on the precise binding interactions, and on the chemistry of nitrates, statins and sartans.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sartan SAR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The template is an imidazole (losartan) or a related heterocycle bearing a butyl chain, linked through a methylene to a biphenyl carrying an acidic group on the distal ring.`,`The distal acidic group is a tetrazole in losartan, valsartan, candesartan, irbesartan and olmesartan; it is a non-classical bioisostere of a carboxylic acid with a similar pKa of about 4.9 but far greater lipophilicity and metabolic stability.`,`Telmisartan is the exception: it uses a carboxylic acid on a bis-benzimidazole scaffold and has the longest half-life, about 24 hours.`,`Losartan is partly a prodrug: CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 oxidise its hydroxymethyl group to the carboxylic acid EXP3174, which is 10 to 40 times more potent and insurmountable.`,`Candesartan cilexetil and olmesartan medoxomil are ester prodrugs cleaved during absorption.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Statin chemistry`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The pharmacophore is the 3,5-dihydroxyheptanoic acid chain, an exact mimic of the mevaldyl-CoA transition state of HMG-CoA reductase, with the 3R,5S configuration.`,`Lovastatin and simvastatin are given as inactive lactones that must be hydrolysed to the open hydroxy acid; pravastatin, fluvastatin, atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are given as the open acid or its salt.`,`Type 1 statins (lovastatin, simvastatin, pravastatin) are fungal-derived decalin ring compounds; type 2 statins (atorvastatin, fluvastatin, rosuvastatin) are fully synthetic and carry a fluorophenyl group plus a larger heterocycle with extra hydrogen bonding.`,`Simvastatin, lovastatin and atorvastatin are CYP3A4 substrates and interact with azoles, macrolides and grapefruit; pravastatin and rosuvastatin largely avoid CYP3A4.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Nitrates and the nitric oxide pathway`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organic nitrates are esters of nitric acid with a polyol: glyceryl trinitrate, isosorbide dinitrate and isosorbide 5-mononitrate.`,`Bioactivation to nitric oxide requires a thiol; mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase 2 is the main activating enzyme for glyceryl trinitrate at therapeutic concentrations.`,`Nitric oxide activates soluble guanylate cyclase, raising cyclic GMP, which activates protein kinase G and dephosphorylates myosin light chain, giving venodilatation at low dose.`,`Tolerance is attributed to depletion of tissue thiols, inactivation of ALDH2 and superoxide generation, and is prevented by a nitrate-free interval of 8 to 12 hours.`,`Sodium nitroprusside releases nitric oxide non-enzymatically and also cyanide, which is why thiosulfate or hydroxocobalamin is used with prolonged infusion; the solution is light sensitive and must be wrapped.`,`Sildenafil inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, preventing cyclic GMP breakdown; the combination with nitrates gives catastrophic hypotension.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Verapamil stereochemistry`,text:`Verapamil is used as a racemate. The S(-) enantiomer is roughly 10 to 20 times more potent on the AV node but is also cleared far more rapidly on first pass. Given orally, proportionally more of the weak R isomer survives, so oral verapamil is less negatively dromotropic per plasma concentration than intravenous verapamil.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Digoxin target and interaction`,text:`Digoxin binds the extracellular alpha subunit of Na+/K+-ATPase. Hypokalaemia increases binding and therefore toxicity, because potassium and digoxin compete for the same site. Quinidine, verapamil and amiodarone raise digoxin levels by inhibiting P-glycoprotein.`}]},{topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Antibacterials are the largest single block of medicinal chemistry in GPAT. The way to hold them is by chemical family, because the family fixes the mechanism, the spectrum and the pattern of resistance. Start with the ring you can draw.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The chemical families`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Family`,`Recognisable feature`,`Prototype`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Beta-lactams`,`Four-membered beta-lactam ring`,`Penicillin G`,`Blocks cell wall cross-linking`],[`Tetracyclines`,`Four fused six-membered rings (naphthacene)`,`Tetracycline`,`30S ribosome`],[`Aminoglycosides`,`Amino sugars on an aminocyclitol`,`Streptomycin`,`30S ribosome`],[`Macrolides`,`Large lactone ring plus sugars`,`Erythromycin`,`50S ribosome`],[`Chloramphenicol`,`Nitrobenzene plus dichloroacetamide`,`Chloramphenicol`,`50S peptidyl transferase`],[`Quinolones`,`Fused 4-oxo-quinoline-3-carboxylic acid`,`Ciprofloxacin`,`DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV`],[`Sulfonamides`,`para-Aminobenzenesulfonamide`,`Sulfamethoxazole`,`Blocks folate synthesis`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The beta-lactam ring`},{kind:`para`,text:`A beta-lactam is a cyclic amide in a four-membered ring. Because the ring is strained, the amide is not flat and not resonance-stabilised in the normal way, so the carbonyl is far more electrophilic than an ordinary amide. That strained carbonyl is what acylates the active-site serine of the bacterial transpeptidase, and it is also why penicillins are so easily destroyed by acid, alkali and beta-lactamase.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Penicillins: beta-lactam fused to a five-membered thiazolidine ring. The whole system is called 6-aminopenicillanic acid, 6-APA.`,`Cephalosporins: beta-lactam fused to a six-membered dihydrothiazine ring, called 7-aminocephalosporanic acid, 7-ACA.`,`The transpeptidase enzymes are also called penicillin-binding proteins, PBPs, because penicillin binds them covalently.`,`Penicillin is a structural mimic of the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the peptidoglycan chain, which is why the enzyme accepts it.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`What each big group is used for`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Narrow spectrum, mostly Gram-positive: penicillin G and V.`,`Beta-lactamase resistant (antistaphylococcal): methicillin, cloxacillin, dicloxacillin, nafcillin.`,`Broad spectrum aminopenicillins: ampicillin, amoxicillin (Gram-positive plus many Gram-negative).`,`Antipseudomonal: carbenicillin, ticarcillin, piperacillin.`,`Bacteriostatic protein-synthesis drugs: tetracyclines, macrolides, chloramphenicol. Aminoglycosides are the exception, they are bactericidal.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which ribosome subunit?`,text:`Buy AT 30, CCEL at 50. Aminoglycosides and Tetracyclines bind 30S; Chloramphenicol, Clindamycin, Erythromycin and Linezolid bind 50S.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two definitions worth marks`,text:`Penicillin G is benzylpenicillin (acid labile, injection only) and penicillin V is phenoxymethylpenicillin (acid stable because the electron-withdrawing oxygen next to the carbonyl of the side chain prevents the side-chain amide from attacking the beta-lactam ring, so it is given orally).`}]},{topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the level where the substituent rules are asked directly: which position of a quinolone gives what, which cephalosporin generation crosses into the Gram-negative side, and exactly how sulfonamides act on a pathway humans do not have.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Penicillin SAR`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The 6-APA nucleus is essential: the intact beta-lactam, the free 3-carboxyl group and the 6-acylamino side chain. Opening the beta-lactam gives inactive penicilloic acid.`,`Stereochemistry must be 2S,5R,6R; the beta-lactam nitrogen must remain non-planar (high degree of pyramidalisation) for reactivity.`,`A bulky side chain at position 6 (the isoxazolyl of cloxacillin, the 2,6-dimethoxyphenyl of methicillin) sterically shields the ring from staphylococcal penicillinase.`,`An electron-withdrawing group alpha to the side-chain carbonyl (the phenoxy oxygen of penicillin V, the alpha-amino of ampicillin) gives acid stability and oral activity.`,`An alpha-amino group (ampicillin, amoxicillin) adds hydrophilicity and Gram-negative porin entry; an alpha-carboxyl (carbenicillin) or ureido group (piperacillin) extends cover to Pseudomonas.`,`Amoxicillin differs from ampicillin only by a para-hydroxyl on the phenyl ring, which raises oral absorption from about 40 percent to about 75 to 90 percent.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cephalosporin generations and the spectrum shift`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Generation`,`Examples`,`Gram-positive`,`Gram-negative`,`Notes`],rows:[[`First`,`Cefazolin, cefalexin, cefadroxil`,`Best`,`Limited`,`No CNS entry`],[`Second`,`Cefuroxime, cefaclor, cefoxitin`,`Good`,`Better`,`Cefoxitin (a cephamycin) covers anaerobes`],[`Third`,`Cefotaxime, ceftriaxone, ceftazidime`,`Less`,`Much better`,`Cross into CSF; ceftazidime covers Pseudomonas`],[`Fourth`,`Cefepime, cefpirome`,`Good again`,`Very broad`,`Zwitterionic, fast porin entry, stable to AmpC`],[`Fifth`,`Ceftaroline, ceftobiprole`,`Covers MRSA`,`Broad`,`Bind PBP2a`]],caption:`The general trend across generations one to four is decreasing Gram-positive cover and increasing Gram-negative cover, increasing beta-lactamase stability, and increasing CSF penetration from the third generation onwards. The fourth generation regains Gram-positive activity.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cephalosporin SAR: position 7 side chain sets the antibacterial spectrum and the beta-lactamase stability; position 3 sets the pharmacokinetics, and an acetoxymethyl group at C3 is hydrolysed by esterases to a less active alcohol.`,`A syn (Z) methoxyimino group on an aminothiazole ring at position 7, as in cefotaxime and ceftriaxone, gives high stability to beta-lactamases; the anti isomer is far less stable.`,`A methoxy group at C7 alpha gives the cephamycins (cefoxitin), which resist beta-lactamases and cover anaerobes.`,`An N-methylthiotetrazole side chain at C3 (cefoperazone, cefamandole, cefotetan) causes a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol and hypoprothrombinaemia.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The other beta-lactams`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Subclass`,`Ring fused to beta-lactam`,`Examples`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Carbapenem`,`Five-membered carbon ring, no sulfur, trans H`,`Imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem`,`Broadest spectrum of all beta-lactams`],[`Monobactam`,`None (monocyclic)`,`Aztreonam`,`Aerobic Gram-negative only, safe in penicillin allergy`],[`Beta-lactamase inhibitor`,`Oxapenam or penicillanic sulfone`,`Clavulanic acid, sulbactam, tazobactam`,`Weak antibacterials, potent suicide inhibitors`],[`Non-beta-lactam inhibitor`,`Diazabicyclooctane`,`Avibactam`,`Reversible, covers class C and some class D`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Imipenem is hydrolysed by renal dehydropeptidase-I, so it is combined with cilastatin, which inhibits that enzyme. Meropenem carries a 1-beta-methyl group that blocks the same enzyme, so it needs no cilastatin. Carbapenems owe their beta-lactamase stability partly to a trans configuration of the C5 and C6 hydrogens and a hydroxyethyl side chain, unlike the cis arrangement of penicillins.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resistance to beta-lactams`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Beta-lactamase production: hydrolysis of the beta-lactam amide bond to the inactive open acid. This is the commonest mechanism.`,`Altered penicillin-binding protein: PBP2a in MRSA (mecA gene) and altered PBPs in penicillin-resistant pneumococci. Beta-lactamase inhibitors are useless here.`,`Reduced permeability: loss of outer membrane porins in Gram-negative organisms.`,`Active efflux pumps.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Clavulanic acid is a suicide substrate`,text:`It is an oxapenam (oxygen replaces the sulfur of the penicillin thiazolidine) with no bulky C6 side chain. The enzyme opens it, but the resulting acyl-enzyme rearranges to an irreversibly bound species, so the inhibition is mechanism-based and irreversible. Combinations: clavulanic acid with amoxicillin or ticarcillin, sulbactam with ampicillin, tazobactam with piperacillin.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tetracyclines`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The nucleus is an octahydronaphthacene-2-carboxamide, that is, four linearly fused six-membered rings labelled A to D.`,`Essential for activity: the C1 to C3 keto-enol system with the C2 carboxamide (ring A), the C10 phenol, the C11 and C12 keto-enol (the BCD chromophore), the C4 dimethylamino group in the alpha orientation and the C12a hydroxyl.`,`The keto-enol systems chelate divalent and trivalent metal ions: calcium, magnesium, aluminium and iron. Chelation with calcium is why tetracyclines stain growing teeth and are avoided in children under 8 and in pregnancy, and chelation with dietary cations in milk and antacids is why absorption falls sharply.`,`Epimerisation at C4 in mildly acidic solution gives 4-epitetracycline, which is inactive. In strong acid, dehydration at C6 gives anhydrotetracycline and then, on epimerisation, epianhydrotetracycline, which is nephrotoxic and causes a Fanconi-like syndrome from degraded old stock.`,`Removing the C6 hydroxyl gives doxycycline and minocycline: more lipophilic, better absorbed, longer acting and chemically stable, since there is no C6 OH to eliminate.`,`Doxycycline is the safest in renal impairment because it is largely eliminated in faeces rather than by the kidney.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Aminoglycosides`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Structure: two or more amino sugars linked by glycosidic bonds to an aminocyclitol ring. Streptomycin has streptidine; gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin, kanamycin and neomycin have 2-deoxystreptamine.`,`They are strongly basic polycations, so they are highly water soluble, not absorbed orally, not distributed to the CNS, and excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration.`,`They bind the 30S subunit at the 16S rRNA A site, causing misreading of mRNA and blocking translocation. They are bactericidal and show concentration-dependent killing with a post-antibiotic effect.`,`Uptake into bacteria is oxygen dependent, so aminoglycosides do not work on anaerobes; the same transport is enhanced by cell-wall-active drugs, which is the basis of the penicillin plus gentamicin synergy.`,`Resistance is mainly by bacterial acetyltransferases, phosphotransferases and adenylyltransferases. Amikacin, which is kanamycin A acylated with an L-hydroxyaminobutyryl (AHBA) side chain, resists most of these enzymes sterically.`,`Suffix rule: names ending in -mycin come from Streptomyces (streptomycin, tobramycin), names ending in -micin come from Micromonospora (gentamicin, netilmicin).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Macrolides and chloramphenicol`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Macrolides have a large lactone ring with a sugar (desosamine, an amino sugar) and usually a second sugar (cladinose). Erythromycin has a 14-membered ring, clarithromycin is its 6-O-methyl ether, azithromycin is a 15-membered ring azalide made by inserting a methylated nitrogen into the ring (Beckmann rearrangement of the oxime).`,`Erythromycin is destroyed by gastric acid: the C6 hydroxyl attacks the C9 ketone giving an inactive hemiketal and then anhydroerythromycin, which is a motilin agonist and causes the cramping. Methylating the C6 hydroxyl (clarithromycin) or removing the C9 ketone (azithromycin) stops this, giving acid stability.`,`Macrolides bind the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and block translocation of the peptide chain; erythromycin and clarithromycin are strong CYP3A4 inhibitors, azithromycin is not.`,`Chloramphenicol is a nitrobenzene derivative with a dichloroacetamide group and two chiral centres. Only the D-threo (1R,2R) isomer is active. The para-nitro group can be replaced bioisosterically by a methylsulfonyl group to give thiamphenicol.`,`Chloramphenicol inhibits peptidyl transferase on the 50S subunit. It causes dose-related reversible marrow suppression and an idiosyncratic irreversible aplastic anaemia attributed to the nitro group, plus grey baby syndrome from immature glucuronidation.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Chelation is not the same as inactivation by degradation`,text:`Tetracycline taken with milk or an antacid loses absorption because the chelate is not absorbed, not because the drug is chemically destroyed. The genuine chemical destruction routes are C4 epimerisation and C6 dehydration.`}]},{topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced antibacterial chemistry is mostly quinolone and sulfonamide SAR position by position, plus the folate pathway argument for selective toxicity and the classification of beta-lactamases.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Quinolone SAR, position by position`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Position`,`Group`,`Contribution`],rows:[[`N1`,`Cyclopropyl (best), ethyl, aryl`,`Potency; cyclopropyl gives broadest spectrum`],[`C2`,`Must stay hydrogen`,`Any substituent blocks binding`],[`C3`,`Carboxylic acid`,`Essential, binds the enzyme-DNA complex with Mg2+`],[`C4`,`Ketone (4-oxo)`,`Essential, chelates Mg2+ with the C3 acid`],[`C5`,`Amino or methyl`,`Raises Gram-positive activity`],[`C6`,`Fluorine`,`Up to 100-fold potency, better cell penetration`],[`C7`,`Piperazine or pyrrolidine`,`Gram-negative and antipseudomonal activity`],[`C8`,`Methoxy (or halogen)`,`Anaerobic cover; halogen raises phototoxicity`]],caption:`The C3 carboxylate and the C4 ketone together form the magnesium-chelating pair that anchors the drug to the DNA-gyrase complex; both are absolutely required.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The parent is nalidixic acid, a naphthyridine (nitrogen at position 8), a first-generation agent used only as a urinary antiseptic.`,`Target: DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) is the primary target in Gram-negative organisms; topoisomerase IV is the primary target in Gram-positive organisms.`,`The C3 carboxyl also chelates aluminium, magnesium, calcium, iron and zinc, so antacids and iron salts reduce absorption in exactly the way they do for tetracyclines.`,`A bulky C7 substituent, as in the aminopyrrolidine of moxifloxacin, reduces the GABA-receptor interaction responsible for seizures and reduces theophylline interaction. Ciprofloxacin, which carries a plain piperazine, is the strongest CYP1A2 inhibitor of the class.`,`A C8 fluorine (lomefloxacin, sparfloxacin) increases phototoxicity; a C8 methoxy (moxifloxacin, gatifloxacin) improves anaerobic cover and lowers resistance selection.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sulfonamide SAR`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The nucleus is para-aminobenzenesulfonamide (sulfanilamide). The amino group must be para to the sulfonamide; ortho and meta isomers are inactive.`,`The N4 aromatic amine must be free, or be convertible to a free amine in the body. Sulfasalazine and succinylsulfathiazole are prodrugs that release the free amine only in the gut.`,`N1 substitution with an electron-withdrawing heterocycle lowers the pKa towards 6 to 7.5, giving maximum activity because a large fraction is ionised at plasma pH and the ionised form resembles the PABA anion most closely.`,`The sulfur must remain directly attached to the benzene ring, and replacing the SO2NH2 group destroys activity, although the carboxamide and other groups give weak analogues.`,`Sulfonamides are competitive inhibitors of dihydropteroate synthase and are therefore bacteriostatic; the effect is antagonised by excess PABA and by pus, which supplies thymidine and purines.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The reason sulfonamides are selectively toxic is that bacteria must make folate from scratch. Bacteria condense para-aminobenzoic acid with pteridine using dihydropteroate synthase; humans have no such enzyme because we absorb preformed folate from the diet through a specific transporter. So the drug has a target that simply does not exist in the host.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`PABA + pteridine -> (dihydropteroate synthase) -> dihydropteroate -> dihydrofolate -> (dihydrofolate reductase) -> tetrahydrofolate`,caption:`Sulfonamides block the first arrow (dihydropteroate synthase) by mimicking PABA. Trimethoprim blocks the second named enzyme, dihydrofolate reductase. Tetrahydrofolate is the one-carbon carrier needed for thymidylate and purine synthesis.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why co-trimoxazole is synergistic`,text:`Sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim block two consecutive steps of the same pathway, which is called sequential blockade. Each alone is bacteriostatic; the pair is bactericidal. Trimethoprim is a 2,4-diaminopyrimidine with roughly 50,000 to 100,000 times greater affinity for bacterial than for human dihydrofolate reductase, which is the source of the selectivity. The conventional ratio is 1 part trimethoprim to 5 parts sulfamethoxazole in the formulation, chosen because it gives about 1 to 20 in plasma, the optimum synergy ratio.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Beta-lactamase classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Ambler class`,`Active site`,`Examples`,`Inhibited by clavulanate?`],rows:[[`A`,`Serine`,`TEM, SHV, CTX-M (ESBLs), KPC`,`Yes, mostly`],[`B`,`Zinc (metallo)`,`NDM-1, IMP, VIM`,`No`],[`C`,`Serine`,`AmpC, cephalosporinases`,`No`],[`D`,`Serine`,`OXA type oxacillinases`,`Variably`]],caption:`Bush-Jacoby is the alternative functional classification. Class B metallo-beta-lactamases hydrolyse carbapenems and are not touched by clavulanate, sulbactam or tazobactam.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two hydrolysis products with different names`,text:`Alkaline or enzymatic opening of the beta-lactam of penicillin gives penicilloic acid. Acid treatment gives penillic acid via the side-chain amide oxygen attacking the ring. Penicilloyl-protein conjugate is the major antigenic determinant responsible for penicillin allergy.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Glycopeptides and others`,text:`Vancomycin is a glycopeptide that binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the peptidoglycan precursor itself, not the enzyme, so beta-lactamase is irrelevant. Resistance in VRE comes from replacing the terminal D-alanine with D-lactate, which loses one hydrogen bond of the five and drops affinity about a thousandfold. Linezolid (an oxazolidinone) binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit and prevents initiation complex formation, and it is a weak reversible monoamine oxidase inhibitor.`}]},{topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Viruses use the host cell machinery, and fungi are eukaryotes like us, so both classes have very few targets that the human cell does not share. Every drug in this topic exists because someone found one of those few differences: a viral enzyme, or ergosterol instead of cholesterol in the fungal membrane.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiviral families`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Chemical nature`,`Example`,`Target`],rows:[[`Nucleoside analogues`,`Modified sugar or base`,`Acyclovir, zidovudine`,`Viral DNA or RNA polymerase`],[`Non-nucleoside RT inhibitors`,`Diverse small heterocycles`,`Nevirapine, efavirenz`,`Allosteric site on HIV-1 RT`],[`Protease inhibitors`,`Peptidomimetics`,`Saquinavir, ritonavir`,`HIV aspartyl protease`],[`Neuraminidase inhibitors`,`Sialic acid analogues`,`Oseltamivir, zanamivir`,`Influenza neuraminidase`],[`Uncoating inhibitors`,`Tricyclic amine (adamantane)`,`Amantadine`,`Influenza A M2 ion channel`],[`Interferons`,`Glycoproteins (biologicals)`,`Interferon alfa-2b`,`Host antiviral state`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antifungal families`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Example`,`Target`,`Effect on the fungus`],rows:[[`Polyenes`,`Amphotericin B, nystatin`,`Binds ergosterol in the membrane`,`Pores form, fungicidal`],[`Azoles`,`Fluconazole, ketoconazole`,`14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51)`,`Ergosterol synthesis blocked`],[`Allylamines`,`Terbinafine, naftifine`,`Squalene epoxidase`,`Squalene accumulates, fungicidal`],[`Echinocandins`,`Caspofungin, micafungin`,`1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase`,`Cell wall fails`],[`Antimetabolite`,`Flucytosine`,`Thymidylate synthase and RNA`,`Fungistatic`],[`Griseofulvin`,`Griseofulvin`,`Microtubules of the fungus`,`Fungistatic, keratin-seeking`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The membrane sterol of a fungus is ergosterol; the membrane sterol of a human cell is cholesterol. Every azole and every polyene exploits that one difference. Echinocandins go one better and attack the fungal cell wall, a structure human cells do not have at all, which is why they are the best tolerated of the systemic antifungals.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Azoles block, polyenes poke`,text:`Azoles block the synthesis of ergosterol (an enzyme inhibitor). Polyenes poke holes by binding the ergosterol that is already there (a physical, binding action).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The single most asked antiviral fact`,text:`Acyclovir is selective because the first phosphorylation is done by the viral thymidine kinase, an enzyme that only infected cells contain. Host cell kinases then add the second and third phosphates. So the active triphosphate accumulates only inside infected cells.`}]},{topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At this level you must be able to say what has been changed in each nucleoside analogue relative to the natural nucleoside, and what each antifungal class does to the ergosterol pathway and where in that pathway it acts.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Nucleoside and nucleotide analogues`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Structural change from the natural nucleoside`,`Activated by`,`Virus`],rows:[[`Acyclovir`,`Acyclic side chain replacing the sugar ring (guanine base)`,`Viral thymidine kinase then host kinases`,`HSV, VZV`],[`Ganciclovir`,`Acyclovir plus an extra hydroxymethyl`,`CMV UL97 phosphotransferase`,`CMV`],[`Zidovudine (AZT)`,`3 prime azido replaces 3 prime OH of thymidine`,`Host cellular kinases`,`HIV`],[`Lamivudine (3TC)`,`Oxathiolane ring, L (unnatural) configuration`,`Host cellular kinases`,`HIV, hepatitis B`],[`Stavudine, didanosine`,`2 prime,3 prime dideoxy or unsaturated`,`Host kinases`,`HIV`],[`Tenofovir`,`Acyclic phosphonate (already a nucleotide)`,`Needs only two more phosphates`,`HIV, hepatitis B`],[`Ribavirin`,`Triazole carboxamide base on ribose`,`Host kinases`,`RSV, hepatitis C (older regimens)`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Every one of these works as a chain terminator, an obligate one when there is no 3 prime hydroxyl to accept the next nucleotide, because the phosphodiester bond simply cannot be formed.`,`Zidovudine has an azido group where the 3 prime hydroxyl should be; lamivudine and the dideoxy drugs have no 3 prime oxygen at all; acyclovir has no 3 prime carbon at all.`,`Acyclovir triphosphate is also about 30 times a better substrate for the viral DNA polymerase than for the human polymerase alpha, which is a second layer of selectivity on top of the thymidine kinase step.`,`Valaciclovir (L-valyl ester) and famciclovir (diacetyl ester of penciclovir) are prodrugs made purely to raise oral bioavailability; valaciclovir uses the peptide transporter PEPT1.`,`Lamivudine and emtricitabine are unusual in being the unnatural L-enantiomers, which is exactly why they are poor substrates for human mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma and cause far less mitochondrial toxicity than zidovudine or stavudine.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The rest of the HIV toolbox`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (nevirapine, efavirenz, etravirine, rilpivirine) need no phosphorylation. They bind a hydrophobic allosteric pocket about 10 angstroms from the catalytic site and lock the enzyme in an inactive conformation. They are non-competitive, active against HIV-1 only, not HIV-2, and a single point mutation (K103N, Y181C) confers high-level resistance.`,`Protease inhibitors are peptidomimetics built around a hydroxyethylene or hydroxyethylamine core, a non-hydrolysable transition-state isostere replacing the scissile peptide bond. They block cleavage of the gag-pol polyprotein, so the virions released are immature and non-infectious. Names end in -navir.`,`Ritonavir is used at sub-therapeutic dose purely as a pharmacokinetic booster because it is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor; cobicistat is a booster with no antiviral activity of its own.`,`Integrase strand transfer inhibitors (raltegravir, dolutegravir) chelate the two magnesium ions in the integrase active site.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Influenza drugs and interferons`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Neuraminidase (sialidase) cleaves sialic acid so that new virions can be released from the infected cell. Zanamivir and oseltamivir carboxylate are transition-state analogues of the sialic acid oxocarbenium ion, built on a dihydropyran (zanamivir) or cyclohexene (oseltamivir) ring.`,`Zanamivir is too polar to be absorbed orally, so it is inhaled. Oseltamivir replaces the polar glycerol side chain with a lipophilic 3-pentyloxy group and is given as the ethyl ester prodrug, hydrolysed by hepatic carboxylesterase to the active carboxylate.`,`Both work on influenza A and B; amantadine and rimantadine work only on influenza A because the M2 proton channel is present only in A, and resistance is now near universal.`,`Interferons alfa, beta and gamma are host glycoproteins. They do not kill the virus directly; they induce cellular proteins such as 2 prime,5 prime-oligoadenylate synthetase, RNase L and a protein kinase that shuts down viral protein translation. Pegylation extends the half-life from hours to about 40 hours, allowing weekly dosing.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Azole antifungals`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Subclass`,`Azole ring`,`Examples`,`Use`],rows:[[`Imidazole`,`Two nitrogens`,`Clotrimazole, miconazole, ketoconazole, econazole`,`Mostly topical`],[`Triazole`,`Three nitrogens`,`Fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, posaconazole`,`Mostly systemic`]],caption:`Moving from two nitrogens to three raises selectivity for fungal CYP51 over human P450s, improves metabolic stability and lengthens the half-life, which is why triazoles displaced imidazoles for systemic use.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The free (unsubstituted) nitrogen of the azole ring, N3 of an imidazole or N4 of a triazole, binds the haem iron of the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase, also called CYP51 or ERG11.`,`Demethylation of lanosterol at C14 is therefore blocked.`,`Ergosterol is not made, and 14-methylated sterols accumulate instead.`,`The membrane loses its normal fluidity and permeability, and membrane-bound enzymes fail, so the effect is fungistatic for most azoles on most yeasts.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why ketoconazole causes endocrine effects`,text:`Ketoconazole is an imidazole and is not selective, so it also inhibits human 17,20-lyase and 11-beta-hydroxylase, giving gynaecomastia, reduced testosterone and adrenal suppression. Fluconazole, a triazole, is far more selective for fungal CYP51 and does not do this at usual doses.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Amphotericin B is not an azole`,text:`It is a polyene macrolide from Streptomyces nodosus with a large lactone ring carrying seven conjugated double bonds on one face and seven hydroxyls on the other, plus a mycosamine sugar and a carboxyl group, so it is amphoteric and amphipathic. It binds ergosterol and forms a pore, and it does not inhibit any enzyme.`}]},{topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The advanced material is the fine detail of selectivity, of formulation chemistry for amphotericin B, and of the exact step each antifungal hits in the sterol pathway.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The ergosterol pathway, step by step`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Acetyl-CoA -> HMG-CoA -> mevalonate -> squalene -> (squalene epoxidase) -> squalene 2,3-epoxide -> lanosterol -> (14-alpha-demethylase) -> ... -> ergosterol`,caption:`Terbinafine and naftifine inhibit squalene epoxidase, so squalene accumulates to toxic levels inside the fungal cell, which makes allylamines fungicidal. Azoles inhibit 14-alpha-demethylase further down, so the effect is loss of ergosterol, which is mostly fungistatic. Amphotericin B acts on the finished ergosterol in the membrane.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Terbinafine is highly lipophilic and keratinophilic, concentrating in skin, nails and adipose tissue; it is the drug of choice for onychomycosis and is a CYP2D6 inhibitor.`,`Griseofulvin is a spiro-benzofuran isolated from Penicillium griseofulvum. It binds fungal tubulin, disrupting the mitotic spindle, and it is deposited in newly formed keratin, so it must be taken until the infected keratin has grown out. Absorption is markedly improved by a fatty meal and by micronisation, and it is a strong enzyme inducer, which is why it reduces the effect of warfarin and of oral contraceptives.`,`Flucytosine (5-fluorocytosine) is a pyrimidine analogue that is taken into the fungus by cytosine permease and deaminated by fungal cytosine deaminase to 5-fluorouracil; human cells lack that deaminase, which is the basis of the selectivity. Gut bacteria do have it, which is why marrow toxicity still occurs. It is always used in combination, classically with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis, because resistance emerges quickly on monotherapy.`,`Echinocandins are semisynthetic lipopeptides (a cyclic hexapeptide with a fatty acyl tail) that non-competitively inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase. They are too large for oral absorption and are given intravenously; they are fungicidal against Candida and fungistatic against Aspergillus, and they have no activity against Cryptococcus, whose wall has little 1,3-beta-glucan.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Amphotericin B: chemistry, toxicity and formulation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Selectivity is only relative: amphotericin B binds ergosterol with higher affinity than cholesterol, but it binds cholesterol too, which is the source of its nephrotoxicity and infusion reactions.`,`Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate is a micellar dispersion, not a true solution, and must be reconstituted in dextrose, never in saline, because electrolytes precipitate the micelles.`,`Lipid formulations (liposomal amphotericin B, lipid complex, colloidal dispersion) reduce nephrotoxicity by keeping the drug associated with lipid until it reaches the fungal ergosterol, which competes for it more strongly than host cholesterol does.`,`Nystatin is chemically similar (a tetraene-diene polyene) but is too toxic for parenteral use, so it is confined to oral thrush and topical candidiasis; it is not absorbed from the gut.`,`Hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia from renal tubular potassium wasting are the classic electrolyte effects; a test dose and pre-medication address the acute infusion reaction.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resistance mechanisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug class`,`Main resistance mechanism`],rows:[[`Azoles`,`ERG11 point mutation, ERG11 overexpression, CDR and MDR efflux pumps`],[`Polyenes`,`Reduced or altered membrane ergosterol content`],[`Echinocandins`,`FKS1 hot-spot mutation in glucan synthase`],[`Flucytosine`,`Loss of cytosine permease or cytosine deaminase`],[`Nucleoside antivirals`,`Loss of viral thymidine kinase, or altered DNA polymerase`],[`NNRTIs`,`Single pocket mutation, K103N or Y181C`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Two different fluorinated pyrimidines`,text:`Flucytosine is an antifungal that must be deaminated to 5-fluorouracil inside the fungus. 5-Fluorouracil itself is an anticancer antimetabolite. Giving flucytosine with 5-fluorouracil, or to a patient with an unusual gut flora load, raises the systemic 5-fluorouracil exposure and the marrow toxicity.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Azole drug interactions`,text:`All azoles inhibit human CYP3A4 to some degree; ketoconazole and itraconazole most, voriconazole also inhibits CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, and fluconazole is a notable CYP2C9 inhibitor (raising warfarin and phenytoin). Itraconazole and ketoconazole need gastric acid for dissolution, so proton pump inhibitors and antacids reduce their absorption, whereas fluconazole absorption is pH independent.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Zidovudine specifics`,text:`Zidovudine is cleared mainly by glucuronidation, not by CYP, and its dose-limiting toxicity is bone marrow suppression with macrocytic anaemia. Mitochondrial toxicity from inhibition of DNA polymerase gamma explains the myopathy and the lactic acidosis with hepatic steatosis seen with the older nucleoside analogues, and it is the reason stavudine and didanosine were abandoned.`}]},{topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Anticancer drugs are classified by what they do to the dividing cell. Almost all the classical agents attack DNA, either by damaging it, by starving it of building blocks, or by wrecking the spindle that pulls the chromosomes apart. The newer agents attack a specific protein that a particular tumour depends on.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The six classical groups`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`What it does`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Alkylating agents`,`Attach alkyl groups to DNA, cross-link the strands`,`Cyclophosphamide, cisplatin, carmustine`],[`Antimetabolites`,`Mimic a normal metabolite and block its enzyme`,`Methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil, 6-mercaptopurine`],[`Mitotic inhibitors`,`Disrupt microtubules of the spindle`,`Vincristine, paclitaxel`],[`Topoisomerase inhibitors`,`Trap the enzyme that unwinds DNA`,`Etoposide, irinotecan`],[`Antitumour antibiotics`,`Intercalate DNA, generate free radicals`,`Doxorubicin, bleomycin`],[`Hormonal and targeted agents`,`Block a receptor or a kinase the tumour needs`,`Tamoxifen, imatinib`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cell cycle specificity`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cell cycle specific drugs kill only cells in a particular phase, so they need prolonged exposure and are given by infusion. Antimetabolites act in S phase; vinca alkaloids, taxanes and bleomycin act in M or G2 phase.`,`Cell cycle non-specific drugs kill resting cells as well, so they are dose dependent and can be given as a bolus. Alkylating agents, platinum compounds, anthracyclines and nitrosoureas are non-specific.`,`The log kill hypothesis of Skipper says a given dose kills a constant fraction of cells, not a constant number, which is why repeated cycles are required.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why the side effects are what they are`},{kind:`para`,text:`Classical cytotoxics cannot tell a cancer cell from any other fast-dividing cell, so the toxicity falls on bone marrow (myelosuppression), gut lining (mucositis and diarrhoea), hair follicles (alopecia) and gonads (infertility). Nausea and vomiting come from the chemoreceptor trigger zone, and hyperuricaemia comes from the purines released by dying cells.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Specific toxicities to attach to names`,text:`Cyclophosphamide gives haemorrhagic cystitis (acrolein, prevented by mesna). Doxorubicin gives cardiomyopathy. Bleomycin gives pulmonary fibrosis. Cisplatin gives nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity. Vincristine gives peripheral neuropathy. Methotrexate gives mucositis and marrow suppression, rescued by folinic acid.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The one drug that does not suppress marrow`,text:`Bleomycin and vincristine are the classic marrow-sparing cytotoxics. Bleomycin is inactivated by a hydrolase that is scarce in lung and skin, which is exactly where its toxicity appears.`}]},{topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable chemistry here is the alkylation mechanism, the exact enzyme each antimetabolite blocks, and the fact that the two mitotic inhibitor families do opposite things to the same protein.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Alkylating agents`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Subclass`,`Examples`,`Distinguishing point`],rows:[[`Nitrogen mustards`,`Mechlorethamine, cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan`,`Bis(2-chloroethyl)amine core`],[`Nitrosoureas`,`Carmustine (BCNU), lomustine (CCNU), streptozocin`,`Lipophilic, cross the blood-brain barrier`],[`Alkyl sulfonates`,`Busulfan`,`Selective for myeloid cells, pulmonary fibrosis`],[`Aziridines`,`Thiotepa, mitomycin C`,`Already contain the three-membered ring`],[`Triazenes`,`Dacarbazine, temozolomide`,`Methylate O6 of guanine`],[`Platinum compounds`,`Cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin`,`Not true alkylators, but cross-link DNA`]]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The nitrogen lone pair of a nitrogen mustard attacks its own beta carbon and expels chloride, giving a strained, highly reactive aziridinium (ethyleneimonium) ion.`,`The aziridinium ion is opened by a nucleophile on DNA, most often the N7 of guanine, giving the first alkylation.`,`The second chloroethyl arm repeats the process, giving the second alkylation.`,`If the two alkylations are on opposite strands the result is an interstrand cross-link, which is the lethal lesion because the strands can no longer separate for replication.`,`Alkylated N7-guanine is also more acidic, which favours mispairing with thymine and depurination, adding mutagenic damage on top of the cross-link.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Aliphatic mustards (mechlorethamine) are so reactive that they are vesicants and must be given by fast-running intravenous line. Attaching an aromatic ring (chlorambucil, melphalan) delocalises the nitrogen lone pair, slows aziridinium formation and gives orally usable, less reactive drugs.`,`Cyclophosphamide is a bioprecursor prodrug: CYP2B6 hydroxylates it at C4 to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which equilibrates with aldophosphamide and then fragments to phosphoramide mustard, the alkylating species, plus acrolein, the bladder toxin.`,`Cisplatin is cis-diamminedichloroplatinum(II). Inside the cell, where the chloride concentration is low, the chlorides are displaced by water to give an aquated, positively charged complex that binds two adjacent N7 guanines, forming mainly intrastrand cross-links. Only the cis isomer works, because the trans isomer cannot reach two adjacent bases.`,`Carboplatin replaces the two chlorides with a cyclobutane dicarboxylate leaving group; it aquates far more slowly, so it is much less nephrotoxic and emetic but more myelosuppressive.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antimetabolites`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Natural molecule mimicked`,`Enzyme or step blocked`],rows:[[`Methotrexate`,`Folic acid`,`Dihydrofolate reductase`],[`Pemetrexed`,`Folic acid`,`Thymidylate synthase (multi-target)`],[`5-Fluorouracil`,`Uracil`,`Thymidylate synthase (as FdUMP)`],[`Cytarabine`,`Deoxycytidine`,`DNA polymerase, chain termination`],[`Gemcitabine`,`Deoxycytidine`,`DNA polymerase and ribonucleotide reductase`],[`6-Mercaptopurine`,`Hypoxanthine`,`De novo purine synthesis`],[`6-Thioguanine`,`Guanine`,`Purine synthesis and incorporation`],[`Hydroxyurea`,`-`,`Ribonucleotide reductase`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Methotrexate is 4-amino-10-methyl folic acid: replacing the 4-hydroxy of the pteridine with an amino group and methylating N10 raises the affinity for dihydrofolate reductase enormously, roughly a thousandfold over the natural substrate, and makes the binding essentially irreversible at usual concentrations.`,`Because dihydrofolate reductase is blocked, tetrahydrofolate is not regenerated, thymidylate synthase stalls and the cell dies for want of thymidine (thymineless death). Folinic acid (leucovorin, N5-formyl tetrahydrofolate) bypasses the block and is the rescue agent; folic acid itself is useless as a rescue because it still needs the blocked enzyme.`,`5-Fluorouracil is uracil with fluorine replacing the C5 hydrogen, a classical monovalent bioisostere. Its nucleotide FdUMP forms a stable ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and the cofactor N5,N10-methylene tetrahydrofolate; the carbon-fluorine bond cannot be broken, so the enzyme is trapped. Leucovorin is added to 5-fluorouracil regimens to stabilise that complex and thereby increase, not reduce, its effect.`,`6-Mercaptopurine is hypoxanthine with sulfur at position 6. It is converted by HGPRT to thioinosinic acid, which blocks the first committed step of purine synthesis. It is inactivated by xanthine oxidase, so allopurinol raises its levels and the dose must be cut to about a quarter. TPMT-deficient patients get severe myelosuppression.`,`Cytarabine is cytosine attached to arabinose instead of ribose. The 2 prime hydroxyl is in the opposite (arabino) configuration, which distorts the sugar and terminates the growing chain. It is deaminated rapidly by cytidine deaminase, hence the need for continuous infusion.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mitotic inhibitors: opposite mechanisms, same target`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Vinca alkaloids`,`Taxanes`],rows:[[`Source`,`Catharanthus roseus (vinca)`,`Taxus brevifolia and T. baccata`],[`Binding site`,`Vinca domain on beta-tubulin`,`Taxane site on beta-tubulin, inner surface`],[`Action on tubulin`,`Prevents polymerisation`,`Promotes and stabilises polymerisation`],[`Net effect on spindle`,`Microtubules disassemble`,`Microtubules cannot disassemble`],[`Cell cycle arrest`,`Metaphase (M phase)`,`G2/M phase`],[`Main toxicity`,`Vincristine: neuropathy; vinblastine: marrow`,`Neuropathy, marrow, hypersensitivity to the vehicle`]],caption:`Both drug families stop mitosis, but for opposite reasons. The spindle must both build and dismantle; block either half and the cell arrests.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Colchicine, vinca and taxol`,text:`Colchicine and the vinca alkaloids both inhibit polymerisation but at different sites (the colchicine site and the vinca site). Paclitaxel does the opposite and stabilises the polymer. Do not group paclitaxel with the vinca alkaloids just because both are spindle poisons.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Vesicant warning`,text:`Vinca alkaloids, doxorubicin and mechlorethamine are vesicants; extravasation causes tissue necrosis. Vincristine must never be given intrathecally, which is invariably fatal.`}]},{topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions cover the two topoisomerases and which drug traps which, the chemistry of the anthracyclines, the hormonal agents, and the molecular targets that define the tyrosine kinase inhibitors.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Topoisomerase inhibitors`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Enzyme`,`Strand break made`,`ATP needed`,`Inhibitors`],rows:[[`Topoisomerase I`,`Single strand`,`No`,`Camptothecin, topotecan, irinotecan`],[`Topoisomerase II`,`Double strand`,`Yes`,`Etoposide, teniposide, anthracyclines, mitoxantrone`]],caption:`Both classes act by stabilising the cleavable complex between enzyme and DNA, so the break made by the enzyme is never resealed. They are therefore topoisomerase poisons rather than simple catalytic inhibitors.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Camptothecin comes from Camptotheca acuminata and carries a pentacyclic ring with an alpha-hydroxy lactone (ring E) that must stay closed for activity; at physiological pH the lactone opens to the inactive carboxylate, which is why the water-soluble analogues topotecan and irinotecan were made.`,`Irinotecan is a prodrug hydrolysed by carboxylesterase to SN-38, which is about a thousand times more potent; SN-38 is inactivated by UGT1A1, so patients with Gilbert syndrome (UGT1A1*28) get severe diarrhoea and neutropenia.`,`Etoposide and teniposide are semisynthetic glycosides of podophyllotoxin from Podophyllum. Podophyllotoxin itself binds tubulin, but the etoposide derivatives lost that activity and act instead on topoisomerase II, an important reversal of mechanism on modification.`,`Etoposide arrests cells in the late S and G2 phase; secondary acute myeloid leukaemia with an 11q23 (MLL) translocation is its characteristic late complication.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antitumour antibiotics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Anthracyclines (doxorubicin, daunorubicin, epirubicin, idarubicin) have a planar tetracyclic aglycone (an anthraquinone) plus the amino sugar daunosamine. They intercalate between base pairs, poison topoisomerase II, and undergo one-electron reduction of the quinone to a semiquinone, which redox cycles and produces superoxide and hydroxyl radicals.`,`Doxorubicin and daunorubicin differ only by a hydroxyl on the C14 side chain: doxorubicin has a hydroxyacetyl group, daunorubicin a plain acetyl. That single hydroxyl broadens doxorubicin from leukaemia only to solid tumours as well.`,`Cardiotoxicity is attributed to iron-dependent free radical damage in a tissue poor in catalase; dexrazoxane, an iron chelator and EDTA analogue, is the protective agent, and the total lifetime dose of doxorubicin is capped around 450 to 550 milligrams per square metre.`,`Bleomycin is a glycopeptide that chelates iron; the drug-Fe(II)-oxygen complex generates radicals that cause single and double strand breaks, preferentially at GC and GT sequences. It is cell cycle specific for G2 and M.`,`Dactinomycin (actinomycin D) intercalates at GC pairs and blocks RNA polymerase, so it inhibits transcription rather than replication. Mitomycin C is bioreductively activated and cross-links DNA, behaving as an alkylating agent and being most active in hypoxic tumour cells.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hormonal agents`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Chemistry`,`Action`],rows:[[`Tamoxifen`,`Triphenylethylene, Z isomer`,`SERM: antagonist in breast, agonist in endometrium and bone`],[`Raloxifene`,`Benzothiophene`,`SERM: no endometrial agonism`],[`Fulvestrant`,`Steroidal 7-alpha-alkylsulfinyl oestradiol`,`Pure antagonist, degrades the receptor`],[`Anastrozole, letrozole`,`Non-steroidal triazoles`,`Reversible aromatase inhibitors`],[`Exemestane`,`Steroidal androstenedione analogue`,`Irreversible (suicide) aromatase inactivator`],[`Flutamide, bicalutamide`,`Non-steroidal anilides`,`Androgen receptor antagonists`],[`Leuprolide, goserelin`,`GnRH peptide analogues`,`Continuous use downregulates the pituitary`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tamoxifen is a prodrug in effect: CYP2D6 converts it to endoxifen (4-hydroxy-N-desmethyltamoxifen), which is roughly a hundred times more potent, so CYP2D6 poor metabolisers and patients on paroxetine may get less benefit.`,`The Z (cis) isomer of tamoxifen is the antioestrogen; the E isomer is actually oestrogenic, a good example of geometrical isomerism reversing pharmacology.`,`Aromatase (CYP19) converts androgens to oestrogens, and it is the only source of oestrogen after the menopause, so aromatase inhibitors are used in postmenopausal women only. In premenopausal women they would trigger a reflex rise in gonadotrophin and increase ovarian oestrogen.`,`Aromatase inhibitors cause osteoporosis and arthralgia; tamoxifen instead protects bone but raises the risk of endometrial carcinoma and venous thromboembolism, a direct consequence of its tissue-selective agonism.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Targeted small molecules`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Molecular target`,`Disease`],rows:[[`Imatinib`,`Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase, also c-Kit and PDGFR`,`Chronic myeloid leukaemia, GIST`],[`Dasatinib, nilotinib`,`Bcr-Abl, active against most imatinib mutants`,`Imatinib-resistant CML`],[`Gefitinib, erlotinib`,`EGFR (HER1) tyrosine kinase`,`Non-small-cell lung cancer with EGFR mutation`],[`Osimertinib`,`EGFR including the T790M mutant`,`Resistant NSCLC`],[`Lapatinib`,`EGFR and HER2`,`Breast cancer`],[`Trastuzumab`,`HER2 extracellular domain (monoclonal antibody)`,`HER2-positive breast cancer`],[`Rituximab`,`CD20 on B cells (monoclonal antibody)`,`B-cell lymphoma`]],caption:`Suffix rule: -tinib denotes a small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor, -mab denotes a monoclonal antibody, -ximab is chimeric, -zumab is humanised and -umab is fully human.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Imatinib is a 2-phenylaminopyrimidine. It binds the ATP pocket of Bcr-Abl but only when the kinase is in its inactive (DFG-out) conformation, which is what gives it selectivity; this is called type II kinase inhibition. Bcr-Abl is the constitutively active fusion kinase produced by the t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome translocation. The T315I gatekeeper mutation removes a hydrogen bond and blocks imatinib, dasatinib and nilotinib alike, and only ponatinib retains activity against it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rescue and protective agents`,text:`Folinic acid rescues methotrexate. Mesna binds acrolein and prevents haemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide. Dexrazoxane protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity. Amifostine protects against cisplatin nephrotoxicity. Allopurinol or rasburicase prevents tumour lysis hyperuricaemia. Glucarpidase is the rescue for methotrexate toxicity in renal failure.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Antagonism between two antimetabolites`,text:`Methotrexate given shortly before 5-fluorouracil is synergistic, because raising the phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate pool increases 5-fluorouracil activation. Given in the reverse order it is antagonistic. Sequence matters in combination chemotherapy and is a favourite examination point.`}]},{topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic bundles three families that GPAT asks together: the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, the steroids, and the diuretics. All three are learned the same way, by recognising a parent skeleton and then reading off what each substituent does.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`NSAID chemical classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Chemical parent`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Salicylates`,`Salicylic acid`,`Aspirin, sodium salicylate, diflunisal`],[`Propionic acids`,`2-Arylpropionic acid (profens)`,`Ibuprofen, naproxen, ketoprofen, flurbiprofen`],[`Acetic acids`,`Aryl or heteroaryl acetic acid`,`Indomethacin, diclofenac, ketorolac, sulindac`],[`Fenamates`,`N-aryl anthranilic acid`,`Mefenamic acid, flufenamic acid`],[`Oxicams`,`Enolic acid (benzothiazine carboxamide)`,`Piroxicam, tenoxicam, meloxicam`],[`Pyrazolones`,`Pyrazolidinedione`,`Phenylbutazone, oxyphenbutazone`],[`Coxibs`,`Diaryl heterocycle`,`Celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib`],[`Para-aminophenol`,`Aniline derivative`,`Paracetamol`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Almost every NSAID is an acid with a pKa of about 3 to 5. That acidity is why they are highly bound to plasma albumin, why they concentrate in inflamed tissue and in the gastric mucosa where they cause direct irritation, and why they displace warfarin and sulfonylureas from albumin. The two big exceptions are paracetamol, which is a phenol and essentially neutral, and the coxibs, which are neutral sulfonamide-bearing heterocycles.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What NSAIDs actually inhibit`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cyclo-oxygenase, COX, converts arachidonic acid into prostaglandin G2 and then H2, from which all the other prostaglandins, prostacyclin and thromboxane are made.`,`COX-1 is constitutive: it sits permanently in the stomach (protective mucus and bicarbonate), the kidney (renal blood flow) and the platelet (thromboxane A2).`,`COX-2 is inducible: it appears at sites of inflammation under the influence of cytokines. It is also constitutively present in the kidney, brain and vascular endothelium, where it makes prostacyclin.`,`Blocking COX-2 gives the wanted anti-inflammatory effect; blocking COX-1 gives most of the gastric and bleeding side effects.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The steroid nucleus`},{kind:`para`,text:`All steroids are built on cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene: three fused six-membered rings labelled A, B and C, plus one five-membered ring labelled D. The 17 carbons of that skeleton are numbered in a fixed order, and the angular methyl groups at C10 and C13 are numbered 18 and 19.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parent hydrocarbon`,`Carbons`,`Hormone family`],rows:[[`Oestrane`,`18`,`Oestrogens (aromatic ring A, no C19 methyl)`],[`Androstane`,`19`,`Androgens (testosterone)`],[`Pregnane`,`21`,`Corticosteroids and progestogens`],[`Cholestane`,`27`,`Cholesterol, the biosynthetic parent`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Alpha is behind, beta is in front`,text:`A substituent drawn with a dashed bond is alpha (below the plane of the ring); a substituent drawn with a bold wedge is beta (above the plane). The C18 and C19 methyl groups are always beta, and they define which face is which.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Aspirin is the odd NSAID`,text:`Aspirin is the only NSAID that inhibits COX irreversibly, by acetylating serine 530 of COX-1 and serine 516 of COX-2. Since a platelet has no nucleus and cannot make new enzyme, the effect lasts the whole 7 to 10 day platelet lifespan, which is why low-dose aspirin works as an antiplatelet drug.`}]},{topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This level is the SAR: what each substituent on an NSAID or a steroid contributes, and the chemistry that distinguishes the diuretic classes from one another.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`General NSAID SAR`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`An acidic group is required, usually a carboxylic acid, or an enol (oxicams) or a sulfonamide or sulfone (coxibs) acting as its bioisostere. This group forms an ionic bond with arginine 120 in the COX channel.`,`The acidic centre must be separated from a flat, lipophilic aromatic system by one or two atoms.`,`A second aryl ring or a bulky lipophilic group, usually out of the plane of the first ring, fits a hydrophobic pocket and raises potency.`,`Substituents ortho to the acid on the ring force the two rings out of plane, which improves the fit; diclofenac is the model, its two ortho chlorines twisting the rings by about 70 degrees.`,`For the arylpropionic acids, an alpha-methyl group raises potency and the S enantiomer carries essentially all the activity.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Acidic group`,`SAR points`],rows:[[`Salicylates`,`COOH with ortho OH`,`Ortho OH essential; acetylation of it gives aspirin`],[`Profens`,`Alpha-methyl arylacetic acid`,`S isomer active; para substitution optimal`],[`Indomethacin`,`Indole-3-acetic acid`,`p-Chlorobenzoyl on N1, 5-methoxy, 2-methyl`],[`Diclofenac`,`Phenylacetic acid`,`2,6-dichloro on the anilino ring twists the rings`],[`Fenamates`,`Anthranilic acid (N-aryl)`,`Substitution on the N-aryl ring at 2, 3 and 6 raises activity`],[`Oxicams`,`Enolic OH, pKa about 4 to 6`,`Long half-life; no carboxyl at all`],[`Coxibs`,`Neutral, sulfonamide or sulfone`,`Two vicinal aryl rings on a central heterocycle`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`COX-1 versus COX-2 selectivity`},{kind:`para`,text:`The COX-1 and COX-2 active sites are almost identical, with one decisive difference. At position 523 COX-1 has isoleucine and COX-2 has valine. Valine is one methyl group smaller, and that missing methyl opens a side pocket off the main channel that exists only in COX-2. A drug carrying a bulky group, typically a phenylsulfonamide or methylsulfone, that reaches into that side pocket can bind COX-2 but simply will not fit COX-1.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Selectivity`,`Drugs`,`Consequence`],rows:[[`COX-1 preferential`,`Aspirin (low dose), ketorolac, indomethacin, piroxicam`,`Most gastric and antiplatelet effect`],[`Non-selective`,`Ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac`,`Balanced`],[`COX-2 preferential`,`Meloxicam, nimesulide, etodolac`,`Less gastric injury at usual dose`],[`COX-2 selective (coxibs)`,`Celecoxib, etoricoxib, parecoxib`,`Least gastric injury, raised cardiovascular risk`]],caption:`Selectivity is a ratio, not an absolute, and it is lost at high dose. It is expressed as the ratio of the IC50 for COX-1 to that for COX-2.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why coxibs raise cardiovascular risk`,text:`Endothelial COX-2 makes prostacyclin, which is antiplatelet and vasodilator. Platelet thromboxane A2 comes from COX-1 and is untouched by a coxib. Removing prostacyclin while leaving thromboxane intact tips the balance towards thrombosis. Rofecoxib was withdrawn for exactly this reason.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Paracetamol is a weak inhibitor of both isoforms in the presence of the high peroxide concentrations found at inflamed sites, which is why it is analgesic and antipyretic but a poor anti-inflammatory. It acts mainly centrally, where peroxide tone is low, and is often attributed to inhibition of a COX-3 splice variant, though the human relevance of COX-3 is disputed. Because it is neutral and not acidic, it does not damage the gastric mucosa and does not affect platelets.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Glucocorticoid SAR`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Structural change`,`Effect`],rows:[[`4,5 double bond and 3-ketone in ring A`,`Essential for all corticoid activity`],[`C11 beta-hydroxyl`,`Essential for glucocorticoid activity`],[`C17 alpha-hydroxyl`,`Raises glucocorticoid activity`],[`C21 hydroxyl with C20 ketone`,`Needed; C21 is the site of esterification`],[`1,2 double bond (delta-1)`,`Raises glucocorticoid 4-fold, salt retention little changed`],[`9-alpha-fluoro`,`Raises both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid activity strongly`],[`16-alpha or 16-beta methyl (or 16-hydroxyl)`,`Abolishes mineralocorticoid (salt-retaining) activity`],[`6-alpha-methyl or 6-alpha-fluoro`,`Raises glucocorticoid potency modestly`]]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Cortisol (hydrocortisone) is the reference, with glucocorticoid potency 1 and appreciable salt retention.`,`Adding the 1,2 double bond gives prednisolone: about 4 times the glucocorticoid activity with less salt retention.`,`Adding a 9-alpha-fluoro to that gives a very potent but strongly salt-retaining compound; fludrocortisone is 9-alpha-fluorohydrocortisone and is used precisely as a mineralocorticoid.`,`Adding a 16-methyl group on top of the 9-alpha-fluoro cancels the salt retention and leaves the high glucocorticoid potency: this is dexamethasone (16-alpha-methyl) and betamethasone (16-beta-methyl), each about 25 to 30 times cortisol with no mineralocorticoid action.`,`Triamcinolone uses a 16-alpha-hydroxyl for the same purpose, and its acetonide is the topical form.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Fluorine up, methyl down`,text:`The 9-alpha-fluoro pushes both activities up; the 16-substituent pushes the mineralocorticoid activity back down. Dexamethasone is the end product of doing both.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diuretic chemistry at a glance`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Chemical feature`,`Site in nephron`,`Example`],rows:[[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors`,`Unsubstituted sulfonamide on a thiadiazole`,`Proximal tubule`,`Acetazolamide`],[`Loop diuretics`,`Sulfamoyl anthranilic or phenoxyacetic acid`,`Thick ascending limb`,`Furosemide, bumetanide`],[`Thiazides`,`Benzothiadiazine-7-sulfonamide 1,1-dioxide`,`Distal convoluted tubule`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`],[`Potassium sparing (ENaC)`,`Pyrazinoylguanidine or pteridine`,`Collecting duct`,`Amiloride, triamterene`],[`Aldosterone antagonists`,`Spirolactone steroid`,`Collecting duct`,`Spironolactone, eplerenone`],[`Osmotic`,`Polyol, freely filtered`,`Whole tubule, mainly loop`,`Mannitol`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ethacrynic acid is the sulfonamide-free loop diuretic`,text:`Furosemide, bumetanide, torasemide, the thiazides and acetazolamide all carry a sulfonamide group and cross-react in sulfa allergy. Ethacrynic acid is a phenoxyacetic acid with an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone (a Michael acceptor that reacts with sulfhydryl groups), has no sulfonamide, and is therefore the choice in genuine sulfonamide allergy.`}]},{topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions probe stereochemistry, the exact chemical requirements of the diuretic pharmacophores, the specific steroids used as sex hormones, and quantitative potency comparisons.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Stereochemistry and metabolic inversion of the profens`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`For all 2-arylpropionic acids the S(+) enantiomer is the eutomer; for ibuprofen the S isomer is roughly 100 to 160 times more potent as a COX inhibitor than the R.`,`Ibuprofen is nevertheless marketed as the racemate because an alpha-methylacyl-CoA racemase carries out unidirectional metabolic inversion of R to S in vivo, so much of the inactive isomer is converted to the active one. The reverse inversion does not occur.`,`Naproxen is marketed as the pure S(+) enantiomer, because its R isomer is hepatotoxic. Dexibuprofen and dexketoprofen are the single-enantiomer versions of the corresponding racemates.`,`The inversion proceeds through a coenzyme A thioester, so it also produces hybrid triglycerides in adipose tissue, a curiosity of the profen class.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Salicylate detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The ortho hydroxyl of salicylic acid is essential; the meta and para isomers are inactive as analgesics. The intramolecular hydrogen bond between the ortho OH and the carboxyl accounts for the low pKa of about 3.0.`,`Aspirin is acetylsalicylic acid, pKa about 3.5. It hydrolyses on storage in moist air to salicylic acid and acetic acid, which is the vinegar smell of old tablets, and it must be assayed for free salicylic acid.`,`Salicylate elimination is capacity limited: at low dose the half-life is 2 to 3 hours, at anti-inflammatory dose 15 to 30 hours, because the glycine (salicyluric acid) and glucuronide routes saturate.`,`Alkalinisation of the urine markedly increases salicylate excretion, since salicylate is a weak acid trapped in its ionised form at high urinary pH; this is the basis of treatment in overdose.`,`Diflunisal is a difluorophenyl salicylic acid that is not acetylated, so it does not inhibit platelets irreversibly and is not associated with Reye syndrome.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Corticosteroid potency table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Steroid`,`Glucocorticoid potency`,`Mineralocorticoid potency`,`Equivalent dose, mg`],rows:[[`Hydrocortisone`,`1`,`1`,`20`],[`Prednisolone`,`4`,`0.8`,`5`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`5`,`0.5`,`4`],[`Triamcinolone`,`5`,`0`,`4`],[`Dexamethasone`,`25 to 30`,`0`,`0.75`],[`Betamethasone`,`25 to 30`,`0`,`0.75`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`10`,`125 to 150`,`-`],[`Aldosterone`,`0.3`,`3000`,`-`]],caption:`These equivalent doses and ratios are asked directly. Note that fludrocortisone is used for its mineralocorticoid action despite a glucocorticoid potency of about 10.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Prednisone and cortisone are 11-keto compounds and are therefore prodrugs; they must be reduced by hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 to prednisolone and cortisol. In severe liver disease and topically they are ineffective, so prednisolone is preferred.`,`Aldosterone circulates largely as the 11,18-hemiacetal; its selectivity at the mineralocorticoid receptor in the kidney is protected by 11-beta-HSD type 2, which converts cortisol to inactive cortisone. Liquorice inhibits that enzyme and produces apparent mineralocorticoid excess.`,`Beclometasone dipropionate, budesonide and fluticasone propionate are designed for inhalation with high first-pass metabolism, so the swallowed fraction is destroyed and systemic exposure is minimised.`,`C21 esters (acetate, succinate, phosphate) alter solubility only: the sodium succinate and sodium phosphate esters are water soluble for injection, while the acetate is an insoluble depot for intra-articular use.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sex steroid chemistry`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oestradiol has an aromatic ring A with a C3 phenolic hydroxyl and no C19 methyl group; aromatisation is what removes C19. Oral oestradiol is destroyed by first pass, so ethinyl oestradiol carries a 17-alpha-ethynyl group, which blocks oxidation of the 17-hydroxyl and makes it orally active.`,`The same trick is used for progestogens: adding a 17-alpha-ethynyl group to 19-nortestosterone gives norethisterone, and adding it to progesterone-like skeletons gives the orally active gestagens. Medroxyprogesterone acetate uses a 6-alpha-methyl and a 17-acetate instead.`,`Testosterone is 17-beta-hydroxyandrost-4-en-3-one. Oral testosterone is destroyed by first pass, so esters of the 17-beta-hydroxyl (propionate, enanthate, cypionate) are used as oily depots, while 17-alpha-methyltestosterone is orally active but hepatotoxic.`,`Finasteride and dutasteride are 4-azasteroids that inhibit 5-alpha-reductase, so testosterone is not converted to the more potent dihydrotestosterone; they are used in benign prostatic hyperplasia and androgenetic alopecia.`,`Mifepristone is a 19-norsteroid with a bulky 11-beta-dimethylaminophenyl group; that bulk converts it from an agonist into a progesterone and glucocorticoid receptor antagonist.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diuretic SAR in detail`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors require an unsubstituted sulfonamide, SO2NH2, attached to an aromatic or heteroaromatic ring; the ionised nitrogen coordinates the active-site zinc. Any substitution on that nitrogen abolishes activity. Acetazolamide is 5-acetamido-1,3,4-thiadiazole-2-sulfonamide.`,`Thiazides are benzothiadiazine-1,1-dioxides that need a sulfamoyl group at C7, an electron-withdrawing group (usually chlorine or trifluoromethyl) at C6, and saturation of the 3,4 double bond, which gives the hydrothiazides and raises potency about tenfold. A lipophilic substituent at C3 markedly prolongs the action, as in the dichloromethyl group of hydroflumethiazide analogues and the benzylthiomethyl of benzthiazide.`,`Chlorthalidone, indapamide and metolazone are thiazide-like: they keep the sulfamoyl group and the chlorine but abandon the benzothiadiazine ring entirely, and chlorthalidone has a half-life of 40 to 60 hours against 6 to 15 hours for hydrochlorothiazide.`,`Loop diuretics of the sulfamoylbenzoic type (furosemide, bumetanide, piretanide) need a sulfamoyl group meta to a carboxyl, a halogen or phenoxy or anilino group at the other meta position, and an activating substituent at the para position; bumetanide replaces the chlorine of furosemide with a phenoxy and the furfurylamino with a butylamino, giving about 40 times the potency.`,`Potassium-sparing agents block the epithelial sodium channel directly: amiloride is a pyrazinoylguanidine, and the strongly basic guanidine group, protonated at all physiological pH values, is what plugs the channel. Triamterene is a pteridine. Neither depends on aldosterone, unlike spironolactone.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Two opposite effects on calcium`,text:`Thiazides reduce urinary calcium and are used in idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium stones, and they help preserve bone density. Loop diuretics increase urinary calcium and are used in hypercalcaemia. Both cause hypokalaemia, hyperuricaemia and hyperglycaemia; thiazides cause hyponatraemia more often, and loop diuretics cause ototoxicity.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Spironolactone versus eplerenone`,text:`Spironolactone is a 17-spirolactone steroid and binds androgen and progesterone receptors as well as the mineralocorticoid receptor, which is why it causes gynaecomastia and menstrual irregularity. Eplerenone carries a 9,11-epoxide and a 7-carbomethoxy group in place of the 7-thioacetyl of spironolactone; that change gives far greater selectivity and almost no antiandrogenic effect, at the cost of lower potency.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-1`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The minimum three-dimensional arrangement of atoms or groups that is essential for biological activity is called the:`,options:[`Auxophore`,`Bioisostere`,`Pharmacophore`,`Lead compound`],answer:2,explanation:`The pharmacophore is the essential feature set; removing any part of it abolishes activity. An auxophore is a non-essential group that only modifies potency, duration or absorption, which is why it is the most tempting wrong answer here.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-2`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which solvent pair is used to measure the partition coefficient P of a drug?`,options:[`n-Octanol and water`,`Chloroform and water`,`Ether and water`,`Olive oil and water`],answer:0,explanation:`n-Octanol is the standard reference phase because its hydroxyl head and long alkyl chain mimic a membrane phospholipid. Olive oil and water was the historical Meyer-Overton system for anaesthetic potency, not the modern log P standard.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-3`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Hammett equation log (Kx / Kh) = sigma . rho, the reaction constant rho is defined as 1 for:`,options:[`Ester hydrolysis of substituted benzoates under acid conditions`,`Hydrolysis of substituted acetates in alkali at 25 degrees Celsius`,`Partitioning of substituted benzenes between octanol and water`,`Ionisation of substituted benzoic acids in water at 25 degrees Celsius`],answer:3,explanation:`Hammett defined the reference reaction as the ionisation of substituted benzoic acids in water at 25 degrees Celsius, and set rho for that reaction to 1 so that sigma values could be derived. Ester hydrolysis is the reference for the Taft steric parameter Es, not for sigma.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-4`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement correctly distinguishes Free-Wilson analysis from Hansch analysis?`,options:[`Free-Wilson uses measured pi and sigma constants; Hansch uses indicator variables`,`Free-Wilson uses indicator variables of 0 or 1 and cannot predict outside the training set`,`Free-Wilson requires a parabolic term in log P; Hansch does not`,`Free-Wilson is applicable only to compounds with a single aromatic ring`],answer:1,explanation:`Free-Wilson is a de novo additive model in which each substituent gets an indicator variable of 1 if present and 0 if absent, so it cannot extrapolate to substituents not in the set. Hansch is the extrathermodynamic approach that uses measured constants such as pi, sigma and Es and therefore can extrapolate.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-5`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weak acid of pKa 4.4 is placed in plasma at pH 7.4. What percentage exists in the ionised form?`,options:[`About 99.9 percent`,`About 90.9 percent`,`About 50 percent`,`About 9.1 percent`],answer:0,explanation:`pH - pKa = 7.4 - 4.4 = 3, so log ([ionised]/[unionised]) = 3 and the ratio is 1000 to 1. The ionised fraction is 1000/1001, which is 99.9 percent. The tempting 90.9 percent answer corresponds to a difference of only one pH unit (a 10 to 1 ratio).`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-6`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The replacement of the carboxylic acid of an angiotensin receptor blocker by a tetrazole ring, as in losartan, is an example of:`,options:[`A monovalent classical bioisostere`,`A divalent classical bioisostere`,`A non-classical bioisostere`,`A ring-chain transformation`],answer:2,explanation:`Tetrazole does not obey the electron-count and valence rules of classical isosterism, but it reproduces the acidity (pKa about 4.9) and the charge distribution of a carboxylate while being more lipophilic and metabolically stable, so it is a non-classical bioisostere. Monovalent classical pairs are things like H for F or OH for NH2.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-7`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For a parabolic Hansch equation log (1/C) = -0.25 (log P)^2 + 1.20 (log P) + 1.8, the optimum log P is:`,options:[`1.2`,`2.4`,`4.8`,`0.6`],answer:1,explanation:`Differentiate and set to zero: log P0 = k2 / (2 k1) = 1.20 / (2 x 0.25) = 2.4. Choosing 4.8 comes from forgetting the factor of 2 in the denominator, and 1.2 comes from reading off the linear coefficient directly.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-8`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A series of chemically unrelated gases including nitrous oxide, cyclopropane and xenon all produce anaesthesia at the same thermodynamic activity. This observation is explained by:`,options:[`The Hammett free energy relationship`,`Grimm hydride displacement law`,`The Topliss decision tree`,`The Ferguson principle`],answer:3,explanation:`Ferguson showed that for structurally non-specific agents the biological effect correlates with thermodynamic activity, Ct / Cs, rather than with absolute concentration, so unrelated molecules act at the same relative saturation. Hammett relationships describe electronic substituent effects within one reaction series and say nothing about non-specific depressants.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-1`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Chloramphenicol palmitate is used in preference to chloramphenicol in paediatric suspensions mainly to:`,options:[`Increase water solubility for injection`,`Target the drug to the colon`,`Mask the bitter taste`,`Prolong the plasma half-life`],answer:2,explanation:`The palmitate is a tasteless, water-insoluble ester hydrolysed by pancreatic lipase in the gut, so it exists to solve a palatability problem. The water-soluble injectable form is the sodium succinate, which is the distractor most often chosen.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-2`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT a prodrug?`,options:[`Lisinopril`,`Enalapril`,`Ramipril`,`Fosinopril`],answer:0,explanation:`Captopril and lisinopril are the two ACE inhibitors that are active as given. Enalapril, ramipril and fosinopril are all ester or phosphinate ester prodrugs hydrolysed in the liver to the corresponding -prilat.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-3`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the Wu classification, sulfasalazine is best placed as:`,options:[`Type IA`,`Type IB`,`Type IIB`,`Type IIA`],answer:3,explanation:`Sulfasalazine is cleaved extracellularly in the gastrointestinal lumen by bacterial azoreductase, which is Type IIA. Type IIB is extracellular conversion in systemic fluids such as plasma, which is where fosphenytoin and chloramphenicol succinate belong.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-4`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement about soft drugs is correct?`,options:[`They are inactive as given and are activated by metabolism`,`They are active as given and are designed to break down predictably to inactive metabolites`,`They resist metabolism completely and are excreted unchanged`,`They are always administered as esters of a natural amino acid`],answer:1,explanation:`A soft drug is the conceptual opposite of a prodrug: it is active as administered and carries a deliberately labile group so that one predictable step inactivates it, as with esmolol and remifentanil. A molecule that is inactive as given and is activated by metabolism is a prodrug, and one that resists metabolism entirely is a hard drug.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-5`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Levodopa is preferred to dopamine in Parkinson disease because levodopa:`,options:[`Is carried across the blood-brain barrier by an amino acid transporter`,`Is a more potent dopamine receptor agonist`,`Is not a substrate for monoamine oxidase anywhere in the body`,`Is converted to dopamine only by gut bacteria`],answer:0,explanation:`Dopamine is too polar to cross the blood-brain barrier, whereas levodopa is an amino acid and uses the large neutral amino acid transporter LAT1, after which brain decarboxylase liberates dopamine. Levodopa has no intrinsic dopamine receptor agonist activity of its own, so greater receptor potency cannot be the reason.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-6`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sultamicillin, in which ampicillin is joined to sulbactam through a methylene dioxide link, is an example of a:`,options:[`Bioprecursor prodrug`,`Soft drug`,`Mutual prodrug`,`Hard drug`],answer:2,explanation:`A mutual prodrug (codrug) joins two pharmacologically active molecules so that each acts as the carrier of the other, which is exactly the ampicillin plus sulbactam arrangement. A bioprecursor has no carrier at all and is transformed by oxidation or reduction.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-7`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Fosphenytoin, on hydrolysis by alkaline phosphatase, releases phenytoin together with:`,options:[`Acetic acid`,`Formaldehyde`,`Pivalic acid`,`Acetaldehyde`],answer:1,explanation:`Fosphenytoin is the phosphate ester of a hydroxymethyl derivative, so cleavage of the phosphate leaves an unstable hydroxymethyl phenytoin that collapses to phenytoin plus formaldehyde. Pivalic acid is the promoiety released by pivampicillin and by pivaloyl esters, and it depletes carnitine.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-8`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Terfenadine was withdrawn and replaced by fexofenadine because:`,options:[`Fexofenadine is the prodrug and is better absorbed`,`The active metabolite fexofenadine caused sedation`,`Terfenadine was not converted at all in poor metabolisers of CYP2D6`,`The parent terfenadine blocked hERG potassium channels when its CYP3A4 metabolism was inhibited`],answer:3,explanation:`Terfenadine is a true prodrug of fexofenadine, and it is the unconverted parent that blocks hERG and causes torsade de pointes when CYP3A4 is inhibited by ketoconazole or erythromycin. Fexofenadine itself is the safe carboxylic acid metabolite and is non-sedating, so blaming the metabolite for sedation reverses the truth.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-1`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The commonest Phase II conjugation reaction and its cofactor are:`,options:[`Sulfation, with PAPS`,`Acetylation, with acetyl coenzyme A`,`Glucuronidation, with UDP-glucuronic acid`,`Methylation, with S-adenosyl methionine`],answer:2,explanation:`Glucuronidation is the most common conjugation pathway, uses UDP-glucuronic acid as the donor and is catalysed by microsomal UDP-glucuronosyltransferases. Sulfation is the next most important but has a limited pool of PAPS and saturates readily, as it does with paracetamol.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-2`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which group of benzodiazepines undergoes glucuronidation directly without any Phase I step?`,options:[`Oxazepam, lorazepam, temazepam`,`Diazepam, chlordiazepoxide, clorazepate`,`Alprazolam, triazolam, midazolam`,`Nitrazepam, clonazepam, flurazepam`],answer:0,explanation:`Lorazepam, oxazepam and temazepam already carry a 3-hydroxyl group, so they bypass oxidation and go straight to conjugation, making them safer in liver disease and in the elderly. Diazepam and clorazepate instead form the long-lived Phase I metabolite nordiazepam.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-3`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which enzyme converts paracetamol to the hepatotoxic metabolite NAPQI?`,options:[`CYP2D6`,`CYP2C9`,`UGT1A1`,`CYP2E1`],answer:3,explanation:`CYP2E1, with lesser contributions from CYP3A4 and CYP1A2, oxidises paracetamol to N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine, which is normally conjugated with glutathione. UGT1A1 catalyses the safe glucuronidation route, not the toxic one, and CYP2E1 is induced by chronic ethanol, which is why alcoholics are at greater risk.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-4`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient who is a poor metaboliser at CYP2C19 is likely to get an inadequate response to:`,options:[`Codeine`,`Clopidogrel`,`Isoniazid`,`Suxamethonium`],answer:1,explanation:`Clopidogrel is a prodrug that requires CYP2C19 for activation, so poor metabolisers form less active thiol metabolite and remain at risk of stent thrombosis. Codeine depends on CYP2D6, isoniazid on NAT2 acetylation and suxamethonium on plasma cholinesterase.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-5`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In the cytochrome P450 mono-oxygenase reaction, the fate of the two oxygen atoms of molecular oxygen is that:`,options:[`One enters the substrate and one becomes water`,`Both are incorporated into the substrate`,`Both are released as superoxide`,`Both become water and the substrate is oxidised by NADPH directly`],answer:0,explanation:`The stoichiometry RH + O2 + NADPH + H+ gives ROH + H2O + NADP+, so exactly one atom is inserted into the substrate and the other is reduced to water. Enzymes that put both atoms into the substrate are dioxygenases, which is why P450s are specifically called mixed-function oxidases or mono-oxygenases.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-6`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a potent enzyme inducer rather than an inhibitor?`,options:[`Ketoconazole`,`Cimetidine`,`Rifampicin`,`Erythromycin`],answer:2,explanation:`Rifampicin is the classic pregnane X receptor mediated inducer of CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and P-glycoprotein, and it takes days to develop because new protein must be synthesised. Ketoconazole, cimetidine and erythromycin all inhibit, and inhibition is immediate.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-7`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug has an intrinsic clearance of 1.5 litres per minute and hepatic blood flow is 1.5 litres per minute. Assuming complete absorption and no gut metabolism, the oral bioavailability is approximately:`,options:[`25 percent`,`50 percent`,`75 percent`,`100 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`E(hepatic) = CL(int) / (Q + CL(int)) = 1.5 / (1.5 + 1.5) = 0.5, so F = 1 - 0.5 = 0.5, that is 50 percent. Choosing 100 percent comes from assuming that complete absorption alone guarantees full bioavailability, which ignores first-pass extraction.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-8`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`All four drugs below are given intravenously, so first-pass metabolism plays no part. Enzyme induction by rifampicin will most markedly reduce the steady-state plasma concentration of which one?`,options:[`Propranolol, a high extraction ratio drug`,`Lignocaine, a high extraction ratio drug`,`Glyceryl trinitrate, cleared mainly by nitrate reductase`,`Warfarin, a low extraction ratio drug`],answer:3,explanation:`Systemic clearance of a low extraction ratio drug is capacity limited, so it is directly proportional to intrinsic clearance and rises steeply with induction. High extraction drugs such as propranolol and glyceryl trinitrate are flow limited, so induction changes their systemic clearance little, although it does further reduce their already low oral bioavailability.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Phenytoin is chemically:`,options:[`2-Methyl-2-propylsuccinimide`,`5-Ethyl-5-phenylbarbituric acid`,`5,5-Diphenylhydantoin`,`5-Phenyl-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one`],answer:2,explanation:`Phenytoin is 5,5-diphenylhydantoin, that is, imidazolidine-2,4-dione bearing two phenyl rings at C5. 5-Ethyl-5-phenylbarbituric acid is phenobarbitone, which shares the aryl-at-C5 idea but places it on a barbiturate ring, so it has hypnotic as well as anticonvulsant action.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Benzodiazepines and barbiturates both act at the GABA-A receptor. Benzodiazepines increase:`,options:[`The frequency of chloride channel opening`,`The duration of chloride channel opening`,`The conductance of each open channel`,`The synthesis of GABA in the nerve terminal`],answer:0,explanation:`Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of channel opening and require GABA to be present, which gives them a ceiling effect. Barbiturates increase the duration of opening and at high concentration open the channel directly without GABA, which is why they are far more lethal in overdose.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Promethazine is an antihistamine rather than an antipsychotic because its phenothiazine side chain has:`,options:[`A piperazine ring instead of a dimethylamino group`,`A sulfur atom replaced by an ethylene bridge`,`An electron-withdrawing substituent at position 2`,`Only two carbon atoms, and is branched, between the ring nitrogen and the basic nitrogen`],answer:3,explanation:`Three unbranched carbon atoms between N10 and the basic side chain nitrogen are required for antipsychotic activity; promethazine has a branched two-carbon chain, so it behaves as an antihistamine and antiemetic. A 2-position electron-withdrawing group raises antipsychotic potency but cannot create it on a two-carbon chain.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Replacing the C2 oxygen of a barbiturate with sulfur gives thiopental, which is ultra short acting because the change:`,options:[`Makes the molecule far more water soluble, so it is excreted rapidly`,`Makes the molecule far more lipophilic, so it redistributes rapidly out of the brain`,`Prevents binding to the GABA-A receptor after a few minutes`,`Converts the drug into a substrate for plasma cholinesterase`],answer:1,explanation:`Thio substitution greatly increases lipid solubility, so thiopental enters the brain within seconds and then redistributes to muscle and fat, ending the effect. Its elimination half-life is actually several hours, so rapid excretion is not the explanation.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which substituent pattern at position 7 of the benzodiazepine nucleus gives the highest potency?`,options:[`Nitro`,`Methyl`,`Methoxy`,`Hydrogen`],answer:0,explanation:`Position 7 requires an electron-withdrawing group, with the approximate order NO2 greater than Br greater than CF3 greater than Cl greater than H. Electron-donating groups such as methoxy and methyl abolish activity, which is exactly why they are offered as distractors.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Nortriptyline differs from amitriptyline in that nortriptyline:`,options:[`Has a dibenzazepine rather than a dibenzocycloheptadiene ring`,`Is a tertiary amine and is relatively serotonin selective`,`Is a secondary amine and is relatively noradrenaline selective`,`Has a chlorine substituent on the aromatic ring`],answer:2,explanation:`Nortriptyline is the N-demethylated secondary amine metabolite of amitriptyline and is relatively selective for noradrenaline reuptake. Both share the dibenzocycloheptadiene ring; the chlorinated dibenzazepine is clomipramine, the most serotonin-selective tricyclic.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Clorazepate is described as a prodrug because it:`,options:[`Is hydrolysed by plasma esterases to diazepam`,`Decarboxylates in gastric acid to nordiazepam`,`Is reduced by gut bacteria to oxazepam`,`Is hydroxylated by CYP3A4 to temazepam`],answer:1,explanation:`Clorazepate carries a 3-carboxyl group that is lost by decarboxylation in the acid of the stomach, releasing nordiazepam (desmethyldiazepam), whose half-life of 40 to 100 hours accounts for accumulation. It contains no ester, so esterase hydrolysis is not the route.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Carbamazepine (an iminostilbene) and imipramine (a dibenzazepine) are closely related tricyclics, yet carbamazepine is an antiepileptic and imipramine an antidepressant. The structural reason is that carbamazepine carries:`,options:[`A seven-membered central ring instead of a six-membered one`,`A saturated central ring that makes the molecule planar`,`A chlorine on one aromatic ring`,`A carbamoyl group on the ring nitrogen instead of a basic aminoalkyl side chain`],answer:3,explanation:`Both have the same angular seven-membered central ring; the difference is that carbamazepine bears a neutral carbamoyl (urea-like) group on N5 whereas imipramine bears a basic dimethylaminopropyl chain needed for monoamine transporter binding. Oxcarbazepine is the 10-keto analogue that avoids the reactive 10,11-epoxide metabolite.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Increasing the size of the substituent on the nitrogen of a catecholamine from hydrogen to tert-butyl shifts activity towards:`,options:[`Alpha 1 receptors`,`Alpha 2 receptors`,`Beta 2 receptors`,`Dopamine D1 receptors`],answer:2,explanation:`The order is hydrogen giving alpha activity (noradrenaline), methyl giving mixed activity (adrenaline), isopropyl giving beta activity (isoprenaline) and tert-butyl giving beta 2 selectivity (salbutamol, terbutaline). Larger N-substituents progressively lose alpha activity, so a shift towards alpha 1 is the reverse of what actually happens.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Atropine is chemically an ester of:`,options:[`Tropine and tropic acid`,`Tropine and benzoic acid`,`Ecgonine and benzoic acid`,`Choline and tropic acid`],answer:0,explanation:`Atropine is tropine esterified with tropic acid, and the hydroxymethyl group of tropic acid provides the hydrogen bond that is important for muscarinic blockade. Ecgonine plus benzoic acid plus methanol gives cocaine, and tropine plus benzoic acid gives tropacocaine.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Terbutaline resists metabolism by catechol-O-methyltransferase because it has:`,options:[`A 3,4-dihydroxy (catechol) ring`,`No ring hydroxyl groups at all`,`A 3-hydroxymethyl-4-hydroxy (saligenin) ring`,`A 3,5-dihydroxy (resorcinol) ring`],answer:3,explanation:`Terbutaline carries a resorcinol ring, which COMT cannot methylate because the enzyme requires the adjacent 3,4-dihydroxy arrangement. Salbutamol solves the same problem differently, with the saligenin (hydroxymethyl) modification, so the saligenin answer would be correct only for salbutamol.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Easson-Stedman hypothesis is supported by the observation that the less active (S) enantiomer of adrenaline is:`,options:[`Completely devoid of any activity`,`Approximately equipotent with the analogue lacking the beta hydroxyl`,`More potent than adrenaline at beta 2 receptors`,`Rapidly converted in vivo to the R isomer`],answer:1,explanation:`If the S isomer merely presents the beta hydroxyl in the wrong direction, it should behave exactly like a molecule with no beta hydroxyl at all, and that is what is measured, which establishes three-point attachment. The S isomer is weak but not completely devoid of activity, so total loss of activity is not what is observed.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The general structure Ar-O-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-NH-CH(CH3)2 describes:`,options:[`An aryloxypropanolamine beta blocker`,`An arylethanolamine beta agonist`,`A phenylalkylamine calcium channel blocker`,`An aminocyclitol`],answer:0,explanation:`Inserting an OCH2 group between the aryl ring and the ethanolamine chain converts the older arylethanolamine antagonists such as dichloroisoprenaline into the far more potent aryloxypropanolamines, which is the modern beta blocker template. The bulky secondary amine substituent is what makes it an antagonist rather than an agonist.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Beta 1 selectivity in metoprolol and atenolol is conferred by:`,options:[`A tert-butyl group on the nitrogen`,`A fused bicyclic naphthalene ring`,`A para substituent on the aryl ring`,`The R configuration at the carbinol carbon`],answer:2,explanation:`A para substituent on the aromatic ring, the methoxyethyl of metoprolol or the carbamoylmethyl of atenolol, gives cardioselectivity. A fused bicyclic ring, as in propranolol, gives non-selective blockade, and the active configuration of these drugs is S, not R.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Pralidoxime is ineffective in organophosphate poisoning once ageing has occurred because ageing involves:`,options:[`Hydrolysis of the carbamoyl-enzyme bond`,`Loss of one alkoxy group from the phosphorylated enzyme, making the bond essentially permanent`,`Oxidation of the oxime group of pralidoxime`,`Reactivation of butyrylcholinesterase in preference to acetylcholinesterase`],answer:1,explanation:`Ageing is dealkylation of the phosphorylated serine, which leaves a negatively charged, extremely stable phosphoryl-enzyme that the oxime nucleophile can no longer attack. Carbamoylated enzyme, formed by neostigmine, hydrolyses spontaneously and needs no reactivator at all.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Atracurium is unusual among neuromuscular blockers because it is inactivated mainly by:`,options:[`Plasma cholinesterase hydrolysis`,`Renal excretion unchanged`,`Hepatic CYP3A4 oxidation`,`Spontaneous Hofmann elimination at body pH and temperature`],answer:3,explanation:`Atracurium undergoes non-enzymatic Hofmann elimination, plus some ester hydrolysis, which makes it independent of renal and hepatic function; the breakdown product laudanosine is a potential convulsant at very high cumulative doses. Suxamethonium, not atracurium, is the drug hydrolysed by plasma cholinesterase.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The zinc-binding group of captopril is a:`,options:[`Carboxylate`,`Phosphinate`,`Sulfhydryl group`,`Tetrazole`],answer:2,explanation:`Captopril uses a thiol to chelate the active-site zinc of angiotensin converting enzyme, and that thiol also causes its characteristic rash and taste disturbance. Most other ACE inhibitors use a carboxylate, fosinopril uses a phosphinate, and a tetrazole belongs to the sartans, not the prils.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Nifedipine tablets are packed in amber containers because the dihydropyridine ring is:`,options:[`Oxidised by light to the inactive pyridine`,`Hydrolysed by moisture to the free acid`,`Reduced by light to the piperidine`,`Racemised by light at C4`],answer:0,explanation:`Light oxidises the 1,4-dihydropyridine to the fully aromatic pyridine, which has no calcium channel blocking activity, so protection from light is essential. Nifedipine has identical esters at C3 and C5 and is therefore achiral, so racemisation is not possible.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Digoxin differs chemically from digitoxin by an additional hydroxyl group at:`,options:[`C3`,`C17`,`C14`,`C12`],answer:3,explanation:`Digoxin carries an extra 12-beta hydroxyl, which makes it more polar, so it is excreted largely unchanged by the kidney with a half-life of about 36 to 40 hours. The C3 and C14 hydroxyls are present in both, and C17 carries the unsaturated lactone in both.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which class of antiarrhythmic drugs in the Vaughan Williams scheme shortens the action potential duration?`,options:[`Class IA`,`Class IB`,`Class IC`,`Class III`],answer:1,explanation:`Class IB agents such as lignocaine and mexiletine block sodium channels weakly with a fast off-rate and shorten action potential duration. Class IA prolongs it because of added potassium blockade, and class III prolongs it markedly, which is why those two carry a torsade risk that class IB does not.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Angiotensin receptor blockers do not cause the dry cough seen with ACE inhibitors because they:`,options:[`Do not inhibit the breakdown of bradykinin`,`Are given as ester prodrugs`,`Block the AT2 receptor rather than AT1`,`Are not absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract`],answer:0,explanation:`ACE also degrades bradykinin and substance P, so inhibiting it raises those peptides and provokes cough and rarely angioedema. Sartans act downstream at the AT1 receptor and leave bradykinin metabolism untouched; they block AT1 and not AT2, so a switch of receptor subtype is not the explanation.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The pharmacophore common to all statins is a chain that mimics:`,options:[`The adenine portion of coenzyme A`,`The isoprene unit of squalene`,`The mevaldyl-CoA transition state of HMG-CoA reductase`,`The steroid nucleus of cholesterol`],answer:2,explanation:`The 3,5-dihydroxyheptanoic acid chain, in the 3R,5S configuration, is a transition-state analogue of the mevaldyl-CoA intermediate, which is why statins bind HMG-CoA reductase with nanomolar affinity. They do not resemble the steroid nucleus at all, since they act far upstream of ring formation.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Losartan is partly a prodrug because CYP2C9 and CYP3A4 oxidise it to EXP3174, which is:`,options:[`A tetrazole with weaker AT1 affinity`,`A carboxylic acid metabolite 10 to 40 times more potent and insurmountable at AT1`,`An inactive glucuronide conjugate`,`A competitive AT2 antagonist`],answer:1,explanation:`The hydroxymethyl group of losartan is oxidised to a carboxylic acid, giving EXP3174, which binds AT1 far more tightly and behaves as an insurmountable (non-competitive) antagonist, accounting for most of the clinical effect. Losartan retains its tetrazole throughout, so an answer describing a weaker tetrazole confuses the two acidic groups.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Amlodipine has a half-life of 35 to 50 hours, far longer than nifedipine, mainly because amlodipine:`,options:[`Is administered as an ester prodrug`,`Lacks the ester groups at C3 and C5`,`Has a para-substituted aryl ring at C4`,`Carries a basic 2-aminoethoxymethyl side chain and is ionised at physiological pH`],answer:3,explanation:`The basic side chain leaves amlodipine largely protonated at physiological pH, so it associates and dissociates slowly with the membrane and the channel, giving a very long duration and a gradual onset without reflex tachycardia. A para-substituted C4 aryl ring would in fact reduce activity, since ortho or meta substitution is required.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-1`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The bicyclic nucleus of the penicillins is:`,options:[`7-Aminocephalosporanic acid`,`4-Oxo-quinoline-3-carboxylic acid`,`6-Aminopenicillanic acid`,`para-Aminobenzenesulfonamide`],answer:2,explanation:`6-Aminopenicillanic acid is the beta-lactam fused to a five-membered thiazolidine ring, and every penicillin is an acyl derivative of its 6-amino group. 7-Aminocephalosporanic acid is the corresponding cephalosporin nucleus, in which the second ring is a six-membered dihydrothiazine.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-2`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following antibiotics is bactericidal despite acting on the 30S ribosomal subunit?`,options:[`Gentamicin`,`Tetracycline`,`Erythromycin`,`Chloramphenicol`],answer:0,explanation:`Aminoglycosides bind the 30S subunit at the 16S rRNA A site, cause misreading and are bactericidal, which is the classic exception to the rule that protein synthesis inhibitors are bacteriostatic. Tetracycline also binds the 30S subunit but is bacteriostatic, and erythromycin and chloramphenicol act on the 50S subunit.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-3`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Amoxicillin differs from ampicillin only by the addition of a:`,options:[`Ureido group on the side-chain amine`,`Alpha-carboxyl group on the side chain`,`Methoxy group at position 6 alpha`,`Para-hydroxyl group on the phenyl ring`],answer:3,explanation:`The para-hydroxyl raises oral bioavailability from about 40 percent for ampicillin to about 75 to 90 percent for amoxicillin; the spectrum is essentially the same. The alpha-carboxyl is carbenicillin, the ureido group belongs to piperacillin and the 6-alpha-methoxy is the cephamycin feature.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-4`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Moving from the first to the third generation of cephalosporins, the general trend is:`,options:[`Increasing Gram-positive cover and decreasing Gram-negative cover`,`Decreasing Gram-positive cover, increasing Gram-negative cover and increasing CSF penetration`,`Unchanged spectrum with increasing oral bioavailability only`,`Increasing activity against MRSA at every step`],answer:1,explanation:`Across generations one to three the Gram-positive activity falls, the Gram-negative activity and beta-lactamase stability rise, and third generation agents such as ceftriaxone and cefotaxime cross into the cerebrospinal fluid. MRSA cover appears only with the fifth generation ceftaroline and ceftobiprole, which bind PBP2a.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-5`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Imipenem is combined with cilastatin because cilastatin:`,options:[`Inhibits renal dehydropeptidase-I and prevents imipenem hydrolysis`,`Inhibits bacterial beta-lactamase`,`Increases the oral absorption of imipenem`,`Blocks the efflux pump that removes imipenem from Gram-negative bacteria`],answer:0,explanation:`Cilastatin has no antibacterial action of its own and simply inhibits the renal brush border dehydropeptidase-I that hydrolyses imipenem, raising urinary levels and preventing a nephrotoxic metabolite. Meropenem needs no cilastatin because its 1-beta-methyl group blocks the same enzyme sterically.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-6`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Tetracyclines should not be taken with milk or antacids because they:`,options:[`Are hydrolysed by the alkaline pH of the stomach contents`,`Are epimerised at C4 by calcium ions`,`Form non-absorbable chelates with divalent and trivalent cations`,`Are adsorbed onto casein micelles and destroyed`],answer:2,explanation:`The C11 and C12 keto-enol system and the C1 to C3 system chelate calcium, magnesium, aluminium and iron, and the chelate is not absorbed, so bioavailability falls sharply. Chemical destruction of tetracyclines is a different phenomenon, occurring by C4 epimerisation in mild acid and C6 dehydration in strong acid.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-7`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which position of the fluoroquinolone nucleus must remain unsubstituted for activity?`,options:[`C5`,`C2`,`C7`,`C8`],answer:1,explanation:`Any substituent at C2 sits directly against the enzyme-DNA complex and abolishes binding, so C2 must stay as hydrogen. C5 substitution raises Gram-positive activity, C7 substitution controls Gram-negative and antipseudomonal cover, and C8 substitution controls anaerobic cover and phototoxicity.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-8`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sulfonamides are selectively toxic to bacteria because human cells:`,options:[`Have a dihydrofolate reductase that sulfonamides cannot reach`,`Convert sulfonamides to inactive acetyl derivatives before they act`,`Lack the transporter that carries sulfonamides into the cell`,`Do not synthesise folate from PABA and instead absorb preformed folate`],answer:3,explanation:`Bacteria must build folate from PABA and pteridine using dihydropteroate synthase, an enzyme humans simply do not possess because we take folate from the diet, so the drug target is absent from the host. Human acetylation of sulfonamides does occur, but it is a metabolic route, not the basis of selective toxicity, and it is what causes crystalluria.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-1`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The selectivity of acyclovir for herpes-infected cells depends on the fact that the first phosphorylation is carried out by:`,options:[`Host cell adenylate kinase`,`Viral DNA polymerase`,`Viral thymidine kinase`,`Host nucleoside diphosphate kinase`],answer:2,explanation:`Only infected cells contain viral thymidine kinase, so acyclovir monophosphate is formed almost exclusively inside them; host kinases then add the remaining two phosphates. Viral DNA polymerase is the ultimate target of the triphosphate, not the enzyme that activates the drug.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-2`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Azole antifungals act by inhibiting:`,options:[`Lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase`,`1,3-Beta-D-glucan synthase`,`Squalene epoxidase`,`Fungal thymidylate synthase`],answer:0,explanation:`The free nitrogen of the azole ring binds the haem iron of lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), so ergosterol synthesis stops and 14-methyl sterols accumulate. Squalene epoxidase is the terbinafine target and glucan synthase is the echinocandin target.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-3`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Zidovudine terminates the growing viral DNA chain because at the 3 prime position it carries:`,options:[`A hydroxyl group`,`A phosphate group`,`A fluorine atom`,`An azido group`],answer:3,explanation:`The 3 prime azido group replaces the 3 prime hydroxyl of thymidine, so no phosphodiester bond can be formed with the next nucleotide and the chain stops. A retained 3 prime hydroxyl would allow elongation to continue, which is why retention of the 3 prime hydroxyl is the direct opposite of the mechanism.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-4`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement about non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors is correct?`,options:[`They must be triphosphorylated before they act`,`They bind an allosteric hydrophobic pocket and are active against HIV-1 but not HIV-2`,`They are competitive inhibitors at the nucleotide binding site`,`They inhibit the viral protease as well as reverse transcriptase`],answer:1,explanation:`NNRTIs such as nevirapine and efavirenz need no phosphorylation, bind a hydrophobic pocket about 10 angstroms from the catalytic site, act non-competitively and have no activity against HIV-2 because that pocket differs. Requiring phosphorylation is the defining feature of the nucleoside analogues, not of this class.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-5`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Oseltamivir is given orally whereas zanamivir must be inhaled because oseltamivir:`,options:[`Replaces the polar glycerol side chain with a lipophilic pentyloxy group and is given as an ethyl ester`,`Is a smaller molecule with fewer rotatable bonds`,`Is a nucleoside analogue and uses the nucleoside transporter`,`Is not a neuraminidase inhibitor at all`],answer:0,explanation:`Zanamivir retains the polar glycerol side chain of the sialic acid transition state and is too hydrophilic to be absorbed, whereas oseltamivir substitutes a lipophilic 3-pentyloxy group and is administered as the ethyl ester prodrug, hydrolysed by hepatic carboxylesterase to the active carboxylate. Both are neuraminidase inhibitors active against influenza A and B.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-6`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Amphotericin B injection must be reconstituted in dextrose and not in saline because:`,options:[`Sodium chloride oxidises the polyene chain`,`Saline raises the pH above the stability limit`,`Electrolytes precipitate the colloidal micellar dispersion`,`Chloride ions displace the mycosamine sugar`],answer:2,explanation:`Conventional amphotericin B deoxycholate is a micellar colloidal dispersion, not a true solution, and electrolytes cause aggregation and precipitation, so 5 percent dextrose is used. The polyene chain is light sensitive but is not oxidised by chloride.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-7`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Terbinafine is fungicidal whereas azoles are largely fungistatic because inhibition of squalene epoxidase:`,options:[`Blocks ergosterol synthesis more completely than CYP51 inhibition`,`Causes toxic accumulation of squalene inside the fungal cell`,`Destroys the fungal cell wall glucan directly`,`Prevents uptake of nutrients through cytosine permease`],answer:1,explanation:`Blocking squalene epoxidase does two things: it deprives the cell of ergosterol and it causes squalene itself to accumulate to toxic intracellular concentrations, and it is that accumulation which kills the cell. Azoles act further down the pathway, so only the sterol depletion occurs and the effect is mostly fungistatic.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-8`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Flucytosine is selectively toxic to fungi because human cells lack:`,options:[`Dihydrofolate reductase`,`Thymidylate synthase`,`Uridine kinase`,`Cytosine deaminase`],answer:3,explanation:`Fungal cytosine deaminase converts flucytosine to 5-fluorouracil inside the fungal cell, and human cells have no such enzyme, which is the basis of the selectivity. Human cells do possess thymidylate synthase and dihydrofolate reductase, and gut bacteria do possess cytosine deaminase, which is exactly why marrow toxicity still occurs.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-1`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Mesna is given with cyclophosphamide to prevent haemorrhagic cystitis caused by:`,options:[`Phosphoramide mustard`,`4-Hydroxycyclophosphamide`,`Acrolein`,`Chloroacetaldehyde`],answer:2,explanation:`Fragmentation of aldophosphamide gives phosphoramide mustard, the alkylating species, plus acrolein, which is an unsaturated aldehyde that damages the bladder urothelium; mesna supplies a thiol that adds across it. Phosphoramide mustard is the therapeutically useful product, not the bladder toxin.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-2`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which anticancer drug is characteristically associated with pulmonary fibrosis and minimal myelosuppression?`,options:[`Bleomycin`,`Doxorubicin`,`Vincristine`,`Cisplatin`],answer:0,explanation:`Bleomycin is inactivated by a hydrolase that is scarce in lung and skin, so its toxicity concentrates there as pulmonary fibrosis, and it spares the marrow. Doxorubicin causes cardiomyopathy, vincristine causes neuropathy and cisplatin causes nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-3`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The reactive intermediate through which nitrogen mustards alkylate DNA is the:`,options:[`Carbocation of the chloroethyl group`,`Nitrosourea isocyanate`,`Epoxide of the side chain`,`Aziridinium (ethyleneimonium) ion`],answer:3,explanation:`The nitrogen lone pair attacks its own beta carbon and expels chloride, giving a strained three-membered aziridinium ring that is opened by the N7 of guanine. Aromatic mustards such as chlorambucil react more slowly precisely because the aryl ring delocalises that lone pair and slows aziridinium formation.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-4`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Folinic acid rescue works after high-dose methotrexate because folinic acid:`,options:[`Displaces methotrexate from dihydrofolate reductase`,`Is already reduced and therefore bypasses the blocked dihydrofolate reductase step`,`Increases the renal excretion of methotrexate`,`Inhibits the polyglutamation of methotrexate inside the cell`],answer:1,explanation:`Folinic acid is N5-formyl tetrahydrofolate, an already reduced folate, so it supplies the one-carbon pool without needing the enzyme that methotrexate has blocked. Folic acid itself is useless for rescue because it still has to pass through dihydrofolate reductase.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-5`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Vinca alkaloids and taxanes both arrest mitosis, but their actions on tubulin are opposite in that:`,options:[`Vincristine prevents polymerisation while paclitaxel stabilises the polymer and prevents disassembly`,`Vincristine stabilises microtubules while paclitaxel prevents their assembly`,`Both prevent polymerisation but at different sites`,`Both stabilise the polymer but in different cell cycle phases`],answer:0,explanation:`Vinca alkaloids bind the vinca domain of beta-tubulin and block polymerisation, so the spindle falls apart; paclitaxel binds the taxane site and hyperstabilises the microtubule so it cannot be dismantled. The spindle must both assemble and disassemble, so blocking either half arrests the cell.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-6`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The dose of 6-mercaptopurine must be reduced to about one quarter when it is given with allopurinol because allopurinol inhibits:`,options:[`Thiopurine methyltransferase`,`Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase`,`Xanthine oxidase`,`Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase`],answer:2,explanation:`6-Mercaptopurine is inactivated by xanthine oxidase, and allopurinol blocks that enzyme, so unchanged drug accumulates and causes severe myelosuppression. TPMT deficiency causes the same clinical problem but by a genetic route rather than a drug interaction, and azathioprine, which releases 6-mercaptopurine, carries the same warning.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-7`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Imatinib is described as a type II kinase inhibitor because it binds Bcr-Abl:`,options:[`Covalently at a cysteine residue in the ATP pocket`,`In the ATP pocket, but only when the kinase is in its inactive DFG-out conformation`,`At an allosteric site remote from the ATP pocket`,`At the substrate binding site rather than the ATP site`],answer:1,explanation:`Imatinib, a 2-phenylaminopyrimidine, occupies the ATP pocket and an adjacent hydrophobic pocket that is exposed only in the inactive DFG-out conformation, and this conformational requirement is what gives it selectivity. The T315I gatekeeper mutation removes a key hydrogen bond and confers resistance to imatinib, dasatinib and nilotinib alike.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-8`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Aromatase inhibitors such as anastrozole are used only in postmenopausal women because in premenopausal women:`,options:[`The ovary makes oestrogen by a route that does not involve aromatase`,`Aromatase is absent from peripheral adipose tissue`,`Hepatic metabolism of the drug is much faster`,`Loss of oestrogen feedback raises gonadotrophin and drives ovarian oestrogen production`],answer:3,explanation:`After the menopause, peripheral aromatase in fat and muscle is the only source of oestrogen, so inhibiting it works. In a premenopausal woman the falling oestrogen removes negative feedback, gonadotrophin rises and the ovary simply overrides the block, so tamoxifen or ovarian suppression is used instead.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-1`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The steroid nucleus is chemically named:`,options:[`Cyclopentanophenanthrone`,`Octahydronaphthacene carboxamide`,`Cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene`,`Perhydroanthracene`],answer:2,explanation:`The steroid skeleton is three fused six-membered rings (A, B and C) plus a five-membered ring D, that is, cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene. Octahydronaphthacene carboxamide is the tetracycline nucleus, offered here because it is the other famous four-ring system in medicinal chemistry.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-2`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Aspirin differs from all other NSAIDs in that it inhibits cyclo-oxygenase:`,options:[`Irreversibly, by acetylating a serine residue`,`Competitively and reversibly`,`Non-competitively at an allosteric site`,`Only in the presence of high peroxide concentrations`],answer:0,explanation:`Aspirin acetylates serine 530 of COX-1 and serine 516 of COX-2, so the enzyme is permanently inactivated and the anucleate platelet cannot replace it for its 7 to 10 day lifespan. Every other NSAID inhibits reversibly and competitively, which is why their antiplatelet effect wears off with the drug.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-3`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The structural basis of COX-2 selectivity in the coxibs is that COX-2 has, at position 523:`,options:[`Isoleucine, which opens a hydrophobic side pocket`,`Tyrosine, which is absent in COX-1`,`Arginine, which forms an ionic bond with the sulfonamide`,`Valine, which is smaller than the isoleucine of COX-1 and opens a side pocket`],answer:3,explanation:`COX-2 carries valine at 523 where COX-1 carries isoleucine; the missing methyl group opens a side pocket that accommodates the bulky phenylsulfonamide or methylsulfone of a coxib, and that group simply will not fit COX-1. Arginine 120 is the residue that anchors the acidic group of a classical NSAID and is present in both isoforms.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-4`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Introducing a 16-alpha-methyl group into a 9-alpha-fluoro corticosteroid, as in dexamethasone, mainly:`,options:[`Increases the mineralocorticoid activity`,`Abolishes the mineralocorticoid activity while retaining high glucocorticoid potency`,`Abolishes the glucocorticoid activity`,`Converts the steroid into a progestogen`],answer:1,explanation:`The 9-alpha-fluoro raises both glucocorticoid and salt-retaining activity, and the 16-substituent then removes the mineralocorticoid component, leaving dexamethasone about 25 to 30 times as potent as cortisol with essentially no sodium retention. Without the 16-substituent the same 9-alpha-fluoro compound is fludrocortisone, which is used as a mineralocorticoid.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-5`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide require:`,options:[`An unsubstituted sulfonamide group attached to an aromatic or heteroaromatic ring`,`An N-substituted sulfonamide on a benzene ring`,`A carboxylic acid ortho to a halogen`,`A benzothiadiazine ring with a chlorine at C6`],answer:0,explanation:`The SO2NH2 group must be unsubstituted, because the ionised nitrogen coordinates the active-site zinc; any substitution on that nitrogen abolishes activity. A benzothiadiazine with a C6 chlorine describes the thiazides, which retain weak carbonic anhydrase activity but act mainly on the distal tubule sodium-chloride cotransporter.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-6`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which diuretic is free of a sulfonamide group and is therefore preferred in genuine sulfonamide allergy?`,options:[`Furosemide`,`Bumetanide`,`Ethacrynic acid`,`Chlorthalidone`],answer:2,explanation:`Ethacrynic acid is a phenoxyacetic acid carrying an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone that reacts with sulfhydryl groups, and it contains no sulfonamide at all. Furosemide, bumetanide, chlorthalidone, the thiazides and acetazolamide all carry a sulfonamide group.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-7`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Ibuprofen is marketed as the racemate even though only the S enantiomer inhibits COX, because:`,options:[`The R enantiomer is a more potent analgesic by a non-COX mechanism`,`An acyl-CoA dependent racemase converts R to S unidirectionally in vivo`,`The racemate is more soluble than either pure enantiomer`,`The S enantiomer racemises rapidly in the stomach`],answer:1,explanation:`Alpha-methylacyl-CoA racemase carries out unidirectional metabolic inversion of R-ibuprofen to the active S form through a coenzyme A thioester, so much of the inactive isomer is salvaged. The reverse conversion does not occur, so the process is one-way and not simple racemisation.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-8`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Prednisone and cortisone are ineffective when applied topically or given in severe liver disease because they:`,options:[`Are too polar to cross the skin`,`Lack the 4,5 double bond needed for receptor binding`,`Are inactivated by 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 in the skin`,`Are 11-keto prodrugs requiring hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 1 for activation`],answer:3,explanation:`Both carry an 11-keto group and must be reduced by hepatic 11-beta-HSD type 1 to prednisolone and cortisol, which carry the essential 11-beta-hydroxyl, so the liver is indispensable. Prednisolone and hydrocortisone are the active forms used topically for exactly this reason.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-9`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which QSAR parameter measures steric bulk and is defined as exactly zero for hydrogen, so that every larger substituent takes a negative value?`,options:[`sigma`,`pi`,`Es`,`MR`],answer:2,explanation:`The Taft steric parameter Es is referenced to hydrogen, which is assigned zero, so bulkier groups such as tert-butyl take negative values (about -1.54). Molar refractivity also reflects size, but it is a positive measure of volume and polarisability from the Lorentz-Lorenz equation and is not zero for hydrogen.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-10`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the four criteria of the Lipinski rule of five?`,options:[`Molecular weight not more than 500`,`Log P not more than 5`,`Not more than 5 hydrogen bond donors`,`Not more than 10 rotatable bonds`],answer:3,explanation:`Lipinski used molecular weight, log P, hydrogen bond donors and hydrogen bond acceptors only. The limit on rotatable bonds, together with a polar surface area not above 140 square angstroms, is the later Veber addition, which is why it is the tempting wrong answer.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-11`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The log P of benzene is 2.13 and that of chlorobenzene is 2.84. The Hansch lipophilicity constant pi for chlorine is therefore:`,options:[`plus 0.71`,`minus 0.71`,`plus 4.97`,`plus 2.84`],answer:0,explanation:`pi(X) = log P(R-X) - log P(R-H) = 2.84 - 2.13 = plus 0.71, and the positive sign means chlorine is more lipophilic than the hydrogen it replaced. The value 4.97 comes from adding the two log P values instead of subtracting, and 2.84 mistakes the log P of the substituted compound for the substituent constant itself.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-12`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A weak base of pKa 5.4 is present in plasma at pH 7.4. The percentage in the unionised, membrane-permeable form is about:`,options:[`About 1 percent`,`About 99 percent`,`About 50 percent`,`About 9 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`For a base, pH = pKa + log ([unionised] / [ionised]), so 7.4 - 5.4 = 2 gives a ratio of 100 to 1 and an unionised fraction of 100/101, about 99 percent. The 1 percent figure is the ionised fraction and is what you obtain if the log term is inverted, which is the rule for a weak acid, not a weak base.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-13`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Replacement of the C2 oxygen of a barbiturate by sulfur, giving thiopental, is an example of:`,options:[`A monovalent classical bioisostere`,`A non-classical bioisostere`,`A divalent classical bioisostere`,`A ring-chain transformation`],answer:2,explanation:`O, S, NH and CH2 form the classical divalent interchange set, so swapping a ring oxygen for sulfur is divalent classical isosterism, and it raises lipophilicity sharply. Monovalent pairs are single-atom exchanges such as hydrogen for fluorine or hydroxyl for amino, which do not describe a doubly bonded ring atom.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-14`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Before any analogue is synthesised, a chemist plots sigma against pi for candidate substituents and deliberately picks one from each of the four quadrants. This tool is:`,options:[`The Topliss decision tree`,`The Craig plot`,`The Free-Wilson matrix`,`The Fujita-Ban mixed approach`],answer:1,explanation:`The Craig plot is a graphical pre-synthesis selection tool, and taking one substituent from each quadrant stops pi and sigma varying together, which would make the regression collinear and meaningless. The Topliss scheme is applied one compound at a time after each analogue has been tested, so by definition it cannot be used before anything has been made.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-15`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hammett sigma constants are tabulated for meta and para substituents but not for ortho substituents because an ortho group:`,options:[`Cannot be conjugated with the aromatic ring`,`Is always electron donating`,`Alters the reaction constant rho instead`,`Adds a steric contribution that cannot be separated from the electronic one`],answer:3,explanation:`An ortho substituent sits directly beside the reaction centre, so its influence is a mixture of electronic and steric effects and no single sigma value can describe it. Taft separated the two by measuring ester hydrolysis under acidic conditions, where only the steric term operates, to give Es, and under basic conditions to give sigma-star.`},{id:`q-mc-physchem-sar-16`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A weak acid has a log P of 3.0 and a pKa of 4.0. Its log D at pH 7.0 is approximately:`,options:[`0`,`3.0`,`minus 3.0`,`6.0`],answer:0,explanation:`log D = log P - log (1 + 10^(pH - pKa)) = 3.0 - log (1 + 10^3) = 3.0 - 3.0 = 0, so at pH 7 the ionisation cancels the intrinsic greasiness and the drug distributes about equally between octanol and water. Answering 3.0 ignores ionisation altogether, and that is precisely the difference between log P and log D.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-9`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Dipivefrine is used in place of adrenaline in glaucoma eye drops because the dipivalyl ester:`,options:[`Makes the drug water soluble for the eye drop`,`Protects adrenaline from COMT in the systemic circulation`,`Raises corneal penetration about ten-fold and is then cleaved by corneal esterase`,`Is actively transported into the ciliary body`],answer:2,explanation:`Esterifying both catechol hydroxyls with pivalic acid masks the polar groups, so the prodrug crosses the cornea far more easily and corneal esterases liberate adrenaline inside the eye, allowing a much smaller dose. The ester is more lipophilic, not more water soluble, so a solubility explanation reverses the chemistry.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-10`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a bioprecursor prodrug, that is, one with no carrier group at all?`,options:[`Enalapril`,`Bacampicillin`,`Dipivefrine`,`Sulindac`],answer:3,explanation:`Sulindac is a sulfoxide that is reduced in the body to the active sulfide; nothing is split off, the molecule itself is transformed, which is the definition of a bioprecursor. Enalapril, bacampicillin and dipivefrine are all carrier-linked ester prodrugs from which a promoiety is hydrolysed.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-11`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Capecitabine liberates 5-fluorouracil preferentially inside tumour tissue because the last enzyme of its three-step cascade is:`,options:[`Hepatic carboxylesterase`,`Cytidine deaminase`,`Thymidine phosphorylase, which is enriched in tumour tissue`,`Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase`],answer:2,explanation:`The cascade runs carboxylesterase in the liver, then cytidine deaminase, then thymidine phosphorylase, and it is that final enzyme, present at much higher activity in tumour than in normal tissue, that gives the targeting. Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase is the enzyme that destroys 5-fluorouracil, which is why patients deficient in it suffer severe toxicity.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-12`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Valaciclovir gives roughly three to five times the oral bioavailability of aciclovir because the L-valyl ester:`,options:[`Is a substrate for the intestinal peptide transporter PEPT1`,`Is far more lipophilic and diffuses passively across the enterocyte`,`Resists degradation by gastric acid`,`Inhibits intestinal P-glycoprotein`],answer:0,explanation:`Attaching valine makes the molecule resemble a small peptide, so it is actively carried across the enterocyte by PEPT1 and then hydrolysed by valaciclovir hydrolase to aciclovir. The valyl ester is itself polar and ionisable, so increased passive lipophilic diffusion cannot be the explanation.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-13`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Esmolol has a half-life of about 9 minutes because, unlike metoprolol, it carries:`,options:[`A tert-butyl group on the amine nitrogen`,`A phenolic hydroxyl that is rapidly glucuronidated`,`An extra methylene group in the oxypropanolamine chain`,`A methyl ester on the para side chain that red cell esterases hydrolyse`],answer:3,explanation:`Esmolol is a soft analogue: the deliberately labile methyl ester is cleaved by red cell esterases to an inactive acid, so the effect disappears within minutes of stopping the infusion. Changing the amine substituent to tert-butyl would alter receptor selectivity rather than duration, since several long-acting beta blockers carry exactly that group.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-14`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aspirin is an ester of salicylic acid, yet it is not classified as a prodrug of salicylic acid because:`,options:[`The ester bond is never hydrolysed in the body`,`Aspirin itself acetylates cyclo-oxygenase, an action salicylic acid cannot perform`,`Salicylic acid is pharmacologically inactive`,`Aspirin is absorbed only from the colon`],answer:1,explanation:`A prodrug must be essentially inactive as administered, and aspirin is not, because the acetyl group it carries irreversibly acetylates the enzyme and that is its defining pharmacological action. Salicylic acid is itself an active analgesic and anti-inflammatory agent, so describing it as inactive compounds the error.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-15`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Tenofovir alafenamide achieves the same antiviral effect as tenofovir disoproxil at roughly one tenth the dose because its phosphoramidate (ProTide) linkage:`,options:[`Is hydrolysed by gastric acid before absorption`,`Removes the need for any intracellular phosphorylation`,`Is stable in plasma and is cleaved by an intracellular hydrolase inside lymphocytes`,`Is cleaved by intestinal alkaline phosphatase during absorption`],answer:2,explanation:`The disoproxil ester is cleaved by plasma and gut esterases, so free tenofovir circulates and exposes the kidney, whereas the amino acid phosphoramidate survives in plasma and is unmasked only inside the lymphocyte, loading the target cell from a far smaller dose. The released phosphonate still requires two further phosphorylations to the active diphosphate, so an answer claiming no phosphorylation is needed is wrong.`},{id:`q-mc-prodrug-design-16`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Loteprednol etabonate was designed by starting from the inactive 17-carboxylic acid metabolite of prednisolone and building an ester back onto it. This tactic is called:`,options:[`The soft analogue approach`,`The inactive metabolite approach`,`Cascade latentiation`,`Mutual prodrug design`],answer:1,explanation:`The inactive metabolite approach begins from a known inactive metabolite and restores activity with a group whose cleavage regenerates that same harmless metabolite, which is why loteprednol raises intraocular pressure so little. The soft analogue approach instead inserts a labile ester into an existing active drug, which is how esmolol was obtained from a metoprolol-like structure.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-9`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which Phase II conjugation usually gives a metabolite LESS water soluble than the parent drug, and was the cause of crystalluria with the older sulfonamides?`,options:[`Glucuronidation`,`Sulfation`,`Acetylation`,`Glutathione conjugation`],answer:2,explanation:`Acetylation, and also methylation, mask a polar amino or hydroxyl group and so reduce water solubility; the acetylated sulfonamides precipitated in the renal tubule. Glucuronidation and sulfation both attach a strongly ionised group and are the routes that make a drug more soluble.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-10`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which drug-metabolising enzyme sits on the outer mitochondrial membrane rather than in the microsomal fraction?`,options:[`UDP-glucuronosyltransferase`,`Monoamine oxidase`,`CYP3A4`,`Flavin mono-oxygenase`],answer:1,explanation:`Monoamine oxidase is a mitochondrial outer membrane enzyme carrying out oxidative deamination of adrenaline, noradrenaline and tyramine. UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, CYP3A4 and the flavin mono-oxygenases are all microsomal, that is, they belong to the smooth endoplasmic reticulum.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-11`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A nursing mother who is an ultrarapid metaboliser at CYP2D6 is given codeine. The danger to the infant arises because in the mother there is:`,options:[`No analgesia, because codeine is not converted to morphine`,`Accumulation of unchanged codeine, causing seizures`,`Failure of morphine glucuronidation`,`Excessive conversion to morphine, which reaches the milk and can cause respiratory depression`],answer:3,explanation:`Codeine is O-demethylated by CYP2D6 to morphine, so gene duplication carriers form far more morphine than expected and infant respiratory depression has been reported. The poor metaboliser at the same locus has the opposite problem, simple failure of analgesia, which is why that option is the tempting mirror image.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-12`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Broad-spectrum antibiotics can reduce the efficacy of a combined oral contraceptive because they:`,options:[`Induce hepatic CYP3A4`,`Chelate the oestrogen in the gut lumen`,`Kill the gut bacteria whose beta-glucuronidase releases the steroid from its biliary glucuronide for reabsorption`,`Inhibit intestinal sulfotransferase`],answer:2,explanation:`Oestrogen glucuronides are excreted in bile, hydrolysed by bacterial beta-glucuronidase and the freed steroid is reabsorbed, so destroying the flora breaks that enterohepatic loop and lowers plasma levels. Enzyme induction is a genuine but separate mechanism, and it belongs to rifampicin, phenytoin and carbamazepine rather than to antibiotics as a group.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-13`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient stabilised on oral theophylline gives up smoking. The expected change and the isoform responsible are:`,options:[`A fall in theophylline concentration, through CYP2E1`,`A rise in theophylline concentration, through loss of CYP1A2 induction`,`A fall in theophylline concentration, through loss of CYP1A2 induction`,`A rise in theophylline concentration, through CYP3A4 inhibition`],answer:1,explanation:`The polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons of tobacco smoke induce CYP1A2, so a smoker clears theophylline faster; on stopping, the induction wanes over days, clearance falls and the concentration rises towards toxicity. Nicotine itself is not the inducer, so nicotine replacement does not maintain the effect.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-14`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Halothane hepatitis is attributed to a reactive metabolite formed by CYP2E1, namely:`,options:[`Trifluoroacetyl chloride, which haptenises hepatic proteins`,`N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine`,`Acrolein`,`Hydrazine`],answer:0,explanation:`Oxidative metabolism of halothane by CYP2E1 gives trifluoroacetyl chloride, which trifluoroacetylates liver proteins and creates neoantigens, producing an immune-mediated hepatitis on re-exposure. N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine is the paracetamol toxin formed by the same isoform, which is why it is the most tempting distractor.`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-15`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug is completely absorbed and undergoes no gut wall metabolism. Its oral bioavailability is 20 percent and hepatic blood flow is 1.5 litres per minute. Its hepatic clearance is:`,options:[`0.30 litres per minute`,`0.80 litres per minute`,`1.20 litres per minute`,`1.88 litres per minute`],answer:2,explanation:`With complete absorption and no gut loss, F = 1 - E, so E = 1 - 0.2 = 0.8, and CL(hepatic) = Q x E = 1.5 x 0.8 = 1.2 litres per minute. Answering 0.30 comes from multiplying hepatic blood flow by the bioavailability instead of by the extraction ratio.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-mc-metabolism-16`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Grapefruit juice greatly raises the area under the curve of ORAL felodipine but has almost no effect when felodipine is given intravenously. The reason is that grapefruit juice:`,options:[`Induces hepatic CYP3A4`,`Inhibits renal tubular secretion of felodipine`,`Displaces felodipine from plasma albumin`,`Inactivates CYP3A4 in the intestinal enterocytes, cutting gut wall first-pass extraction`],answer:3,explanation:`The furanocoumarins of grapefruit cause mechanism-based inactivation of enterocyte CYP3A4, so the gut wall extraction term of the bioavailability equation collapses and much more drug reaches the circulation; an intravenous dose never passes that barrier, hence the route dependence. Induction would lower, not raise, the concentration, so it is the opposite of what is observed.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Ethosuximide, the drug of choice for absence seizures, is chemically a:`,options:[`Hydantoin`,`Succinimide (pyrrolidine-2,5-dione)`,`Oxazolidinedione`,`Branched-chain carboxylic acid`],answer:1,explanation:`Ethosuximide is a 2-ethyl-2-methylsuccinimide, that is, a pyrrolidine-2,5-dione, and it acts on T-type calcium channels in thalamic neurones. Trimethadione is the oxazolidinedione used historically for the same indication, and valproate is the branched-chain carboxylic acid.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Barbituric acid itself has no depressant activity. Hypnotic activity appears only when:`,options:[`One hydrogen at C5 is replaced by an alkyl group`,`Both hydrogens at C5 are replaced by carbon substituents`,`The C2 oxygen is replaced by sulfur`,`N1 is methylated`],answer:1,explanation:`Both C5 hydrogens must be replaced by carbon substituents, and the total carbon count of the two should be about 4 to 8; a monosubstituted barbiturate is inactive. Thio substitution at C2 and methylation at N1 only shorten the duration of an already active compound, they do not create activity.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-11`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Among the phenothiazines, the substituent at position 2 that gives the highest antipsychotic potency is:`,options:[`Hydrogen, as in promazine`,`Chlorine, as in chlorpromazine`,`A methoxy group`,`Trifluoromethyl, as in fluphenazine`],answer:3,explanation:`Position 2 needs an electron-withdrawing group and the potency order is trifluoromethyl greater than chlorine greater than hydrogen, so fluphenazine and trifluoperazine are the most potent of the series. A methoxy group is electron donating and therefore works in the wrong direction, and chlorine gives only the intermediate potency seen with chlorpromazine.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-12`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Midazolam can be supplied as a clear aqueous injection with no organic co-solvent, unlike diazepam, because:`,options:[`Its fused imidazole ring system opens at the acidic pH of the ampoule to a water-soluble form that closes again at pH 7.4`,`It carries a 3-hydroxyl group that is directly glucuronidated`,`It carries a 7-nitro group, which is strongly hydrophilic`,`It has no 5-aryl ring`],answer:0,explanation:`Below about pH 4 the 4,5 bond opens reversibly to give an ionisable open-ring form that dissolves in water; at blood pH the ring closes and the lipophilic species enters the brain rapidly. A 3-hydroxyl affects metabolism rather than formulation, and a 7-nitro group, which belongs to nitrazepam and clonazepam, confers no water solubility at all.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-13`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antipsychotic is a quinolinone that acts as a partial agonist at D2 receptors rather than as an antagonist?`,options:[`Clozapine`,`Risperidone`,`Aripiprazole`,`Olanzapine`],answer:2,explanation:`Aripiprazole is a dihydroquinolinone and a D2 partial agonist, so it damps down transmission where dopamine is high and supports it where dopamine is low. Clozapine (a dibenzodiazepine) and olanzapine (a thienobenzodiazepine) are antagonists with a high 5-HT2A to D2 ratio, and risperidone is a benzisoxazole antagonist whose 9-hydroxy metabolite is marketed as paliperidone.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-14`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Fluoxetine is far safer than amitriptyline in overdose mainly because fluoxetine:`,options:[`Is poorly absorbed at high doses`,`Has a much shorter half-life`,`Is a weaker inhibitor of serotonin reuptake`,`Has abandoned the tricyclic ring, so it lacks the muscarinic, alpha-1 and histamine H1 blockade of the tricyclics`],answer:3,explanation:`Fluoxetine is an aryloxypropylamine carrying a para-trifluoromethylphenoxy group; dropping the tricycle removes the muscarinic, alpha-1 adrenergic and H1 blockade that produce the anticholinergic delirium, hypotension and cardiac conduction block of tricyclic overdose. Fluoxetine in fact has an unusually long half-life, extended further by its active metabolite norfluoxetine, so a short half-life is the reverse of the truth.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-15`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Alkalinising the urine speeds the elimination of phenobarbitone in overdose because phenobarbitone:`,options:[`Is a weak base whose unionised form is trapped in alkaline urine`,`Is chelated by bicarbonate ions`,`Is a weak acid of pKa about 7.4, so a higher urinary pH increases the ionised fraction that cannot be reabsorbed`,`Is actively secreted by an alkali-dependent tubular transporter`],answer:2,explanation:`In a 5,5-disubstituted barbiturate the acidity comes from the imide NH and the pKa is about 7.4 to 8.0, so raising urinary pH shifts the drug to its ionised form, which cannot diffuse back across the tubular membrane. Calling phenobarbitone a weak base inverts its chemistry: barbiturates form sodium salts for injection precisely because they are acids.`},{id:`q-mc-cns-agents-16`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Flumazenil is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site. Structurally it differs from diazepam in that flumazenil:`,options:[`Is an imidazobenzodiazepine that lacks the 5-aryl ring`,`Carries no electron-withdrawing halogen anywhere on the molecule`,`Has a six-membered central ring`,`Is not a benzodiazepine derivative at all`],answer:0,explanation:`Flumazenil keeps the benzodiazepine framework with a fused imidazole and an ester side chain but has no 5-phenyl ring, and losing that aryl group is what turns an agonist into a pure competitive antagonist. It does carry a ring fluorine, and it is genuinely a benzodiazepine derivative, unlike zolpidem, zaleplon and zopiclone, which occupy the same site from completely different scaffolds.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Hyoscine (scopolamine) differs chemically from atropine by the presence of:`,options:[`A quaternary nitrogen`,`A benzoyl ester in place of the tropoyl ester`,`A 6,7-epoxide bridge`,`An additional hydroxymethyl group on the acid`],answer:2,explanation:`Hyoscine is atropine carrying an epoxide bridge across C6 and C7; the extra lipophilicity lets it enter the brain, so it is sedative and antiemetic where atropine is a central stimulant at high dose. Quaternisation is the change made in ipratropium and methylatropine, and it does the opposite, keeping the drug out of the brain.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Suxamethonium is best described chemically as:`,options:[`A bis-quaternary aminosteroid`,`Two acetylcholine molecules joined tail to tail`,`A benzylisoquinoline dimer`,`A tertiary amine carbamate`],answer:1,explanation:`Suxamethonium (succinylcholine) is two acetylcholine molecules linked through their acetyl ends, which is why it activates the nicotinic receptor before blocking it by persistent depolarisation, and why plasma cholinesterase hydrolyses it. The aminosteroids (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium) and benzylisoquinolines (tubocurarine, atracurium) are the non-depolarising families.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-11`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Removing both ring hydroxyl groups from the catecholamine skeleton, as in amphetamine, gives a compound that:`,options:[`Is a more potent direct agonist at beta 2 receptors`,`Is resistant to MAO but is still a good COMT substrate`,`Cannot be absorbed from the gut`,`Acts mainly indirectly by releasing stored noradrenaline, is orally active and enters the brain`],answer:3,explanation:`Losing the catechol hydroxyls destroys direct receptor potency but also removes the COMT substrate feature and much of the polarity, so the molecule survives oral dosing, crosses the blood-brain barrier and works by displacing stored noradrenaline. Escape from MAO is a separate modification, produced by the alpha-methyl group, which amphetamine also happens to carry.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-12`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Salmeterol acts for about 12 hours whereas salbutamol acts for 4 to 6 hours, mainly because salmeterol:`,options:[`Is given at a much larger dose`,`Carries a long lipophilic N-substituent that anchors it at an exosite of the beta 2 receptor`,`Is a catechol protected from COMT by an ester promoiety`,`Is eliminated unchanged by the kidney`],answer:1,explanation:`The long lipophilic tail on the amine nitrogen binds a hydrophobic exosite adjacent to the receptor, so the active head repeatedly re-engages the binding site instead of diffusing away, and the molecule escapes both COMT and MAO. It is used in microgram doses, so a larger dose is not the explanation, and it is not a catechol at all.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-13`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Bethanechol differs from carbachol by a beta-methyl group on the choline chain, and this group:`,options:[`Gives muscarinic selectivity with negligible nicotinic action`,`Makes the ester susceptible to acetylcholinesterase`,`Converts the molecule into a nicotinic antagonist`,`Removes the quaternary positive charge`],answer:0,explanation:`The beta-methyl group, present in methacholine and bethanechol, selects for muscarinic receptors, so bethanechol stimulates bladder and gut without the ganglionic effects carbachol produces. Resistance to cholinesterase in both carbachol and bethanechol comes from the carbamate ester, not from the beta-methyl group.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-14`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Central antimuscarinic poisoning is treated with physostigmine rather than neostigmine because physostigmine:`,options:[`Is a tertiary amine and therefore reaches the brain`,`Is quaternary and so is confined to the periphery`,`Binds the enzyme only by ionic and hydrogen bonding`,`Is not a carbamate and so acts instantly`],answer:0,explanation:`Physostigmine is a tertiary amine carbamate, uncharged enough to cross the blood-brain barrier and reverse central antimuscarinic delirium, whereas the quaternary neostigmine stays peripheral. Both carbamoylate the active-site serine; it is edrophonium that binds by ionic and hydrogen bonding alone and therefore lasts only minutes.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-15`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The active enantiomer of adrenaline is designated R and that of propranolol S, yet both are laevorotatory and both present the same three pharmacophoric features. The change of descriptor is caused by:`,options:[`A genuine change in the spatial arrangement of the pharmacophore`,`The difference in the sign of optical rotation`,`The ether oxygen of the aryloxypropanolamine changing the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priorities`,`Racemisation of propranolol in vivo`],answer:2,explanation:`Inserting the OCH2 group places an oxygen next to the carbinol carbon, and oxygen outranks the carbon it displaced, so the priority order is reshuffled and the same three-dimensional arrangement is now labelled S. The spatial positions of the aryl group, the hydroxyl and the amine are unchanged, so a real change in the pharmacophore is not what happens.`},{id:`q-mc-ans-agents-16`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Dobutamine is used as a racemate and its net effect is mainly beta 1 stimulation with little change in systemic vascular resistance because:`,options:[`Both enantiomers are pure beta 1 agonists`,`It is converted to dopamine in vivo`,`It is a catechol and is destroyed before it reaches alpha receptors`,`One enantiomer is an alpha 1 agonist and the other an alpha 1 antagonist, so the alpha effects largely cancel`],answer:3,explanation:`The two enantiomers have opposing actions at alpha 1 receptors, one agonist and one antagonist, so in the racemate those effects offset each other and the shared beta 1 agonism dominates. Dobutamine is not converted to dopamine and has no dopamine receptor action, which is the usual confusion with a dopamine infusion.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Diltiazem belongs to which chemical class of calcium channel blocker?`,options:[`Dihydropyridine`,`Phenylalkylamine`,`Benzothiazepine`,`Benzofuran`],answer:2,explanation:`Diltiazem is a benzothiazepine and its effects sit between the vessel-selective dihydropyridines and the cardio-selective phenylalkylamine verapamil, all three binding different sites on the same L-type channel alpha-1 subunit. Benzofuran is the amiodarone skeleton, not a calcium channel blocker class.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Amiodarone causes hypothyroidism or thyrotoxicosis because the molecule is:`,options:[`A di-iodinated benzofuran, so each dose delivers a large iodine load`,`A steroid that suppresses thyroid stimulating hormone`,`A thiourea derivative like carbimazole`,`A perchlorate salt that blocks iodide uptake`],answer:0,explanation:`Amiodarone is a di-iodinated benzofuran with a diethylaminoethoxy side chain, so a maintenance dose supplies many times the daily iodine requirement and can push the thyroid either way. Its extreme lipophilicity separately explains the volume of distribution of 60 to 70 litres per kilogram and the half-life of 40 to 60 days.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-11`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which pair of statins is given as an inactive lactone that must be hydrolysed to the open dihydroxy acid before it can inhibit HMG-CoA reductase?`,options:[`Atorvastatin and rosuvastatin`,`Lovastatin and simvastatin`,`Pravastatin and fluvastatin`,`Rosuvastatin and pravastatin`],answer:1,explanation:`Lovastatin and simvastatin are fungal-derived lactones and only the ring-opened hydroxy acid mimics the mevaldyl-CoA transition state. Pravastatin, fluvastatin, atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are supplied already in the open acid or salt form, so they need no such activation.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-12`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which angiotensin receptor blocker carries a carboxylic acid instead of the usual tetrazole as its acidic group?`,options:[`Losartan`,`Valsartan`,`Irbesartan`,`Telmisartan`],answer:3,explanation:`Telmisartan is built on a bis-benzimidazole scaffold and uses a carboxylic acid; it also has the longest half-life of the class at about 24 hours. Losartan, valsartan and irbesartan all carry the tetrazole, which is a non-classical bioisostere of a carboxyl with a similar pKa of about 4.9 but greater lipophilicity and metabolic stability.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-13`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`At therapeutic concentrations glyceryl trinitrate is bioactivated to nitric oxide mainly by:`,options:[`Mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase 2, with a thiol taking part`,`Soluble guanylate cyclase`,`Xanthine oxidase`,`Endothelial nitric oxide synthase`],answer:0,explanation:`ALDH2 in mitochondria performs the thiol-dependent bioactivation, and the consumption of tissue thiols together with inactivation of that enzyme is the accepted basis of nitrate tolerance, avoided by an 8 to 12 hour nitrate-free interval. Soluble guanylate cyclase is the target of the nitric oxide once it is formed, not the enzyme that forms it, and nitrates bypass nitric oxide synthase entirely, which is why they still work when the endothelium is damaged.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-14`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Hypokalaemia increases digoxin toxicity because potassium and digoxin:`,options:[`Are both eliminated by renal tubular secretion`,`Form an insoluble complex in plasma`,`Compete for the same site on the extracellular face of Na+/K+-ATPase`,`Compete for the same plasma protein binding site`],answer:2,explanation:`Digoxin binds the extracellular alpha subunit of the sodium pump at a site that potassium also occupies, so when plasma potassium falls the drug binds more readily and toxicity appears at ordinary plasma digoxin concentrations. Displacement from plasma protein is not relevant, since digoxin is only about 25 percent protein bound.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-15`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`For the same plasma concentration, oral verapamil slows atrioventricular conduction less than intravenous verapamil. The reason is that:`,options:[`The oral product is a different salt of the drug`,`The more potent S(-) enantiomer is preferentially removed on first pass, so relatively more of the weaker R isomer survives`,`Oral verapamil is absorbed only from the colon`,`The R isomer is converted to the S isomer in the liver`],answer:1,explanation:`Verapamil is a racemate and the S(-) isomer, which is roughly 10 to 20 times the more potent on the AV node, is also cleared much faster on first pass, so the circulating mixture after an oral dose is enriched in the weak R isomer. There is no metabolic inversion of R to S for verapamil, unlike the unidirectional inversion seen with the profens.`},{id:`q-mc-cvs-agents-16`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`If the sugar residues at C3 of digoxin are removed to leave the aglycone digoxigenin, the consequence is:`,options:[`Complete loss of inotropic activity, because the sugars are part of the pharmacophore`,`Conversion of the molecule into a mineralocorticoid`,`Retention of inotropic activity, with altered potency, onset and duration`,`Loss of the C17 lactone as well`],answer:2,explanation:`The pharmacophore is the steroid carrying the C14-beta hydroxyl, the C3-beta hydroxyl and the C17-beta unsaturated lactone; the sugars are not needed for intrinsic activity and only modify potency, onset and duration. The change that genuinely destroys activity is saturating the C17 lactone, which is why that ring, not the sugar chain, is the part to protect.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-9`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Aztreonam can usually be given to a patient with severe penicillin allergy because it:`,options:[`Contains no beta-lactam ring at all`,`Is a monocyclic beta-lactam with no fused second ring, so it does not share the penicilloyl antigenic determinant`,`Is destroyed before it can reach the immune system`,`Is a glycopeptide rather than a beta-lactam`],answer:1,explanation:`Aztreonam is a monobactam: the beta-lactam ring stands alone, without the thiazolidine of a penicillin or the dihydrothiazine of a cephalosporin, and its degradation products do not cross-react with the penicilloyl determinant responsible for penicillin allergy. It certainly does contain a beta-lactam ring, and its spectrum is confined to aerobic Gram-negative organisms.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-10`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Chloramphenicol has two chiral centres and hence four stereoisomers. The antibacterially active one is the:`,options:[`D-threo (1R,2R) isomer`,`L-threo isomer`,`D-erythro isomer`,`L-erythro isomer`],answer:0,explanation:`Only the D-threo (1R,2R) form fits the peptidyl transferase site of the 50S subunit; the other three isomers are inactive. The nitro group of the benzene ring can be replaced bioisosterically by a methylsulfonyl group to give thiamphenicol, but the stereochemistry cannot be altered.`,tag:`Structure ID`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-11`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Removing the C6 hydroxyl group, as in doxycycline and minocycline, gives a tetracycline that is:`,options:[`Less lipophilic and more dependent on renal excretion`,`More susceptible to C4 epimerisation`,`More lipophilic, better absorbed, longer acting, and unable to undergo C6 dehydration`,`Devoid of metal-chelating ability`],answer:2,explanation:`Without the C6 hydroxyl there is no site for acid-catalysed dehydration, so the nephrotoxic anhydro degradation products cannot form, and the extra lipophilicity gives near-complete oral absorption and a long half-life; doxycycline is also eliminated largely in faeces, making it the safest tetracycline in renal impairment. The keto-enol systems that chelate calcium, magnesium and iron are untouched, so the interaction with milk and antacids remains.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-12`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Erythromycin is destroyed by gastric acid because:`,options:[`The lactone ring is hydrolysed to the open hydroxy acid`,`The desosamine sugar is protonated and lost`,`The 14-membered ring contracts to a 13-membered ring`,`The C6 hydroxyl attacks the C9 ketone, giving an inactive hemiketal and then anhydroerythromycin`],answer:3,explanation:`Acid promotes intramolecular attack of the C6 hydroxyl on the C9 ketone; the resulting anhydroerythromycin has no antibacterial action but is a motilin agonist, which is the source of the cramping and diarrhoea. Clarithromycin blocks the reaction by methylating that C6 hydroxyl and azithromycin by removing the C9 ketone during ring expansion to a 15-membered azalide.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-13`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Amikacin retains activity against many gentamicin-resistant Gram-negative organisms because amikacin:`,options:[`Is not taken up by bacteria at all`,`Binds the 50S rather than the 30S subunit`,`Carries an L-gamma-amino-alpha-hydroxybutyryl (AHBA) side chain on N1 that sterically blocks the bacterial modifying enzymes`,`Is a semisynthetic macrolide rather than an aminoglycoside`],answer:2,explanation:`Amikacin is kanamycin A acylated with an L-hydroxyaminobutyryl group, and that bulky side chain keeps the bacterial acetyl-, phospho- and adenylyltransferases away from the amino and hydroxyl groups they would otherwise modify. It binds the same 16S rRNA A site on the 30S subunit as every other aminoglycoside, so a change of target is not the explanation.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-14`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A bacteriostatic tetracycline is generally not combined with a bactericidal penicillin because:`,options:[`The two chelate one another in the gut lumen`,`Tetracycline halts bacterial growth, and a penicillin can kill only cells that are actively building peptidoglycan`,`Tetracycline induces bacterial beta-lactamase`,`Penicillin blocks the entry of tetracycline into the bacterial cell`],answer:1,explanation:`A penicillin acylates the transpeptidase and kills only while new cell wall is being laid down, so a drug that stops growth removes the very state the beta-lactam needs, and the pair can be antagonistic. Chelation is a real tetracycline problem, but it involves divalent and trivalent metal ions from milk, antacids and iron salts, not beta-lactams.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-15`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Vancomycin resistance in enterococci arises because the peptidoglycan precursor now ends in:`,options:[`D-alanyl-D-lactate instead of D-alanyl-D-alanine, so one of the five hydrogen bonds is lost`,`D-alanyl-D-alanine instead of L-alanyl-L-alanine`,`A modified penicillin-binding protein designated PBP2a`,`An extended pentaglycine bridge`],answer:0,explanation:`Substituting D-lactate for the terminal D-alanine turns an amide NH into an ester oxygen, so one of the five hydrogen bonds to vancomycin cannot form and affinity falls roughly a thousandfold. PBP2a is the MRSA mechanism and concerns beta-lactams only, because vancomycin binds the peptidoglycan substrate rather than the enzyme.`},{id:`q-mc-antibiotics-16`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Adding clavulanic acid will not restore beta-lactam activity against an organism producing NDM-1, because NDM-1:`,options:[`Is an efflux pump rather than an enzyme`,`Is a modified outer membrane porin`,`Is a class B metallo-beta-lactamase that uses zinc rather than an active-site serine`,`Is a class A serine enzyme that is already saturated with substrate`],answer:2,explanation:`Clavulanate, sulbactam and tazobactam are mechanism-based inhibitors that must first be opened by an active-site serine, so they cannot touch the zinc-dependent Ambler class B enzymes NDM-1, IMP and VIM, which also hydrolyse carbapenems. The class A serine enzymes such as TEM, SHV and CTX-M are exactly the ones clavulanate does inhibit.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-9`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Amantadine is active against influenza A but not influenza B because its target, the M2 proton channel:`,options:[`Is present only in influenza A`,`Is present only in influenza B`,`Is a host cell protein`,`Is simply another name for the neuraminidase of influenza A`],answer:0,explanation:`Blocking the M2 channel prevents acidification of the virion interior and therefore uncoating, and influenza B carries no M2 protein at all, so the drug has nothing to block. Neuraminidase is a quite different target and is present in both A and B, which is why oseltamivir and zanamivir cover both.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-10`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antifungal drug target has no structural or functional counterpart at all in human cells?`,options:[`Ergosterol in the plasma membrane`,`Lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase`,`1,3-Beta-D-glucan synthase of the cell wall`,`Squalene epoxidase`],answer:2,explanation:`Human cells have no cell wall and synthesise no 1,3-beta-glucan, so the echinocandins attack a structure the host does not possess, which is why caspofungin and micafungin are the best tolerated systemic antifungals. Humans do possess a 14-alpha-demethylase and a squalene epoxidase for cholesterol synthesis, and cholesterol resembles ergosterol closely enough for amphotericin B to bind it, which is the source of its toxicity.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-11`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ketoconazole causes gynaecomastia and adrenal suppression whereas fluconazole at usual doses does not, because ketoconazole:`,options:[`Is a triazole and binds fungal CYP51 more tightly`,`Is metabolised to an androgen receptor antagonist`,`Displaces testosterone from sex hormone binding globulin`,`Is an imidazole and is far less selective, so it also inhibits human 17,20-lyase and 11-beta-hydroxylase`],answer:3,explanation:`The two-nitrogen imidazole ring is much less discriminating between fungal CYP51 and human steroidogenic P450 enzymes, so ketoconazole blocks 17,20-lyase and 11-beta-hydroxylase as well. Fluconazole is a triazole, and it is exactly that third ring nitrogen that improves selectivity for the fungal enzyme and lengthens the half-life, which is why triazoles replaced imidazoles for systemic use.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-12`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Lamivudine and emtricitabine cause far less mitochondrial toxicity than zidovudine or stavudine because they are:`,options:[`Given at much lower doses`,`Unnatural L-enantiomers and therefore poor substrates for human DNA polymerase gamma`,`Not phosphorylated at all`,`Excreted unchanged by the kidney`],answer:1,explanation:`Both are oxathiolane nucleosides in the unnatural L configuration, so the human mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma accepts them poorly while viral reverse transcriptase still does, and the myopathy and lactic acidosis of the older analogues are largely avoided. They are still phosphorylated by host kinases to the triphosphate, so an answer denying phosphorylation contradicts the mechanism.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-13`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A sub-therapeutic dose of ritonavir is added to another protease inhibitor in order to:`,options:[`Widen the antiviral spectrum to include HIV-2`,`Prevent the partner drug from being phosphorylated`,`Inhibit CYP3A4 and so raise and sustain the concentration of the partner drug`,`Reduce the plasma protein binding of the partner drug`],answer:2,explanation:`Ritonavir is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor, so at booster dose it slows metabolism of the partner protease inhibitor and permits lower and less frequent dosing; cobicistat performs the same task with no antiviral action of its own. Protease inhibitors are peptidomimetics that never require phosphorylation, which is a property of the nucleoside analogues.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-14`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Griseofulvin must be continued until the infected keratin has grown out because the drug:`,options:[`Is deposited in newly formed keratin, where it binds fungal tubulin and wrecks the mitotic spindle`,`Is fungicidal only in the dermis`,`Inhibits ergosterol synthesis only in the nail bed`,`Is inactivated by sweat on the skin surface`],answer:0,explanation:`Griseofulvin, a spiro-benzofuran from Penicillium griseofulvum, is laid down in keratin as it is made and binds fungal tubulin there, so only the new keratin is protected and the old infected keratin must be shed. Its absorption is improved by a fatty meal and by micronisation, and it is a strong enzyme inducer that reduces the effect of warfarin and oral contraceptives.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-15`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Ganciclovir is effective in cytomegalovirus disease although aciclovir is not, because the first phosphorylation of ganciclovir in an infected cell is carried out by:`,options:[`Viral thymidine kinase`,`The cytomegalovirus UL97 phosphotransferase`,`Host adenylate kinase`,`Viral DNA polymerase`],answer:1,explanation:`Cytomegalovirus encodes no thymidine kinase, so aciclovir is barely phosphorylated in CMV-infected cells, whereas the UL97 protein kinase phosphorylates ganciclovir efficiently, and UL97 mutation is a common resistance mechanism. Viral DNA polymerase is the ultimate target of the triphosphate, not the enzyme that activates the drug.`},{id:`q-mc-antivirals-antifungals-16`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient already taking a proton pump inhibitor is started on an oral azole. Which absorption is most likely to be impaired?`,options:[`Fluconazole, because it needs an acidic pH to dissolve`,`Itraconazole capsules, because their dissolution depends on gastric acid`,`Voriconazole, because it is a weak base`,`Terbinafine, because it is an allylamine`],answer:1,explanation:`Ketoconazole and itraconazole are poorly soluble weak bases whose dissolution requires gastric acid, so acid suppression sharply reduces their absorption, and an acidic drink or the oral solution formulation is used to get round it. Fluconazole absorption is essentially pH independent and nearly complete, and terbinafine is not an azole at all.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-9`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a cell cycle NON-specific agent, able to kill resting cells as well as dividing ones?`,options:[`Methotrexate`,`Vincristine`,`Cyclophosphamide`,`Bleomycin`],answer:2,explanation:`Alkylating agents, platinum compounds, anthracyclines and nitrosoureas damage DNA whatever phase the cell is in, so their effect is dose dependent and they can be given as a bolus. Methotrexate acts in S phase, and vincristine and bleomycin in M or G2, so all three are cycle specific and need prolonged exposure by infusion.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-10`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which agent protects against doxorubicin cardiotoxicity, and by what mechanism?`,options:[`Mesna, by supplying a thiol that adds across an unsaturated aldehyde`,`Dexrazoxane, by chelating iron`,`Amifostine, by binding platinum`,`Folinic acid, by bypassing a blocked enzyme`],answer:1,explanation:`Anthracycline cardiotoxicity is attributed to iron-dependent free radical damage in a tissue poor in catalase, and dexrazoxane is an EDTA-like chelator that removes the iron needed for that redox cycling. Mesna is the closest wrong answer because it is also a protective agent, but it binds acrolein and protects the bladder from cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-11`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Only cis-diamminedichloroplatinum(II) is cytotoxic; the trans isomer is not. The reason is that:`,options:[`The trans isomer cannot be aquated inside the cell`,`The trans isomer does not enter the cell`,`The two leaving groups must be cis for the platinum to bridge two adjacent N7 guanines`,`The trans isomer binds only to RNA`],answer:2,explanation:`Inside the cell, where chloride is low, the chlorides are displaced by water and the resulting positively charged complex must reach two neighbouring guanine N7 atoms, which only the cis geometry allows. Carboplatin keeps that cis arrangement but replaces the chlorides with a cyclobutane dicarboxylate that aquates far more slowly, making it less nephrotoxic and emetic but more myelosuppressive.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-12`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Leucovorin (folinic acid) is added to a 5-fluorouracil regimen in order to:`,options:[`Rescue the marrow, as it does after high-dose methotrexate`,`Increase the renal excretion of 5-fluorouracil`,`Prevent the conversion of 5-fluorouracil to FdUMP`,`Stabilise the ternary complex of FdUMP with thymidylate synthase, increasing the cytotoxic effect`],answer:3,explanation:`FdUMP, thymidylate synthase and N5,N10-methylene tetrahydrofolate form a ternary complex, and extra reduced folate makes that complex more stable, so leucovorin deepens the block rather than relieving it. That is the exact opposite of its role after high-dose methotrexate, where it bypasses the inhibited dihydrofolate reductase, and the reversal is the classic trap.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-13`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Methotrexate binds dihydrofolate reductase roughly a thousand times more tightly than the natural substrate because, compared with folic acid, it carries:`,options:[`A 4-amino group in place of the 4-hydroxy of the pteridine ring, together with an N10-methyl group`,`A glutamate tail replaced by aspartate`,`An additional benzene ring`,`A fully reduced pteridine ring`],answer:0,explanation:`The 4-amino substitution and N10-methylation give the huge affinity gain that makes the inhibition effectively irreversible at therapeutic concentrations, so tetrahydrofolate is never regenerated and the cell dies for want of thymidine. A fully reduced pteridine ring describes folinic acid, and that is precisely why folinic acid bypasses the blocked enzyme instead of inhibiting it.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-14`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient with Gilbert syndrome (UGT1A1*28) develops severe diarrhoea and neutropenia on irinotecan because:`,options:[`Irinotecan is not converted to SN-38`,`Irinotecan itself is glucuronidated by UGT1A1`,`SN-38, the active metabolite formed by carboxylesterase, is normally inactivated by glucuronidation through UGT1A1`,`UGT1A1 is the enzyme that activates irinotecan`],answer:2,explanation:`Carboxylesterase converts irinotecan to SN-38, about a thousand times more potent, and UGT1A1 then glucuronidates SN-38 for elimination; low UGT1A1 activity leaves SN-38 to accumulate. Calling UGT1A1 the activating enzyme reverses the pathway, since glucuronidation here is the off switch, not the on switch.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-15`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Podophyllotoxin binds tubulin, yet its semisynthetic glycoside etoposide does not. Etoposide instead:`,options:[`Intercalates between DNA base pairs`,`Stabilises the cleavable complex of topoisomerase II with DNA, so the double-strand break is never resealed`,`Inhibits dihydrofolate reductase`,`Inhibits topoisomerase I`],answer:1,explanation:`Glycosylation abolished the tubulin binding and created an entirely new mechanism, trapping the topoisomerase II-DNA complex so that persistent double-strand breaks accumulate; its characteristic late complication is secondary acute myeloid leukaemia with an 11q23 translocation. Topoisomerase I, which makes single-strand breaks, is the target of the camptothecins topotecan and irinotecan, not of etoposide.`},{id:`q-mc-anticancer-16`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A woman taking tamoxifen for breast cancer is started on paroxetine. The concern is that paroxetine:`,options:[`Induces CYP3A4 and destroys tamoxifen`,`Inhibits CYP2D6 and so reduces formation of endoxifen, the far more potent active metabolite`,`Displaces tamoxifen from plasma albumin`,`Converts the Z isomer of tamoxifen into the E isomer`],answer:1,explanation:`CYP2D6 converts tamoxifen to endoxifen, roughly a hundred times more potent at the oestrogen receptor, so a strong CYP2D6 inhibitor such as paroxetine or fluoxetine can blunt the therapeutic benefit, and genetic poor metabolisers face the same problem. Geometric isomerism does matter for tamoxifen, since the Z isomer is the antioestrogen and the E isomer is oestrogenic, but no drug interconverts them in the body.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-9`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which NSAID class owes its acidity to an enolic hydroxyl and contains no carboxylic acid group at all?`,options:[`Fenamates`,`Oxicams`,`Propionic acids`,`Salicylates`],answer:1,explanation:`Piroxicam, tenoxicam and meloxicam are benzothiazine carboxamides whose enol has a pKa of about 4 to 6, so they behave as acids without any carboxyl, and they are notably long acting. Fenamates are N-aryl anthranilic acids, so they do carry a carboxylic acid group.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-10`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of these analgesics is essentially neutral rather than acidic, and therefore neither irritates the gastric mucosa directly nor affects platelet function?`,options:[`Diclofenac`,`Piroxicam`,`Paracetamol`,`Ketorolac`],answer:2,explanation:`Paracetamol is a para-aminophenol derivative and an essentially neutral phenol, so it neither concentrates in the acidic gastric mucosa nor inhibits platelet thromboxane synthesis. The other three are acids with pKa values of about 3 to 5, and all three carry the usual gastric and antiplatelet risk.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-11`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The two chlorine atoms on the anilino ring of diclofenac contribute mainly by:`,options:[`Increasing water solubility`,`Chelating the haem iron of cyclo-oxygenase`,`Preventing hepatic glucuronidation`,`Forcing the two aromatic rings out of plane by about 70 degrees, which improves the fit in the COX channel`],answer:3,explanation:`The bulky ortho substituents twist the rings out of coplanarity, and that twisted shape is what fits the hydrophobic pocket of the cyclo-oxygenase channel, raising potency. The ionic anchor of the molecule is its carboxylate binding to arginine 120, and the chlorines contribute nothing to that interaction.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-12`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Introducing a 1,2 double bond into hydrocortisone gives prednisolone, which has:`,options:[`About four times the glucocorticoid activity, with salt retention little changed`,`About one quarter of the glucocorticoid activity`,`Greatly increased mineralocorticoid activity`,`No activity until it is reduced in the liver`],answer:0,explanation:`The delta-1 modification raises glucocorticoid potency roughly fourfold while leaving the salt-retaining activity almost unchanged, so the therapeutic ratio improves; the equivalent doses are hydrocortisone 20 mg and prednisolone 5 mg. Needing hepatic reduction before it works describes prednisone, the 11-keto compound, whereas prednisolone already carries the essential 11-beta-hydroxyl.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-13`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Oral oestradiol is largely destroyed by first-pass metabolism, whereas ethinyl oestradiol is orally active because its 17-alpha-ethynyl group:`,options:[`Makes the molecule much more water soluble`,`Sterically hinders oxidation of the neighbouring 17-beta-hydroxyl`,`Aromatises ring A`,`Prevents absorption from the stomach`],answer:1,explanation:`The bulky ethynyl substituent shields the 17-beta-hydroxyl from oxidation to the ketone, so the molecule survives hepatic first pass; the same tactic makes norethisterone an orally active progestogen. Ring A of oestradiol is already aromatic, which is precisely why oestrogens have no C19 methyl group.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-14`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Saturating the 3,4 double bond of a benzothiadiazine, giving a hydrothiazide such as hydrochlorothiazide, mainly:`,options:[`Abolishes the diuretic effect`,`Removes the need for the C7 sulfamoyl group`,`Raises potency about tenfold`,`Converts the drug into a pure carbonic anhydrase inhibitor`],answer:2,explanation:`Reduction of the 3,4 double bond raises potency roughly tenfold, which is why every marketed agent of this class is a hydrothiazide; the C7 sulfamoyl group and the electron-withdrawing group at C6 remain essential. Thiazides do retain weak carbonic anhydrase activity, but their main action is on the sodium-chloride cotransporter of the distal convoluted tubule.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-15`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Eplerenone causes far less gynaecomastia than spironolactone because eplerenone:`,options:[`Is not a steroid at all`,`Is a more potent mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist`,`Blocks the epithelial sodium channel directly instead`,`Carries a 9,11-epoxide and a 7-carbomethoxy group in place of the 7-thioacetyl, giving far greater receptor selectivity`],answer:3,explanation:`Spironolactone also binds androgen and progesterone receptors, which is what produces gynaecomastia and menstrual disturbance, and the epoxide plus carbomethoxy modifications of eplerenone largely remove that off-target binding, at the cost of lower potency. Direct blockade of the epithelial sodium channel is the amiloride and triamterene mechanism, and it is aldosterone independent.`},{id:`q-mc-nsaids-steroids-16`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Aspirin has a pKa of about 3.5. Raising the urinary pH from 5.5 to 7.5 changes the ratio of ionised to unionised drug in the tubular fluid from about 100 to 1 to about:`,options:[`10000 to 1`,`1000 to 1`,`200 to 1`,`10 to 1`],answer:0,explanation:`For a weak acid log ([ionised] / [unionised]) = pH - pKa, so at pH 5.5 the ratio is 10^2, that is 100 to 1, and at pH 7.5 it is 10^4, that is 10000 to 1. That hundredfold rise in the ionised, non-reabsorbable fraction is the basis of urinary alkalinisation in salicylate overdose; the 200 to 1 answer comes from doubling the ratio instead of applying the logarithm.`,tag:`Numerical`}],n=[{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-1`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Partition coefficient equation?`,back:`P = C(octanol) / C(water); log P is its base-10 logarithm. Log D is the same at a stated pH, allowing for ionisation.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-2`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`The four classical QSAR parameters and what each measures?`,back:`pi = lipophilicity (Hansch), sigma = electronic (Hammett), Es = steric (Taft), MR = molar refractivity.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-3`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Optimum log P for oral absorption and for CNS entry?`,back:`About 2 for both; the CNS range is usually quoted as 1.5 to 2.7.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-4`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Lipinski rule of five?`,back:`MW not above 500, log P not above 5, H-bond donors not above 5, acceptors not above 10. Two or more violations predict poor oral absorption.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-5`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Craig plot versus Topliss tree?`,back:`Craig plot is graphical selection of substituents before synthesis (pi against sigma); Topliss is a stepwise operational scheme applied after each analogue is tested.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-6`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Optimum log P from a parabolic Hansch equation?`,back:`log P0 = k2 / (2 k1), where k1 is the coefficient of the squared term and k2 that of the linear term.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-1`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Prodrug versus soft drug?`,back:`Prodrug is inactive and becomes active. Soft drug is active and is designed to become inactive in one predictable step.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-2`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Carrier-linked versus bioprecursor prodrug?`,back:`Carrier-linked has a temporary carrier cleaved off (enalapril). Bioprecursor has no carrier and is transformed by oxidation or reduction (levodopa, cyclophosphamide).`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-3`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`The two ACE inhibitors that are not prodrugs?`,back:`Captopril and lisinopril.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-4`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Chloramphenicol palmitate versus sodium succinate?`,back:`Palmitate masks taste for oral suspension (pancreatic lipase). Succinate gives water solubility for injection (plasma esterase).`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-5`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Which prodrug is activated by colonic bacterial azoreductase?`,back:`Sulfasalazine, giving 5-aminosalicylic acid plus sulfapyridine. It is also a mutual prodrug.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-6`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Toxic promoieties to remember?`,back:`Formaldehyde from fosphenytoin and the double esters of ampicillin; pivalic acid from pivampicillin, which depletes carnitine.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-1`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Phase I versus Phase II in one line?`,back:`Phase I is functionalisation (oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis) by P450. Phase II is conjugation by transferases, giving polar inactive products.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-2`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`P450 mono-oxygenase stoichiometry?`,back:`RH + O2 + NADPH + H+ gives ROH + H2O + NADP+. One oxygen atom enters the drug, the other becomes water.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-3`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Commonest Phase II route and its cofactor?`,back:`Glucuronidation, using UDP-glucuronic acid with microsomal UGT.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-4`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Enzyme inducers mnemonic?`,back:`CRAP GPS: Carbamazepine, Rifampicin, Alcohol (chronic), Phenytoin, Griseofulvin, Phenobarbitone, Smoking.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-5`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Paracetamol toxic metabolite, enzyme and antidote?`,back:`NAPQI, formed by CYP2E1; N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-6`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Benzodiazepines needing no Phase I step?`,back:`LOT: Lorazepam, Oxazepam, Temazepam. Safe in liver disease.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Barbituric acid chemical parent?`,back:`Malonylurea. It is inactive until both C5 hydrogens are replaced by carbon substituents.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Benzodiazepines versus barbiturates at GABA-A?`,back:`Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of chloride channel opening; barbiturates increase the duration and at high dose open it directly.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Essential benzodiazepine substituent at position 7?`,back:`An electron-withdrawing group: NO2 greater than Br greater than CF3 greater than Cl greater than H.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`How many carbons between phenothiazine N10 and the basic nitrogen for antipsychotic activity?`,back:`Three, unbranched. Two carbons gives an antihistamine such as promethazine.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Phenytoin chemical name?`,back:`5,5-Diphenylhydantoin.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Why is thiopental ultra short acting?`,back:`The C2 sulfur makes it very lipophilic, so it redistributes from brain to muscle and fat. Its elimination half-life is still hours.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`What is catechol?`,back:`Benzene bearing hydroxyl groups at positions 3 and 4. It is the COMT substrate feature.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Effect of increasing the N-substituent size on a catecholamine?`,back:`H gives alpha (noradrenaline), methyl gives both (adrenaline), isopropyl gives beta (isoprenaline), tert-butyl gives beta 2 (salbutamol).`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Easson-Stedman hypothesis in one sentence?`,back:`The less active enantiomer is equipotent with the analogue lacking the beta hydroxyl, proving three-point attachment.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Beta blocker general structure?`,back:`Ar-O-CH2-CH(OH)-CH2-NH-R, the aryloxypropanolamine, with R isopropyl or tert-butyl; the S isomer is active.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`What confers beta 1 selectivity?`,back:`A para substituent on the aryl ring: metoprolol, atenolol, betaxolol, bisoprolol.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Which structural changes escape COMT and MAO?`,back:`Resorcinol (terbutaline) or saligenin (salbutamol) ring escapes COMT; an alpha-methyl group (ephedrine) escapes MAO.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-1`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Three zinc-binding groups used by ACE inhibitors?`,back:`Thiol (captopril), carboxylate (most prils) and phosphinate (fosinopril).`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-2`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Why does the C4 aryl ring of a dihydropyridine need ortho or meta substitution?`,back:`It must sit perpendicular to the dihydropyridine ring in the bound conformation; a para substituent or plain phenyl lowers activity.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-3`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Cardiac glycoside SAR essentials?`,back:`C14-beta hydroxyl, C17-beta unsaturated lactone, C3-beta hydroxyl for sugars, A/B cis and C/D cis ring fusion.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-4`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Digoxin versus digitoxin?`,back:`Digoxin has an extra 12-beta hydroxyl, so it is more polar, renally excreted, half-life 36 to 40 hours. Digitoxin is hepatic, 5 to 7 days.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-5`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Vaughan Williams class III drugs?`,back:`Potassium channel blockers: amiodarone, sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide. They prolong action potential duration and QT.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-6`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Statin pharmacophore?`,back:`3,5-Dihydroxyheptanoic acid chain (3R,5S), a transition-state mimic of mevaldyl-CoA.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-1`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Penicillin and cephalosporin nuclei?`,back:`6-Aminopenicillanic acid (beta-lactam plus thiazolidine) and 7-aminocephalosporanic acid (beta-lactam plus dihydrothiazine).`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-2`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Why is the beta-lactam carbonyl so reactive?`,back:`The strained four-membered ring prevents amide resonance, leaving a highly electrophilic carbonyl that acylates the active-site serine of the transpeptidase.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-3`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Spectrum trend across cephalosporin generations 1 to 4?`,back:`Gram-positive cover falls then returns at generation 4; Gram-negative cover, beta-lactamase stability and CSF entry rise. MRSA cover only at generation 5.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-4`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`The three beta-lactamase inhibitors and their partners?`,back:`Clavulanic acid with amoxicillin or ticarcillin, sulbactam with ampicillin, tazobactam with piperacillin.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-5`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Quinolone: what do C3, C4, C6 and C7 contribute?`,back:`C3 carboxyl and C4 ketone chelate Mg2+ and are essential; C6 fluorine gives up to 100-fold potency; C7 piperazine gives Gram-negative and antipseudomonal cover.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-6`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Why are sulfonamides selectively toxic?`,back:`Bacteria must synthesise folate from PABA using dihydropteroate synthase; humans have no such enzyme and absorb preformed folate.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-7`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Why is co-trimoxazole bactericidal?`,back:`Sequential blockade of two consecutive steps: sulfamethoxazole blocks dihydropteroate synthase, trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase. Each alone is bacteriostatic.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-1`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Why is acyclovir selective?`,back:`The first phosphorylation needs viral thymidine kinase, present only in infected cells; host kinases add the other two phosphates.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-2`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`What replaces the 3 prime hydroxyl in zidovudine?`,back:`An azido group, so the chain cannot be extended. Lamivudine instead uses an oxathiolane ring in the unnatural L configuration.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-3`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Azole mechanism?`,back:`The free azole nitrogen binds the haem iron of lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), so ergosterol is not made.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-4`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Imidazole versus triazole?`,back:`Imidazole has two ring nitrogens and is mostly topical; triazole has three, is more selective for fungal CYP51 and is used systemically.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-5`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Amphotericin B mechanism and chemistry?`,back:`A polyene macrolide that binds membrane ergosterol and forms pores. Amphoteric and amphipathic; reconstitute in dextrose, never saline.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-6`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Targets of terbinafine, echinocandins and flucytosine?`,back:`Squalene epoxidase; 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase; conversion to 5-fluorouracil by fungal cytosine deaminase.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-1`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Reactive intermediate of a nitrogen mustard?`,back:`The aziridinium (ethyleneimonium) ion, which alkylates N7 of guanine and can cross-link the two DNA strands.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-2`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Cyclophosphamide activation and its two products?`,back:`CYP2B6 hydroxylation gives 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which fragments to phosphoramide mustard (active) plus acrolein (bladder toxin, blocked by mesna).`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-3`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Enzyme targets of methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil and hydroxyurea?`,back:`Dihydrofolate reductase; thymidylate synthase (as FdUMP); ribonucleotide reductase.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-4`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Vinca alkaloids versus taxanes?`,back:`Vincas prevent tubulin polymerisation; taxanes stabilise the polymer and prevent disassembly. Both arrest mitosis.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-5`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Topoisomerase I versus II inhibitors?`,back:`Topo I (single-strand break): camptothecin, topotecan, irinotecan. Topo II (double-strand break): etoposide, anthracyclines, mitoxantrone.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-6`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Imatinib target and its origin?`,back:`Bcr-Abl tyrosine kinase from the t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome; also c-Kit and PDGFR. T315I is the resistant gatekeeper mutation.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-1`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Six NSAID chemical classes with an example each?`,back:`Salicylate (aspirin), propionic acid (ibuprofen), acetic acid (diclofenac), fenamate (mefenamic acid), oxicam (piroxicam), coxib (celecoxib).`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-2`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Structural basis of COX-2 selectivity?`,back:`COX-2 has valine at position 523 where COX-1 has isoleucine; the smaller residue opens a side pocket that fits the bulky sulfonamide or sulfone of a coxib.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-3`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Why does aspirin act as an antiplatelet drug at low dose?`,back:`It irreversibly acetylates serine 530 of platelet COX-1, and the anucleate platelet cannot make new enzyme for its 7 to 10 day life.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-4`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`What do the 1,2 double bond, 9-alpha-fluoro and 16-methyl do to a corticosteroid?`,back:`Delta-1 raises glucocorticoid activity about 4-fold; 9-alpha-fluoro raises both activities; 16-methyl abolishes the mineralocorticoid activity.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-5`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Steroid parent hydrocarbons?`,back:`Oestrane 18 carbons, androstane 19, pregnane 21, cholestane 27. Ring system is cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-6`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Diuretic chemistry: which class does what?`,back:`Acetazolamide is a thiadiazole sulfonamide (proximal); furosemide a sulfamoyl anthranilic acid (loop); hydrochlorothiazide a benzothiadiazine (distal); amiloride a pyrazinoylguanidine (collecting duct).`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-7`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Why are Hammett sigma values not tabulated for ortho substituents?`,back:`An ortho group adds a steric effect on top of the electronic one. Taft separated them using ester hydrolysis: acidic conditions give Es (steric only), basic conditions give sigma-star.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-8`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Log D of a weak acid from its log P?`,back:`log D = log P - log (1 + 10^(pH - pKa)). For a weak base the exponent becomes (pKa - pH).`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-9`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`Grimm hydride displacement law?`,back:`Adding a hydrogen atom plus an electron to an atom gives a pseudoatom that behaves like the element to its right. It generates the classical isostere sets.`},{id:`f-mc-physchem-sar-10`,topicId:`mc-physchem-sar`,front:`What thermodynamic activity indicates a structurally non-specific drug?`,back:`About 0.01 to 1 at the effective dose means a physical, non-specific action (general anaesthetics, preservatives). Below about 0.001 means a specific receptor action.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-7`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Why is aspirin NOT a prodrug of salicylic acid?`,back:`Aspirin is active in its own right: its acetyl group irreversibly acetylates cyclo-oxygenase, which salicylic acid cannot do. A prodrug must be essentially inactive as given.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-8`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`The three enzymes of the capecitabine cascade, in order?`,back:`Hepatic carboxylesterase, then cytidine deaminase, then thymidine phosphorylase, which is enriched in tumour tissue and gives the targeting. The product is 5-fluorouracil.`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-9`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`The two approaches to soft drug design?`,back:`Inactive metabolite approach (build activity back onto a known inactive metabolite: loteprednol etabonate). Soft analogue approach (insert a labile ester into an active drug: esmolol, remifentanil).`},{id:`f-mc-prodrug-design-10`,topicId:`mc-prodrug-design`,front:`Which transporter carries valaciclovir across the gut wall?`,back:`The peptide transporter PEPT1, because the L-valyl ester makes the molecule resemble a small peptide. Valaciclovir hydrolase then releases aciclovir.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-7`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Which Phase II routes make a metabolite LESS water soluble?`,back:`Acetylation and methylation. Acetylated sulfonamides are the classic cause of crystalluria.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-8`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Hepatic clearance and extraction ratio equations?`,back:`E = CL(int) / (Q + CL(int)); CL(hepatic) = Q x E; with complete absorption and no gut loss, F = 1 - E. Q is about 1.5 litres per minute.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-9`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Where do the non-microsomal enzymes sit?`,back:`MAO on the outer mitochondrial membrane; alcohol dehydrogenase, aldehyde oxidase and xanthine oxidase in the cytosol; cholinesterase in plasma. Only glucuronidation among Phase II routes is microsomal.`},{id:`f-mc-metabolism-10`,topicId:`mc-metabolism`,front:`Toxic metabolites of halothane and isoniazid?`,back:`Halothane gives trifluoroacetyl chloride by CYP2E1, which haptenises liver proteins and causes immune hepatitis. Isoniazid gives acetylhydrazine and hydrazine.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Antiepileptic drugs by chemical class?`,back:`Phenytoin hydantoin; ethosuximide succinimide; trimethadione oxazolidinedione; carbamazepine iminostilbene carboxamide; valproate branched carboxylic acid; lamotrigine phenyltriazine.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Why can midazolam be given as a plain aqueous injection?`,back:`Its fused imidazole ring system opens at the acidic pH of the ampoule to a water-soluble form, and closes again at pH 7.4 to the lipophilic species that enters the brain.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Atypical antipsychotic scaffolds?`,back:`Clozapine dibenzodiazepine; olanzapine thienobenzodiazepine; risperidone benzisoxazole (metabolite paliperidone); aripiprazole quinolinone and a D2 partial agonist; haloperidol butyrophenone.`},{id:`f-mc-cns-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-cns-agents`,front:`Phenothiazine side-chain amine type versus clinical profile?`,back:`Piperazine: most potent, most extrapyramidal. Aliphatic dimethylamino: sedation and hypotension. Piperidine (thioridazine): most anticholinergic, least extrapyramidal.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Hyoscine versus atropine?`,back:`Hyoscine carries a 6,7-epoxide bridge, so it is more lipophilic, enters the brain and is sedative and antiemetic. Atropine is a central stimulant at high dose.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Why does salmeterol last about 12 hours?`,back:`Its long lipophilic N-substituent anchors it at an exosite beside the beta 2 receptor, so the active head keeps re-engaging. It also escapes both COMT and MAO.`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Neuromuscular blocker chemical families?`,back:`Suxamethonium is two acetylcholines joined tail to tail. Non-depolarisers are aminosteroids (pancuronium, vecuronium, rocuronium) or benzylisoquinolines (tubocurarine, atracurium, mivacurium).`},{id:`f-mc-ans-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-ans-agents`,front:`Neostigmine versus physostigmine?`,back:`Neostigmine is a quaternary carbamate and stays peripheral. Physostigmine is a tertiary carbamate, enters the brain, and is the antidote in central antimuscarinic poisoning.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-7`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Type 1 versus type 2 statins?`,back:`Type 1 are fungal-derived decalin compounds (lovastatin, simvastatin, pravastatin). Type 2 are fully synthetic, with a fluorophenyl group and a larger heterocycle (atorvastatin, fluvastatin, rosuvastatin).`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-8`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Which statins are given as inactive lactones?`,back:`Lovastatin and simvastatin. They must be hydrolysed to the open 3,5-dihydroxy acid; the others are supplied as the open acid or its salt.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-9`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Nitrate bioactivation and tolerance?`,back:`Mitochondrial ALDH2 with a thiol releases nitric oxide, which raises cyclic GMP through soluble guanylate cyclase. Tolerance follows thiol depletion and ALDH2 inactivation; prevent it with an 8 to 12 hour nitrate-free interval.`},{id:`f-mc-cvs-agents-10`,topicId:`mc-cvs-agents`,front:`Amiodarone chemistry and its consequences?`,back:`A di-iodinated benzofuran with a diethylaminoethoxy chain. Iodine gives the thyroid effects; extreme lipophilicity gives a Vd of 60 to 70 litres per kilogram and a half-life of 40 to 60 days.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-8`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Why is penicillin V acid stable while penicillin G is not?`,back:`The electron-withdrawing phenoxy oxygen alpha to the side-chain carbonyl stops the side-chain amide oxygen attacking the beta-lactam ring in acid, so penicillin V can be given orally.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-9`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`What does losing the C6 hydroxyl do to a tetracycline?`,back:`Doxycycline and minocycline become more lipophilic, better absorbed and longer acting, and cannot undergo C6 dehydration. Doxycycline is largely faecally eliminated, so it is safest in renal impairment.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-10`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`Why is erythromycin acid labile, and how was it fixed?`,back:`The C6 hydroxyl attacks the C9 ketone, giving an inactive hemiketal and then anhydroerythromycin (a motilin agonist). Clarithromycin methylates the C6 OH; azithromycin removes the C9 ketone as a 15-membered azalide.`},{id:`f-mc-antibiotics-11`,topicId:`mc-antibiotics`,front:`How do enterococci become vancomycin resistant?`,back:`The precursor terminus D-alanyl-D-alanine is replaced by D-alanyl-D-lactate. One of the five hydrogen bonds is lost and affinity falls about a thousandfold.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-7`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Ganciclovir versus aciclovir: who does the first phosphorylation?`,back:`Aciclovir is phosphorylated by herpes simplex or varicella zoster thymidine kinase. CMV has no thymidine kinase, so ganciclovir is phosphorylated by the CMV UL97 phosphotransferase.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-8`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Why do lamivudine and emtricitabine cause little mitochondrial toxicity?`,back:`They are unnatural L-enantiomers, so human DNA polymerase gamma accepts them poorly while viral reverse transcriptase still does.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-9`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Which azoles need gastric acid for absorption?`,back:`Ketoconazole and itraconazole capsules; antacids and proton pump inhibitors reduce their absorption. Fluconazole absorption is pH independent and nearly complete.`},{id:`f-mc-antivirals-antifungals-10`,topicId:`mc-antivirals-antifungals`,front:`Griseofulvin: source, target and absorption?`,back:`A spiro-benzofuran from Penicillium griseofulvum. It binds fungal tubulin and is deposited in new keratin. Absorption improves with a fatty meal and micronisation; it is a strong enzyme inducer.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-7`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Cell cycle specific versus non-specific agents?`,back:`Specific: antimetabolites (S phase), vinca alkaloids, taxanes and bleomycin (M or G2). Non-specific: alkylating agents, platinums, anthracyclines, nitrosoureas.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-8`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Why is only the cis isomer of platinum active?`,back:`The two leaving groups must be cis so the aquated platinum can bridge two adjacent N7 guanines. Carboplatin keeps the cis geometry but uses a cyclobutane dicarboxylate that aquates slowly.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-9`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Why is leucovorin added to 5-fluorouracil?`,back:`It stabilises the FdUMP-thymidylate synthase ternary complex and so increases cytotoxicity. After high-dose methotrexate the same drug does the opposite job and rescues the host.`},{id:`f-mc-anticancer-10`,topicId:`mc-anticancer`,front:`Irinotecan activation and the UGT1A1 problem?`,back:`Carboxylesterase gives SN-38, about a thousand times more potent. UGT1A1 glucuronidates SN-38 for elimination, so UGT1A1*28 (Gilbert) causes severe diarrhoea and neutropenia.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-7`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`What do ortho substituents do in an NSAID?`,back:`They force the two aryl rings out of plane, improving the fit in the COX channel. Diclofenac is the model, its 2,6-dichloro anilino ring twisting the system by about 70 degrees.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-8`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Corticosteroid equivalent doses?`,back:`Hydrocortisone 20 mg, prednisolone 5 mg, methylprednisolone and triamcinolone 4 mg, dexamethasone and betamethasone 0.75 mg.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-9`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`What makes ethinyl oestradiol orally active?`,back:`The 17-alpha-ethynyl group sterically blocks oxidation of the 17-beta-hydroxyl, so the molecule survives hepatic first pass. The same trick gives norethisterone.`},{id:`f-mc-nsaids-steroids-10`,topicId:`mc-nsaids-steroids`,front:`Spironolactone versus eplerenone?`,back:`Eplerenone has a 9,11-epoxide and a 7-carbomethoxy group instead of the 7-thioacetyl: far more selective for the mineralocorticoid receptor, so no gynaecomastia, but less potent.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};